window.CSA_DATA = {"domains":[{"id":"case-management","title":"Case Management","weight":25,"intro":"The largest exam domain: how a case type is modeled as a lifecycle and then made to move by itself through routing, SLAs, approvals, conditions, child cases, waits and automations. Expect scenario questions that ask which App Studio option (or which Dev Studio rule) satisfies a business requirement.","objectives":[{"objective":"Case lifecycle: stages, statuses, and instructions","modules":["Defining a Case Lifecycle","User guidance"],"summary":["A case type is the reusable model of a repeatable business transaction, and a case is one running instance of it with its own ID. The lifecycle is organized as **Stages > Processes > Steps**. A stage is a business milestone named with a noun or gerund of no more than two words (it should read naturally in the sentence 'This case is in <Stage>'). Processes and steps are named verb + noun. Every step is either an **assignment** (a person does the work) or an **automation** (the system does the work). Stages can be primary (the happy path) or alternate (exceptions such as cancellation or investigation).","Stage behavior is set in the stage properties: whether the case advances automatically when the stage finishes or waits, and whether finishing the stage resolves the case. You cannot configure transitions on the Create stage. To jump out of sequence (for example into or out of an alternate stage) use a Change Stage automation. The default Create process is a multi-step form: one assignment, one user, several views, with Next/Previous navigation and Submit on the last view. The process option 'Allow users to go back to previous step' adds a Previous button. New processes start in **draft mode**, which lets them run while incomplete; draft mode must be off before production. App Studio clears it automatically when the case type saves without errors, and turning it off by hand is a Dev Studio task.","The case status tells users where a case stands. Standard values are New, Open, Pending-Approval, Pending-Fulfillment, Resolved-Completed, Resolved-Cancelled and Resolved-Rejected. Set a status on a stage (applied on entry; the final stage holds the resolution status) or on an individual step when you need more detail. Reuse existing statuses and keep to about ten or fewer per case type. Instructions belong on steps that need user input. The form shows the step's instructions by default, and you can replace them with custom text or hide them."],"keyFacts":["Hierarchy: Case Type > Stages > Processes > Steps; a step is an Assignment (human) or an Automation (system).","Stage names: noun or gerund, at most 2 words. Process and step names: verb + noun.","Transitions cannot be configured on the **Create** stage.","The default Create process is a multi-step form: single assignment, single user, multiple views; navigation styles are horizontal, vertical or standard.","Processes are created in draft mode by default. Leaving draft mode on in production is a guardrail violation. App Studio clears it automatically on an error-free save; turning it off manually happens only in Dev Studio.","Standard statuses: New, Open, Pending-Approval, Pending-Fulfillment, Resolved-Completed, Resolved-Cancelled, Resolved-Rejected.","Best practice: no more than 10 statuses per case type, and reuse existing ones.","Status can be set on a Stage (on entry) or on a Step (more specific).","Form views show step instructions by default; override them with custom text or hide them.","In Dev Studio a new case type maps to a Work class and a pyDefault Case Type rule, and each process maps to a Flow rule."],"table":{"headers":["Element","Naming","Purpose"],"rows":[["Stage","Noun/gerund, at most 2 words","Business milestone; primary or alternate"],["Process","Verb + noun","Group of steps; Flow rule in Dev Studio"],["Step","Verb + noun","Assignment (user) or Automation (system)"],["Status","Standard values such as Pending-Approval","Where the case stands; set on stage or step"]]},"examTraps":["Stage names that are actions ('Review application') are wrong; actions belong to processes and steps.","You cannot set transitions, SLAs or escalations on the Create stage.","Draft mode is ON by default, not off. App Studio turns it off automatically; only Dev Studio turns it off manually.","Creating a new custom status when a standard one fits goes against best practice."]},{"objective":"Service level agreements: urgency, goal, deadline, and passed deadline","modules":["Completing work on time","Escalating late work"],"summary":["An SLA (called 'Goal & deadline' in App Studio) sets time milestones and raises urgency as each one passes. The **goal** is when the work should ideally be done, the **deadline** is when it becomes late, and the **passed deadline** repeats after the deadline, either a fixed number of times or until the work completes. Goal and deadline are measured from the start of the work and fire once each. Passed deadline can only be configured in Dev Studio on the SLA rule. Urgency ranges from 0 to 100. Each milestone can add 1 to 100. Once urgency reaches 100, further increments are ignored but escalation actions still run.","SLAs can sit at the case type level (from creation or reopening until resolution), the stage level (entry to exit), the process level (start until the last step finishes), and on assignment (Collect Information) and approval steps. You cannot put an SLA or escalation on the Create stage. In the case type Settings tab > Goal & deadline you choose Use SLA: Custom, Existing or Never, and Calculate time from start of: This Case, Parent Case or Top-level Case, with durations in days plus HH:MM:SS. App Studio creates a Dev Studio SLA rule behind the scenes. An existing SLA rule shows in the App Studio list only if it is marked as a relevant record.","Case urgency and assignment urgency are different values. A new assignment starts at the case urgency, and the initial case urgency is set on the case type Settings tab (default 10, set by the Work- pyDefault data transform). Stage and process SLAs change case urgency. Changing case urgency affects only assignments created afterward, not existing ones. Worklists and work queues are ordered by urgency, and Get Next Work picks the most urgent assignment. App Studio escalations can notify the case creator or participants. Dev Studio adds advanced options: initial urgency, delay timers, variable interval length, a business-day calendar, and escalation actions beyond notification such as reassigning the work or resolving the case."],"keyFacts":["Urgency range is 0 to 100. Default initial case urgency is 10. Each milestone adds 1 to 100.","At urgency 100, further increments are ignored but escalation actions still run.","Goal and deadline fire once each. Passed deadline repeats a fixed number of times or until the work completes.","Passed deadline is configured **only in Dev Studio** on the SLA rule.","Example: start 10, goal +10 gives 20, deadline +10 gives 30.","SLA levels: case type, stage, process, assignment step, approval step. Not allowed on the Create stage.","Use SLA: Custom / Existing / Never. Calculate from: This Case / Parent Case / Top-level Case.","Changing case urgency affects only new assignments, not existing ones.","Get Next Work returns the highest-urgency assignment first.","Reassigning or resolving the case as an escalation action is a Dev Studio SLA option; App Studio escalations notify (creator or participants)."],"table":{"headers":["Milestone","Meaning","Fires","Where configured"],"rows":[["Goal","Desired completion time","Once, measured from start","App Studio or Dev Studio"],["Deadline","Work is late after this","Once, measured from start","App Studio or Dev Studio"],["Passed deadline","Continued lateness after the deadline","Repeats N times or until done","Dev Studio SLA rule only"]]},"examTraps":["Urgency cannot go above 100, but escalations keep running after it gets there.","Raising case urgency does not re-rank assignments that already exist.","Passed deadline is not in the App Studio Goal & deadline panel.","'Calculate time from Parent/Top-level case' is how a child case SLA can be measured from an ancestor's start."]},{"objective":"Routing assignments to users, work groups, work queues, and by business logic","modules":["Routing Assignments to users","Creating and managing teams of users"],"summary":["Each assignment must be routed to someone. The options are the **current user**, a **specific user** (the assignment goes to that person's worklist), a **work queue** (a shared list for a team), or **business logic** (conditions pick the destination, for example sending recreational-vehicle loans to an RV underwriters queue). Worklists and work queues both list open assignments in urgency order. An assignment in a work queue stays there until a team member pulls it or a manager sends it to someone.","In App Studio a team is a work group, and in Dev Studio a user is an operator. A work group is a cross-functional team with a manager and exactly one work queue. A work queue needs a work group and a work group needs a work queue. The default queue is default@<org>. Every operator must belong to at least one work group and can belong to several. The work group manager is used for notifications, approvals, routing and escalations, and works from the Web Portal. **Authorized managers** can help transfer assignments and do not have to be work group members. They cannot perform approvals, and if they are outside the group they cannot complete its work-queue assignments. In Dev Studio a decision table can act as a router, and routing to a queue updates the Assign-Workbasket assignment."],"keyFacts":["Four routing options: current user, specific user (worklist), work queue, business logic (conditions).","Worklist = an operator's own assignments. Work queue = shared assignments for a team.","Each work group has a manager and **exactly one** work queue; neither can exist without the other.","Default work queue: default@<org>.","An operator must belong to at least one work group and can belong to many.","Work-queue items stay until a member pulls them or a manager sends them.","Authorized managers can transfer assignments, need not be members, cannot approve, and cannot complete work-queue assignments if outside the group.","Terminology: Team = Work Group, User = Operator.","Best practice is to configure people in App Studio; use Dev Studio for advanced items such as permissions."],"table":{"headers":["Routing option","Use when"],"rows":[["Current user","The person doing the work continues"],["Specific user (worklist)","One named person must do it"],["Work queue","Any qualified team member can pick it up"],["Business logic","The destination depends on case data"]]},"examTraps":["Authorized managers are not approvers.","A work group has one work queue, not several.","Routing to a work queue does not put the work on anyone's worklist until it is pulled or sent."]},{"objective":"Approvals, including cascading approvals with a reporting structure or an authority matrix","modules":["Designing an approval Process","Cascading approvals","Decision tables and trees"],"summary":["The Approve/Reject step routes like any assignment (user, work queue or business logic) and supports email approval and mobile approval, so approvers can respond without opening the application. On **approve**, the case continues or changes to a specific stage, and you can optionally set a status. On **reject**, the case resolves (default status Resolved-Rejected), continues, or changes stage.","Choosing the Cascading option creates a chain of approvals. A **reporting structure** cascade reads the operator ID record and uses either the Reporting Manager or the Workgroup Manager. The number of approvers can be One, All (the whole hierarchy), or Custom (levels decided by When rules, for example amount thresholds). An **authority matrix** is more flexible and is the right choice when at least one approver is outside the reporting structure. Its approver list is filled by a decision table, data page, activity or data transform, and approvals run in list order. If you use a decision table it must be set to **Evaluate all rows**, otherwise it returns only one result. Among decision rules, only decision tables (not decision trees) can drive an authority matrix."],"keyFacts":["Approve: continue, or change to a specific stage; optionally set a status.","Reject: resolve (default Resolved-Rejected), continue, or change stage.","Email approval and mobile approval let approvers act outside the application.","Reporting structure uses the operator record: Reporting Manager or Workgroup Manager.","Reporting structure approver count: One, All, or Custom (When rules and thresholds).","Authority matrix: use when at least one approver is outside the reporting chain.","Authority matrix sources: Decision table, Data Page, Activity, or Data Transform.","A decision table feeding an authority matrix must use Evaluate all rows; approvals happen in list order.","Decision trees cannot drive an authority matrix."],"table":{"headers":["","Reporting structure","Authority matrix"],"rows":[["Approvers come from","Operator record (Reporting Manager or Workgroup Manager)","A list built by a DT, data page, activity or data transform"],["Best for","Approvals that go up the management chain","Approvers outside the chain (e.g. Legal, Finance)"],["How many","One / All / Custom (When rules)","Every entry in the list, in order"],["Key setting","Number of approvers","DT must Evaluate all rows"]]},"examTraps":["Choosing authority matrix when every approver is in the manager chain; reporting structure is simpler.","Forgetting Evaluate all rows, so only one approver is returned.","Picking a decision tree for an authority matrix.","Assuming rejection always resolves the case; it can also continue or change stage."]},{"objective":"Identifying and handling duplicate cases","modules":["Identifying duplicate Cases"],"summary":["The **Search duplicate Cases** automation step compares the current case with existing cases in two passes. First, **basic conditions** filter candidates: a case must match ALL of them to be considered, and you can cap how many candidates are returned. Next, each candidate is scored with **weighted conditions**, each worth 1 to 100. If the total meets or exceeds the threshold, the case is a potential duplicate.","If potential duplicates are found, a user reviews them and either resolves the current case as a duplicate (status Resolved-Duplicate) or ignores the matches and continues. If nothing matches, the case continues automatically. For example: date of birth as a basic condition, four weighted conditions of 25 each, and a threshold of 50, so any two weighted matches on a same-birthdate case flag it."],"keyFacts":["Implemented with the Search duplicate Cases automation step.","Basic conditions: ALL must match for a case to be a candidate; you can limit the number of candidates.","Weighted conditions: each weight is 1 to 100; candidate is a potential duplicate when the sum is at least the threshold.","Resolving as a duplicate sets the status to Resolved-Duplicate.","The user can also ignore the matches and continue.","No matches: the case simply continues."],"table":{"headers":["Condition type","Role","Rule"],"rows":[["Basic","Filter candidates","All must be true"],["Weighted","Score candidates","Weights 1 to 100; sum compared with threshold"]]},"examTraps":["A case that matches every weighted condition but fails a basic condition is never a candidate.","The threshold check is 'at least' the threshold, so equal counts as a match.","Duplicates are not resolved automatically; a user decides."]},{"objective":"Optional actions in the case lifecycle and for data objects","modules":["Adding optional Actions to a workflow"],"summary":["Optional actions are work users can start whenever they need to, outside the planned steps. A **user action** is a single step or screen, and an **optional process** is several steps. Either can be **case-wide** (available in every stage) or limited to one **stage**. Edit details and Change Stage are built-in case-wide actions. Configure them with the Optional Actions button in the upper-right of the Workflow tab; at runtime users open them from the Actions menu. Data objects also support an Update Details action so users can change only the information they need, with validation and pre/post-processing.","The **promoted action** appears as a button (on the case header, in the summary panel) so users do not have to open the Actions menu. It is the first stage-wide action other than Edit details, or if there is none, the first case-wide action. Only user actions can be promoted, not optional processes."],"keyFacts":["User action = single step/screen. Optional process = multi-step.","Scope: case-wide or stage-only.","Built-in case-wide actions: Edit details and Change Stage.","Configured with the Optional Actions button (upper-right of the Workflow tab); run from the Actions menu.","Promoted action = first stage-wide action other than Edit details; otherwise the first case-wide action.","Only user actions can be promoted; optional processes cannot.","Data objects offer an Update Details action for targeted updates.","Change Stage is useful for moving into or out of alternate stages."],"table":{"headers":["","User action","Optional process"],"rows":[["Size","One screen","Multiple steps"],["Can be promoted","Yes","No"],["Scope","Case-wide or stage","Case-wide or stage"]]},"examTraps":["Adding the same step to every stage when a case-wide optional action does the job.","Expecting an optional process to show as the promoted button.","Edit details is skipped when the promoted action is chosen from the stage actions."]},{"objective":"Using conditions to automate workflow decisions","modules":["Automating workflow decisions","Skipping a Process or a Stage"],"summary":["Conditions let the case decide its own path from data. A condition is a field, a comparator and a value, and several conditions combine with AND. They are used in decision shapes (the orange diamond in the Process Modeler) to choose a branch, which can also leave the stage through Change Stage. They are also used in business-logic routing, custom approval levels, and stage and process skip rules. A When rule answers a single yes/no question. When several properties together determine an outcome, use a decision table. A decision shape that references a decision table automatically gets one connector per possible result.","Values that change often should not be hard-coded. Configuration settings (which replace Dynamic System Settings) can hold parameters such as an approval monetary limit and are referenced through the Condition Builder. Delegated decision tables let process owners make small production changes, such as lowering a threshold, without a developer."],"keyFacts":["Condition = field + comparator + value; multiple conditions are ANDed.","Decision shape (orange diamond) creates conditional paths and can go to another stage via Change Stage.","When rule = yes/no; decision table = outcome depends on several properties.","A decision shape referencing a DT auto-adds one connector per result.","Configuration settings replace DSS and are referenced through the Condition Builder (for example an approval monetary limit).","Delegated decision tables let business owners change values in production (e.g. a threshold from $500 to $400).","Custom levels in reporting-structure approvals are decided with When rules."],"examTraps":["Hard-coding a business threshold that the business wants to change itself.","Using a When rule where the result depends on many inputs and needs more than yes/no.","Choosing Dynamic System Settings; configuration settings are their replacement."]},{"objective":"Purpose of Flow Actions","modules":["Automating workflow decisions"],"summary":["A flow action defines what a user can do at a step. In App Studio flow actions are called user actions. A flow action is attached either to a connector or to an assignment shape. A **connector action** completes the assignment and moves the flow forward. A **local action** updates the assignment without advancing it, and is configured in the assignment properties.","Flow actions are also where step-level logic hooks in. Validate rules are assigned to flow actions. Pre- and post-processing can run data transforms (for example copying a shipping address to billing) or save a savable data page. A Validate rule can be qualified by flow action, so the same field can have different validation on different forms. Flow actions can be marked as relevant records so App Studio can reuse them."],"keyFacts":["Flow action = what a user can do at an assignment; called a User action in App Studio.","Connector action: completes the assignment and advances the flow.","Local action: updates the assignment without advancing; configured in assignment properties.","Validate rules are assigned to flow actions.","Pre/post-processing on a flow action can run a data transform or trigger a savable data page.","Flow actions (user actions) are a relevant-record rule type."],"table":{"headers":["","Connector action","Local action"],"rows":[["Completes assignment","Yes","No"],["Advances flow","Yes","No"],["Configured on","Connector leaving the assignment","Assignment properties"]]},"examTraps":["A local action never moves the case forward.","Validation for a single screen belongs to the flow action, not the property definition."]},{"objective":"Pausing and resuming processing with wait steps","modules":["Pausing and resuming Case processing"],"summary":["A Wait automation step pauses a process until something happens. A **Timer** wait resumes after a set date/time interval or at a date/time stored in a property. A **Case Dependency** wait makes a parent case wait until all or any child cases of a chosen type reach a status, or reach 'To be resolved' (any status starting with Resolved).","Case dependency options include whether to consider the child's current status or only changes made after the parent reaches the wait, and whether a user may cancel the wait. The child cases must already exist when the parent reaches the wait step; otherwise the wait is skipped. A wait step is different from a case simply sitting at an assignment until a user acts."],"keyFacts":["Two wait types: Timer and Case Dependency (case status).","Timer: set a date/time interval, or reference a date/time property.","Case Dependency: all or any child cases of a type reach a status, or 'To be resolved'.","'To be resolved' = any status beginning with Resolved.","Options: consider current status vs only changes after reaching the wait; allow user to cancel the wait.","Children must be created before the wait step, otherwise the wait is skipped.","A wait step is not the same as waiting on a user assignment."],"table":{"headers":["Wait type","Resumes when"],"rows":[["Timer (interval)","A fixed time has passed"],["Timer (reference property)","The date/time in a field is reached"],["Case dependency","All/any child cases of a type reach a status or are resolved"]]},"examTraps":["If the children already resolved before the parent arrives and you chose only changes after reaching the wait, the parent may never resume.","Creating child cases after the wait step means the wait is skipped.","Using an SLA goal as a pause; SLAs raise urgency and escalate, they do not hold the flow."]},{"objective":"Skipping a stage or process based on conditions","modules":["Skipping a Process or a Stage"],"summary":["Stages and processes can be conditional, and their defaults are opposite. A **stage** is never skipped unless you add a 'Skip stage when' condition. A **process** always starts unless you add a 'Start process when' condition. Each condition is a field, comparator and value, and multiple conditions are combined with AND, so all must be true.","Use a skip rule when whole parts of the lifecycle do not apply to some cases, such as skipping an inspection stage for low-value claims or running a reference-check process only for senior hires. If you instead need to jump to a specific stage from the middle of a flow, use a Change Stage automation or a decision shape."],"keyFacts":["Stage default: never skipped. Configure 'Skip stage when'.","Process default: always starts. Configure 'Start process when'.","Condition = field + comparator + value; multiple conditions are ANDed.","For a stage you describe when to SKIP; for a process you describe when to RUN.","Change Stage automation moves the case to a named stage out of sequence."],"table":{"headers":["","Stage","Process"],"rows":[["Default","Never skipped","Always starts"],["Condition label","Skip stage when","Start process when"],["Condition is true means","Stage is skipped","Process runs"]]},"examTraps":["The logic is inverted between stage (skip when) and process (start when).","Multiple conditions are AND, not OR."]},{"objective":"Child cases","modules":["Creating a child Case"],"summary":["A top-level case has no parent. A child case is work that must be completed to resolve the parent, and it lets work run in parallel. The parent cannot resolve until its children do. Use child cases when work is done by different parties, needs separate reporting, runs on a different time frame, or is common to several case types (for example a reusable Body Injury case). In the class hierarchy a child case type sits under the parent's work class.","Create children with the Create Case automation step. Dev Studio adds options such as creating a top-level case instead of a child and creating multiple child cases. When passing data, decide between a **snapshot copy** at creation (fine for data that won't change, such as accident details) and **referencing the parent's data** when it must stay in sync (such as a travel itinerary). Referencing is an advanced Dev Studio configuration. Related tools: the Wait step (case dependency), Update a Case (current case or all child/descendant cases), SLAs calculated from the Parent or Top-level case, and the Related Cases widget."],"keyFacts":["Top-level case = no parent. A parent waits for its children before it resolves.","Reasons: different parties, separate reporting, different time frames, reuse across case types, parallel processing.","Created with the Create Case automation step.","Dev Studio options: top-level vs child, and multiple child cases.","Data propagation copies a snapshot at creation; referencing parent data keeps it synchronized (Dev Studio advanced).","The child case type class sits under the parent's class (e.g. Laptop Request under IT Ticket).","Update a Case automation can update the current case or all child/descendant cases."],"table":{"headers":["","Copy (propagation)","Reference parent data"],"rows":[["Data","Snapshot taken at child creation","Always reads the parent's current values"],["Use for","Data that won't change (accident details)","Data that must stay synchronized (itinerary)"],["Where","Create Case step","Dev Studio (advanced)"]]},"examTraps":["Propagated data does not update in the child when the parent changes.","Child cases are for separate units of work, not just for grouping screens.","Parallel processing for a distinct unit of work suggests a child case, not more steps in the parent."]},{"objective":"Automation shapes","modules":["Automation shapes in the Case Life Cycle","Sending emails during Case processing"],"summary":["Automation steps let the system do work without a person, which saves time and reduces errors. Add them from a step with More > Automations, or in the Process Modeler with Add > Automations. App Studio automations include: Attach content, Change to a Stage, Change to next Stage, Change to previous Stage, Connect Generative AI, Create Case, Create PDF (needs a workaround in Constellation), Generate document, Persist Case (makes a temporary case permanent), Post to Pulse, Push notification, Run Data Transform, Send email, Send notification (multiple channels), Update a Case (current or all child/descendant cases), Search duplicate Cases, Wait (timer or case status), and Questionnaire. A Save Data Page step writes data back to a system of record.","In the Process Modeler, Start is a green circle, End a red circle, an assignment a green rectangle, a subprocess such as Approve/Reject a blue rectangle, an automation a yellow rectangle, and a decision an orange diamond. Automation steps do not get a form view; one is generated automatically only for user steps."],"keyFacts":["Add via Step > More > Automations, or Process Modeler Add > Automations.","Persist Case turns a temporary case into a permanent one.","Run Data Transform copies or sets data (e.g. shipping address to billing).","Send notification targets multiple channels; Send email sends email only.","Update a Case works on the current case or all child/descendant cases.","Wait pauses on a timer or a case status.","Create PDF needs a workaround in Constellation.","Shapes: Start green circle, End red circle, Assignment green rectangle, Subprocess blue rectangle, Automation yellow rectangle, Decision orange diamond.","No form view is generated for automation steps."],"examTraps":["Create Case makes a new (child) case; Persist Case keeps an existing temporary case.","Send email vs Send notification: only Send notification covers several channels.","Change to a Stage goes to a named stage; Change to next/previous Stage is relative."]},{"objective":"Decision tables and decision trees","modules":["Calculating fields using Decision tables","Decision tables and trees"],"summary":["A **decision table** checks the same set of properties across many combinations. Each column is a property or expression, and rows are evaluated top to bottom; the first row whose conditions are all true returns its result. An Otherwise row at the bottom guarantees a result. Setting **Evaluate all rows** (Results tab) returns every matching row instead, which an authority matrix needs, and disables 'Allowed to return values'. A table built in App Studio becomes a DT rule in the Work- class. A Dev Studio table must be marked as a relevant record before App Studio users can use it. Delegation lets process owners make small production changes, and delegation options on the Results tab control what they can change (for example add columns but not rows). Decision tables can also drive flow decision shapes and assignment routing.","A **decision tree** is a set of branches, each with a comparison value, operator and action (return, continue, or evaluate a nested branch), checked top-down with an otherwise value. Use a tree when different properties matter on different branches or when conditions depend on each other. Use a table when the same properties apply everywhere. The wrong choice shows up as a table full of empty cells or a tree full of repeated conditions. **Show conflicts** flags rows or branches that can never be reached (for example 'Score > 900' above 'Score > 1000'); conflicts appear as warnings on save and on the Guardrails landing page, and none are shown when Evaluate all rows is on. **Show completeness** adds rows for missing combinations, which can produce many rows that you decide whether to keep."],"keyFacts":["DT rows are evaluated top to bottom; the first row with all conditions true wins.","An Otherwise result guarantees a return value.","Evaluate all rows returns all matches, disables 'Allowed to return values', and is required for an authority matrix.","Only decision tables (not trees) support authority-matrix cascading approvals.","Table = same properties in many combinations. Tree = different properties or nested, dependent conditions.","Show conflicts finds unreachable rows/branches; warnings appear on save and on the Guardrails landing page.","Show completeness adds rows for missing conditions.","App Studio DTs are created in the Work- class; Dev Studio DTs need relevant-record marking for App Studio use.","Delegation lets business owners change values in production; Results tab controls what they can change.","Trees can be referenced from flows, Declare Expressions, activities and routers."],"table":{"headers":["","Decision table","Decision tree"],"rows":[["Best for","Same properties, many combinations","Different properties per branch, nested logic"],["Evaluation","Rows top to bottom, first full match","Branches top-down, nested"],["Symptom of wrong choice","Many empty cells","Duplicated conditions"],["Authority matrix","Supported (Evaluate all rows)","Not supported"]]},"examTraps":["A broad condition placed above a narrower one hides it; Show conflicts flags the narrower row.","Show completeness adds rows; it does not remove conflicts.","Turning on Evaluate all rows suppresses conflict reporting.","A Dev Studio DT is invisible in App Studio until it is marked as a relevant record."]},{"objective":"Personas","modules":["Inviting users to an application","Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["A persona is the business view of a type of user, managed in App Studio under Users > User Management > Personas tab. The default persona is 'Users'. Creating a persona also creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group (creating an access group does not create a persona). Each persona maps to a role (configured in Dev Studio), but not every role has a persona. Users are invited by email and given a persona or a developer role, and generic shared accounts such as 'admin' should not be used. Autopilot can suggest personas.","In the lifecycle's Personas & Channels section you can draft which personas use which channels in each stage and plan them into releases. Drafting does not configure access: by default a persona can reach all pages. To restrict a portal, assign the persona when you configure the portal (for example a Manager Portal). Several personas can share one channel interface."],"keyFacts":["Managed in App Studio: Users > User Management > Personas tab. Default persona: 'Users'.","Creating a persona creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group; not the reverse.","Each persona has a role, but not every role has a persona.","Persona and channel access can be set per stage; personas can share channels.","Drafting persona/channel mapping in the lifecycle does NOT restrict access; personas see all pages by default.","Restrict a portal by assigning personas when configuring the portal.","Users are invited by email with a persona or developer role; avoid generic accounts like admin."],"examTraps":["Assuming the lifecycle Personas & Channels draft enforces security.","Thinking an access group created in Dev Studio creates a persona.","Persona = business alignment; role = specific permissions. They are not the same object."]}],"questions":[{"id":"cm-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A business analyst is sketching the lifecycle for a mortgage case type and proposes four names for the second stage. Which name follows Pega's stage-naming guidance?","options":["Review the loan application","Step 2 - Credit check","Send application to underwriter for review","Underwriting"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"Stages are milestones and should be a noun or gerund of no more than two words, so 'Underwriting' is correct ('This case is in Underwriting' reads naturally). 'Review the loan application' is a verb + noun action, which is how processes and steps are named. 'Send application to underwriter for review' is a long action phrase that describes a step. 'Step 2 - Credit check' uses step numbering and describes a task, not a milestone.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"A team has been testing several processes that still have unconfigured steps. The release manager asks what must happen with draft mode before the application goes to production. Which two statements are accurate? (Choose two.)","options":["Draft mode must be off before production; leaving it on is a guardrail violation.","Processes are created with draft mode off, so the team must have turned it on manually.","App Studio automatically turns off draft mode when the case type is saved without errors.","Draft mode can be turned off manually only in App Studio.","Processes in draft mode cannot run until every step is fully configured."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Draft mode must be turned off before production, and leaving it on causes a guardrail violation. App Studio clears draft mode automatically when the case type saves with no errors. Processes are created in draft mode by default, so it does not have to be turned on. Turning draft mode off manually is done in Dev Studio, not App Studio. The point of draft mode is that the process can run even when its configuration is incomplete.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-003","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"In an order case, the Fulfillment stage has three steps. The case should show 'Open' for most of the stage, but while the 'Await warehouse pick' step is active the status must read Pending-Fulfillment. What should the system architect configure?","options":["Set the status Pending-Fulfillment on the Fulfillment stage.","Set the status Pending-Fulfillment on the 'Await warehouse pick' step.","Create a custom status 'Warehouse' and apply it to the case type Settings tab.","Add an alternate stage named Pending-Fulfillment and move the case into it with Change Stage."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A status set on a step is more specific than one set on a stage, so setting Pending-Fulfillment on the 'Await warehouse pick' step changes the status only while that step is active. Setting it on the stage would apply it from stage entry, for the whole stage. A custom status goes against the best practice of reusing standard statuses (Pending-Fulfillment already exists), and statuses are not set on the Settings tab. An alternate stage for a status is an over-built workaround that misuses stages, which represent milestones.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-004","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A case type's initial urgency is 10. Its SLA adds 15 at the goal and 20 at the deadline. In Dev Studio a passed-deadline interval adds 10 each day and repeats 3 times. If the case is never resolved, what is the highest urgency the SLA can produce?","options":["45","55","75","100"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Urgency increments are cumulative: 10 + 15 (goal) = 25, + 20 (deadline) = 45, then three passed-deadline increments of 10 = 75. 45 counts only the goal and deadline and ignores the passed deadline. 55 counts only one passed-deadline repetition. 100 is the maximum urgency, but this configuration never reaches it because the passed deadline repeats a fixed 3 times.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-005","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A requirement says: 'After the review deadline is missed, keep escalating to the manager every day until the review is completed.' Which two statements about meeting this requirement are true? (Choose two.)","options":["The repeating escalation is a passed-deadline milestone, which is configured only in Dev Studio on the SLA rule.","The App Studio Goal & deadline panel has a passed-deadline section with repeat options.","A passed deadline can repeat a fixed number of times or until the work is complete.","The goal milestone can be set to repeat daily until the deadline.","Once urgency reaches 100, the passed-deadline escalation actions stop running."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Passed deadline is the milestone that repeats after the deadline, and it is configured only on the Dev Studio SLA rule. It can repeat a fixed number of times or until the work completes, which covers 'until completed'. App Studio Goal & deadline offers goal and deadline but not passed deadline. The goal fires only once. When urgency hits 100, further urgency increments are ignored but escalation actions keep running, so the escalation does not stop.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-006","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A case has an open assignment with urgency 20. A stage SLA then reaches its goal and raises the case urgency to 40. What happens to the urgency of assignments?","options":["The existing assignment is immediately raised to 40.","Assignment urgency is never related to case urgency.","Both the case and the existing assignment are reset to the default urgency of 10.","The existing assignment stays at 20; assignments created after the change start at the new case urgency."],"answer":[3],"explanation":"Changing case urgency affects only new assignments. A new assignment's initial urgency equals the case urgency, so later assignments start at 40 while the existing one stays at 20. The existing assignment is not re-ranked. Nothing resets urgency to 10; that is only the default initial case urgency. Assignment urgency is related to case urgency because new assignments inherit it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-007","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A requirement says that if a claim review assignment misses its deadline, the system must automatically reassign it to a supervisor. In App Studio the architect sees only notification options for escalation. What should the architect do?","options":["Set the deadline urgency increment to 100, which automatically reassigns the assignment.","Configure the reassignment as an escalation action on the SLA rule in Dev Studio.","Add a Change to next Stage automation after the review step.","Configure a stage-only optional action called Reassign."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Dev Studio SLA rules offer advanced escalation actions beyond notifications, including reassigning the work or resolving the case, so this is configured on the SLA rule in Dev Studio. Reaching urgency 100 caps urgency but does not reassign anything. A Change to next Stage automation runs only after the review step completes, which does not happen when the deadline is missed. An optional action needs a user to start it, so it is not automatic.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-008","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A Policy case creates Claim child cases, and each Claim creates Payment child cases. The business requires every Payment's SLA to be measured from when the Policy case started. How should the Payment case type's Goal & deadline be configured?","options":["Use SLA: Never, and rely on the Policy case's SLA.","Calculate time from start of: This Case.","Calculate time from start of: Parent Case.","Calculate time from start of: Top-level Case."],"answer":[3],"explanation":"The Policy case is the top-level case (it has no parent), so 'Top-level Case' measures the Payment's SLA from the Policy's start. 'Parent Case' would measure from the Claim, which is the Payment's direct parent. 'This Case' measures from the Payment's own start. 'Never' removes the Payment SLA, which does not meet the requirement for a Payment SLA.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-009","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"Any of the eight adjusters on the Auto Claims team may handle the 'Assess damage' step, and adjusters should take the next item when they are free instead of having work pushed to them. How should the step be routed?","options":["To the current user","To a specific user's worklist","To the Auto Claims work queue","To the work group manager, who forwards each item"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"A work queue holds shared assignments that any team member can pull, which is exactly the requirement. Routing to the current user leaves the work with whoever created or advanced the case. A specific user's worklist ties the work to one named person. Sending everything to the manager adds a manual bottleneck; managers can send work, but the requirement is for adjusters to pull it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-010","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect is setting up teams for a new application. Which two statements about work groups and work queues are true? (Choose two.)","options":["A work group has a manager and exactly one work queue.","An operator can belong to only one work group.","An assignment in a work queue stays there until a team member pulls it or a manager sends it to someone.","A work queue can be created without a work group if it is only used for routing.","Authorized managers of a work group can perform approvals on its behalf."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Each work group has a manager and exactly one work queue, and a work-queue assignment waits until a member pulls it or a manager sends it. An operator must belong to at least one work group and can belong to many. A work queue requires a work group (and vice versa). Authorized managers can help transfer assignments but cannot perform approvals.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-011","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"Priya, an operations lead, is not a member of the Billing work group but has been added as one of its authorized managers. What can Priya do?","options":["Approve cascading approval steps routed to the Billing manager.","Replace the work group's single work queue with a second one.","Help transfer Billing assignments to other users.","Complete assignments in the Billing work queue."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Authorized managers help transfer assignments and do not have to be members of the work group. They cannot perform approvals. Because Priya is outside the work group, she cannot complete its work-queue assignments. A work group always has exactly one work queue, so adding a second one is not possible.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-012","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"Expense reports under $5,000 need the requester's direct manager. From $5,000 to $25,000 the manager's manager must also approve, and above $25,000 one more level up the chain must approve. All approvers are in the requester's management chain. What should the architect configure?","options":["A cascading approval using an authority matrix populated by a decision tree","A cascading approval using the reporting structure with the number of approvers set to All","A cascading approval using the reporting structure with a Custom number of approvers driven by When rules","Three separate single-level Approve/Reject steps, each routed to a specific user"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Every approver is in the reporting chain and the number of levels depends on amount thresholds, which is what the reporting structure's Custom option (levels decided by When rules) provides. An authority matrix is meant for approvers outside the reporting structure, and decision trees cannot drive it. 'All' would send every expense through the entire hierarchy regardless of amount. Separate steps routed to specific users ignore each requester's own manager and are hard to maintain.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-013","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"A vendor contract must be approved in sequence by the Procurement lead, the Legal counsel and the Finance controller. None of them is in the requester's reporting line, and which people are needed depends on contract type and value. Which approach fits best?","options":["Cascading approval using the reporting structure, Workgroup Manager","Cascading approval using an authority matrix built from a decision table set to Evaluate all rows","A single Approve/Reject step routed to a work queue shared by all three","Cascading approval using the reporting structure, number of approvers One"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"When approvers are outside the reporting structure and depend on case data, use an authority matrix. A decision table set to Evaluate all rows returns every matching approver, and approvals happen in list order. The reporting structure only walks the Reporting Manager or Workgroup Manager chain, so neither reporting-structure option can reach Legal and Finance. A single step routed to a shared queue gets one approval from whoever picks it up, not a sequence of three.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-014","objective":3,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect is building the decision rule behind an authority-matrix cascading approval. Which two statements are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["The decision table must be set to Evaluate all rows, otherwise only one approver is returned.","A decision tree with an otherwise branch can be used instead of a decision table.","Approvals are requested in the order the approvers appear in the list.","All approvers in the list receive the approval at the same time.","Each approver must have a Reporting Manager in their operator record."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"A decision table that is not set to Evaluate all rows stops at the first match and returns only one result, so Evaluate all rows is required. Authority-matrix approvals go in list order. Decision trees cannot drive an authority matrix; only decision tables can. Approvals are sequential, not simultaneous. The Reporting Manager field is used by the reporting-structure option, not by an authority matrix.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-015","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"An architect adds an Approve/Reject step and keeps the default rejection behavior. What happens when the approver rejects the request?","options":["The case continues to the next step with status Pending-Approval.","The case is resolved with the status Resolved-Rejected.","The case moves to the previous stage for rework.","The case is resolved with the status Resolved-Cancelled."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"By default, rejection resolves the case with the status Resolved-Rejected. Continuing or changing stage are other choices you can configure, but they are not the default. Pending-Approval describes a case waiting for approval, not a rejected one. Resolved-Cancelled is a different standard resolution status.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-016","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"A Search duplicate Cases step uses Date of birth as its only basic condition. It has four weighted conditions of 25 each (last name, phone, email, postal code) and a threshold of 50. Case A has the same DOB and matches last name and email. Case B has a different DOB and matches all four weighted conditions. Case C has the same DOB and matches only phone. Which cases are flagged as potential duplicates?","options":["Case A only","Case B only","Cases A and B","Cases A, B and C"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Only cases that match every basic condition become candidates, so Case B (different DOB) is excluded no matter how high its weighted score would be. Case A scores 25 + 25 = 50, which meets the threshold of 50, so it is flagged. Case C scores 25, which is below the threshold. So only Case A is a potential duplicate.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-017","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"A Search duplicate Cases step finds a potential duplicate, and the reviewing user confirms that the new case really is a duplicate. What happens to the current case?","options":["It is merged into the matching case and deleted.","It is converted into a child case of the matching case.","It is resolved with the status Resolved-Cancelled.","It is resolved with the status Resolved-Duplicate."],"answer":[3],"explanation":"Confirming the duplicate resolves the current case with the status Resolved-Duplicate. Cases are not merged or deleted by this step. Resolved-Cancelled is a different status that is not used for duplicates. The step does not create parent-child relationships.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-018","objective":4,"type":"multi","stem":"Which two statements about configuring duplicate case detection are true? (Choose two.)","options":["A case must satisfy all basic conditions to be considered a candidate.","Each weighted condition is given a weight between 1 and 100.","Weighted conditions are evaluated first, and basic conditions are then used to break ties.","When no duplicate is found, the case is resolved automatically.","The total of the weighted conditions must equal exactly 100 for any case to be flagged."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Basic conditions filter candidates and all of them must match. Each weighted condition carries a weight from 1 to 100. Basic conditions run first, and weighted conditions then score the candidates, not the other way round. When there are no matches the case continues; it is not resolved. A candidate is flagged when its weighted sum reaches the configured threshold, which does not have to be 100.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-019","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"Customer service agents must be able to update a customer's contact phone number at any point in the life of a service case, using one screen. What is the best design?","options":["Add an 'Update phone' step to the first process of every stage.","Add a case-wide optional user action.","Add a stage-only optional process to the first stage.","Add an alternate stage and move the case there with Change Stage."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A single-screen task that must be available in every stage is a case-wide user action. Adding a step to every stage forces every case through it and duplicates configuration. A stage-only optional process is limited to one stage, and a process is meant for multi-step work. An alternate stage changes the case's milestone just to edit one field, which is the wrong tool.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-020","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"In the Review stage, the stage-only optional actions are listed in this order: the optional process 'Verify documents', then the user action 'Add note'. The case-wide actions are the built-in Edit details and Change Stage. Which action becomes the promoted action in the Review stage?","options":["Edit details","Verify documents","Add note","Change Stage"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The promoted action is the first stage-wide action other than Edit details, and only user actions (not optional processes) can be promoted. 'Verify documents' is an optional process, so it is skipped and 'Add note' becomes the promoted action. Case-wide actions such as Change Stage are used only when there is no eligible stage-wide action. Edit details is explicitly excluded from the stage-level choice.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-021","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"Which three statements about optional actions are true? (Choose three.)","options":["A user action is a single step, while an optional process can contain multiple steps.","Optional processes can be promoted as a button on the case.","At runtime, users start optional actions from the Actions menu.","Optional actions can be configured only in Dev Studio.","Edit details and Change Stage are built-in case-wide actions."],"answer":[0,2,4],"explanation":"User actions are single-screen tasks and optional processes are multi-step. Users open optional actions from the Actions menu, and Edit details and Change Stage come built in as case-wide actions. Only user actions can be promoted, not optional processes. Optional actions are configured in App Studio with the Optional Actions button on the Workflow tab, so 'only in Dev Studio' is false.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-022","objective":6,"type":"single","stem":"After a 'Review claim' step, claims flagged as suspicious must go to an Investigation alternate stage. Claims under $1,000 go straight to payment, and all others need manager approval. How should the architect model this inside the process?","options":["Add a decision shape with conditional branches; the suspicious branch uses Change to a Stage to go to Investigation.","Set 'Skip stage when' on the Investigation stage so non-suspicious claims bypass it.","Create a child case for every claim and use a Wait step for its resolution.","Add a stage-only optional action that the reviewer runs when a claim looks suspicious."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A decision shape sends the case down different paths based on conditions, and a branch can leave the stage with Change to a Stage, which is the way to reach an alternate stage. Alternate stages are not part of the primary sequence, so a skip condition on one does not route cases into it. Child cases with a wait add separate work items that the requirement does not need. An optional action depends on the user remembering to start it, so the decision is not automated.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-023","objective":6,"type":"multi","stem":"The business wants to change the auto-approval monetary limit several times a year without asking IT to change the process. Which two designs meet this requirement? (Choose two.)","options":["Store the limit in a configuration setting and reference it from the condition through the Condition Builder.","Hard-code the limit in the decision shape's condition and change it in each release.","Put the limit in a delegated decision table that the process owner can update in production.","Store the limit in a new Dynamic System Setting.","Set the limit in pySetFieldDefaults so it loads with every view."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Configuration settings hold parameters such as an approval monetary limit and are referenced through the Condition Builder. Delegated decision tables let process owners make small production changes such as adjusting a threshold. Hard-coding requires a developer release for every change. Configuration settings replace Dynamic System Settings, so a new DSS is not the recommended choice. pySetFieldDefaults is system-maintained and should not be modified.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-024","objective":7,"type":"single","stem":"While working on a 'Prepare quote' assignment, agents sometimes need to record a customer callback note. Doing so must not complete the assignment or move the case forward. How should the architect configure the note action?","options":["As a connector flow action leading to the next step","As a Send notification automation after the assignment","As a Change to next Stage automation","As a local flow action configured in the assignment properties"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"A local action updates the assignment without advancing it and is configured in the assignment properties, which matches the requirement. A connector action completes the assignment and advances the flow. Change to next Stage moves the case forward, which is the opposite of what is required. A notification automation runs only after the assignment completes and does not capture input.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-025","objective":7,"type":"multi","stem":"Which two statements about flow actions are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["Validate rules are assigned to flow actions, so validation can differ by screen.","A connector action completes the assignment and advances the flow.","A local action is defined on the connector that leaves the assignment.","Flow actions created in Dev Studio can never be reused in App Studio.","A flow action defines the SLA goal for the assignment it belongs to."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Validate rules are attached to flow actions, which lets the same field have different validation on different forms, and a connector action completes the assignment and moves the flow on. Local actions are configured in the assignment properties, not on a connector. Flow actions (user actions) can be marked as relevant records so App Studio can reuse them. SLAs are separate rules applied at case, stage, process or step level, not part of a flow action.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-026","objective":8,"type":"single","stem":"A Property Purchase case creates several Inspection child cases early in its lifecycle. In a later stage the parent must pause until every Inspection is resolved. Some inspections may already be Resolved-Completed before the parent reaches that point. How should the Wait step be configured?","options":["Case dependency on all Inspection children, status 'To be resolved', considering only changes after the parent reaches the wait","Case dependency on all Inspection children, status 'To be resolved', considering the children's current status","Case dependency on any Inspection child, status Resolved-Completed","Timer wait using a reference date/time property for the expected inspection date"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"'To be resolved' matches any status that starts with Resolved, 'all' waits for every Inspection, and considering current status counts children that resolved before the parent arrived. If only changes after the wait are considered, children that are already resolved never trigger again and the parent could wait forever. 'Any' resumes after the first child resolves, and Resolved-Completed alone misses other resolved statuses. A timer resumes at a date, not when the work is done.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-027","objective":8,"type":"single","stem":"A welcome-kit process must pause and resume three days after the date stored in the case field Policy Start Date. Which configuration should the architect use?","options":["A Wait step of type Timer that references a date/time property","A Wait step of type Case dependency","An SLA with a goal of three days","An assignment routed to a work queue that users process after three days"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A Timer wait can resume at a date/time taken from a property, which is the right tool for 'relative to Policy Start Date'. A case-dependency wait waits on child case statuses, not dates. An SLA goal raises urgency and can escalate, but it does not pause the flow. Routing to a work queue depends on people remembering, which is not an automatic pause.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-028","objective":8,"type":"multi","stem":"Which two statements about Wait steps are true? (Choose two.)","options":["For a case-dependency wait, if the child cases have not been created before the case reaches the wait step, the wait is skipped.","A Wait step is the same as a case waiting at an assignment for a user to act.","A case-dependency wait can be configured to let a user cancel the wait.","A timer wait can be configured only in Dev Studio.","The 'To be resolved' option matches only the Resolved-Completed status."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Child cases must exist before the wait step, otherwise the wait is skipped, and a case-dependency wait can allow a user to cancel it. A wait step is a system pause, which is different from a case sitting at a user assignment. Wait (timer or case status) is an App Studio automation, so timers are not Dev Studio-only. 'To be resolved' matches any status beginning with Resolved, not just Resolved-Completed.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-029","objective":9,"type":"single","stem":"In a hiring case, the Background Check stage applies only to candidates with no previous employment at the company. Within the Review stage, a Reference Check process should run only for senior roles. What should the architect configure?","options":["'Start process when' on Background Check, and 'Skip stage when' on Reference Check","'Skip stage when' on Background Check (condition: has previous employment), and 'Start process when' on Reference Check (condition: role is senior)","'Skip stage when' on Background Check (condition: no previous employment), and 'Start process when' on Reference Check (condition: role is not senior)","A decision shape in the Create stage that resolves the case for returning employees"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Stages use 'Skip stage when' and processes use 'Start process when'. To skip Background Check for returning employees the condition is 'has previous employment', and to run Reference Check for senior roles the condition is 'role is senior'. The first option puts the condition types on the wrong elements. The third option has the logic inverted, so it would skip the check for new hires and run references for junior roles. Resolving the case is not the requirement; returning employees still continue through the other stages.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-030","objective":9,"type":"single","stem":"A stage has two 'Skip stage when' conditions: Order total < 100 and Customer tier = Gold. When is the stage skipped?","options":["When either condition is true","Only when both conditions are true","Never, because stages always run unless they have a single condition","Only when neither condition is true"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Multiple conditions are combined with AND, so the stage is skipped only when both are true. 'Either' describes OR logic, which is not how multiple conditions combine. Stages do support multiple conditions. 'Neither' inverts the logic.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-031","objective":10,"type":"multi","stem":"In which two situations is a child case the right design choice? (Choose two.)","options":["A medical review is done by a separate team in parallel with the main claim and has its own reporting.","Body-injury handling is identical across auto, home and travel claim case types and should be reused.","Agents need one screen to update a customer's address at any time.","The case must pause three days before sending a reminder.","A long form should be split into several screens for readability."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Child cases suit work done by a different party, in parallel, with separate reporting, and work common to several case types that should be reused. A one-screen update at any time is a case-wide optional user action. A three-day pause is a Timer wait step. Splitting a form into several screens is a multi-step form.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cm-032","objective":10,"type":"single","stem":"A Business Trip parent case creates Hotel Booking child cases. The travel itinerary on the parent changes often, and every child must always show the current itinerary. Which approach should the architect use?","options":["Data propagation on the Create Case step, which copies the itinerary when the child is created","Have the child reference the parent's itinerary data, an advanced configuration in Dev Studio","A Run Data Transform automation in each child after it is created","A Wait step in each child that resumes when the parent changes status"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Data that must stay synchronized should be referenced from the parent; this is an advanced Dev Studio configuration. Data propagation copies a snapshot when the child is created, so later itinerary changes are not reflected. A data transform run once after creation is also a one-time copy. A Wait step pauses processing but does not keep data in sync.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-033","objective":10,"type":"single","stem":"An architect needs a Create Case step that starts a new top-level case instead of a child, or creates multiple child cases in one step. Where are these options configured?","options":["In App Studio, on the case type Settings tab","In the Optional Actions dialog of the Workflow tab","In the Personas & Channels section of the lifecycle","In Dev Studio"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"The Create Case automation adds child cases in App Studio, while Dev Studio provides the options to create a top-level case instead of a child and to create multiple child cases. The Settings tab manages case-level items such as urgency and goal & deadline. Personas & Channels drafts user access per stage. The Optional Actions dialog configures user-initiated actions, not case creation.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-034","objective":11,"type":"multi","stem":"After the 'Capture shipping details' step, the system must (1) copy the shipping address into the billing address, (2) tell the customer the order was accepted by email and by mobile push, and (3) move the case directly into the Fulfillment stage. Which three automation steps should the architect add? (Choose three.)","options":["Run Data Transform","Persist Case","Send notification","Change to a Stage","Questionnaire"],"answer":[0,2,3],"explanation":"Run Data Transform copies data such as a shipping address into billing. Send notification can reach several channels at once, covering both email and push. Change to a Stage moves the case to a named stage. Persist Case turns a temporary case into a permanent one, which is not required here. Questionnaire collects answers to questions and does nothing to move data or notify.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-035","objective":11,"type":"single","stem":"A quick-quote case is created as a temporary case so abandoned quotes are not stored. If the customer accepts the quote, the case must be kept permanently. Which automation step achieves this?","options":["Create Case","Update a Case","Persist Case","Change to next Stage"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Persist Case turns a temporary case into a permanent one. Create Case starts a new case rather than keeping the current one. Update a Case changes field values in the current case or its descendants but does not change whether it is stored. Change to next Stage moves the lifecycle forward but leaves the case temporary.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-036","objective":12,"type":"single","stem":"A credit decision table has these rows in order: Row 1: Credit score > 900 returns 'Gold'. Row 2: Credit score > 1000 returns 'Platinum'. Otherwise returns 'Standard'. The architect clicks Show conflicts. What is reported?","options":["Row 2 can never be reached because any score over 1000 already matches Row 1.","Row 1 can never be reached because Row 2 is more specific.","The table is incomplete, and rows are added for scores of 900 or below.","No conflict, because the Otherwise result covers every score."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Rows are evaluated top to bottom and the first match wins. Any score over 1000 is also over 900, so it stops at Row 1 and Row 2 is unreachable, which is what Show conflicts reports. Row 1 is reachable (scores 901 to 1000). Adding missing rows is what Show completeness does, not Show conflicts. Otherwise guarantees a result but does not fix an unreachable row.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-037","objective":12,"type":"single","stem":"The discount rule works like this: for Gold customers the discount depends on region; for Silver customers it depends on the number of past orders; Bronze customers always get 0%. Modeled as a decision table, most cells would be empty. Which rule type fits better, and why?","options":["A decision table with Evaluate all rows, because it returns all matching discounts","A decision tree, because different properties matter on each branch and conditions are nested","A When rule, because the result is a single value","A decision table with Show completeness, to fill in the empty cells"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Decision trees suit logic where different properties are evaluated on different branches and conditions depend on earlier ones; a table full of empty cells is the warning sign of choosing the wrong rule. Evaluate all rows returns many results, but one discount is needed. A When rule returns only true or false. Show completeness adds rows for missing combinations, which would make the table bigger, not fix the design.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-038","objective":12,"type":"multi","stem":"Which two statements about decision tables are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["Rows are evaluated from top to bottom, and the first row whose conditions are all true returns its result.","An Otherwise result at the bottom makes sure the table always returns a value.","Show completeness removes rows that can never be reached.","Turning on Evaluate all rows makes conflict detection report more problems.","A decision table created in Dev Studio is automatically available in App Studio."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Decision tables return the first row that fully matches, evaluating top down, and Otherwise guarantees a result. Show completeness adds rows for missing conditions; Show conflicts is what finds unreachable rows. With Evaluate all rows on, no conflicts are shown. A Dev Studio decision table must be marked as a relevant record before App Studio users can use it.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-039","objective":13,"type":"single","stem":"In the lifecycle's Personas & Channels section, the architect drafted that the Manager persona uses only the Manager Portal. In testing, managers can still reach every page of the Web Portal. What explains this, and what is the fix?","options":["Drafting persona-channel mappings does not configure access; restrict the portal by assigning personas when configuring the portal.","The draft takes effect only after the case type leaves draft mode, so turn draft mode off.","Personas cannot be restricted; create a separate application for managers.","The Manager persona is missing a work queue; add one in Dev Studio."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Drafting persona and channel mappings in the lifecycle is a planning aid and does not set access. By default a persona can reach all pages, so access must be restricted by assigning personas when configuring the portal. Process draft mode is unrelated to persona access. Personas can be restricted, so a separate application is not needed. A work queue is created automatically with a persona and has nothing to do with portal visibility.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cm-040","objective":13,"type":"multi","stem":"An architect creates a new 'Claims Supervisor' persona in App Studio. Which two things are true? (Choose two.)","options":["Default permissions and a work queue are created for the persona.","An access group is created automatically for the persona.","If an access group had been created in Dev Studio instead, a matching persona would have been created automatically.","Every access role in the application must have a persona.","The new persona replaces the default persona, which is named Admin."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Creating a persona creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group. This does not work in reverse: creating an access group does not create a persona. Every persona has a role, but not every role has a persona. The default persona is 'Users', not Admin, and adding personas does not replace it; generic accounts such as admin are discouraged.","difficulty":"medium"}]},{"id":"data-integration","title":"Data and Integration","weight":20,"intro":"The second-largest domain: how Pega models data (fields, data objects, relationships), keeps it correct (calculations, validation, defaults), and connects it to systems of record through data pages, refresh strategies, savable data pages and simulation. Expect scenario questions that hinge on one configuration word such as Refer vs Copy, Thread vs Node, or Lookup vs Data Transform.","objectives":[{"objective":"Explain the data model and its levels (conceptual, logical, physical)","modules":["The Data Model"],"summary":["A data model is a picture of the data an application needs and how the pieces connect. It pays off in reuse, easier maintenance and the ability to adapt and scale; in practice the Lead System Architect reviews it before any data objects are created. Pega describes three levels of refinement. The conceptual model is drafted by the Business Architect with stakeholders and names entities, their attributes and the relationships between them; it is a living document whose purpose is to cut rework later. The logical model translates that picture into Pega terms: entities become data objects and attributes become fields. The physical model decides how data is stored and accessed, which is where integration settings live.","In App Studio the Integration Designer is the single place to see data objects, data pages, their dependencies and the external systems behind them, with an optional Integration Map diagram on top. The physical level is visible there. Each data object also has a visual Data Model diagram where fields can be added, changed or removed. All of this supports the center-out idea: the data access layer is insulated from business logic, so moving a system of record (for example from on-premises to the cloud) does not force changes to the logic or the views."],"keyFacts":["Conceptual model: owned by the Business Architect and stakeholders; entities, attributes, relationships; living document; reduces rework.","Logical model: Pega-specific refinement; entity maps to a data object, attribute maps to a field.","Physical model: storage and access details plus integration settings; surfaced in the Integration Designer.","The LSA verifies the data model before data objects are created.","Integration Designer (App Studio) = one place for data objects, data pages, dependencies and external systems; Integration Map is optional.","Data pages decouple integration from how the application uses data, so a source can change without changing views.","Center-out: data access layer insulated; changing the system of record does not require logic changes.","Benefits named for a data model: reuse, maintenance, adaptability/scalability."],"table":{"headers":["Level","Who / focus","Pega artifact"],"rows":[["Conceptual","Business Architect + stakeholders; entities, attributes, relationships","Living design document (no rules yet)"],["Logical","Refined for Pega","Data objects (entities) and fields (attributes)"],["Physical","How data is stored and accessed","Integration settings, data pages, external systems in Integration Designer"]]},"examTraps":["Mapping entities to data objects is the logical level, not the conceptual one; the conceptual level is still tool-neutral.","Integration settings and storage choices belong to the physical model; do not pick 'logical' just because data objects are mentioned.","The conceptual model is not frozen after sign-off; it is described as a living document."]},{"objective":"Describe data types, data objects, relationships, data records and field types","modules":["Capturing and presenting data","The Data Model","Creating a data relationship"],"summary":["A field is a named, reusable element that holds one value per case (Dev Studio calls it a property). App Studio guesses the type from the name you type (default Text (single line); a name containing 'date' becomes Date only), so always check before submitting. Type choice matters: Integer drops leading zeros, so IDs and postal codes that are never used in arithmetic should be Text; money should be Currency (codes and separators) rather than Decimal; Date & time values are normalized to UTC/GMT; Email, Phone and URL render as action links and Email/URL include validation; Percentage shows .5 as 50%; Address adapts its fields to the selected country; User reference points at operators mapped to the app. Fancy types include Attachment and Location.","A data object is a template that groups related fields together with their views, rules and calculations, and can be reused across case types and even several times in one case (source and target account). Objects can inherit (Person-Customer) and can be sourced from a local Pega system of record, an external SOR, or nothing at all when the case captures the data. Best practice: use a standard object such as Address-Postal or Address-Email, extend one that partially fits (Person-Employee), and only then create a new one. A data object added from the workflow starts as a draft and must be made permanent on the Data objects and integrations page. A data record is one instance of a data object; locally it is a row in a database table shown on the Records tab. Changing records is a data change, while changing the fields is a process change. Records from an external source are not stored in Pega.","Relationships are containers that relate data without storing it themselves. Embedded data keeps user-entered data inside the case in a consistent format. Data reference reuses a data object that lives outside the case (local records or external SOR). Case reference pulls data from another case. Query is a read-only, parameterized lookup of external data. Each can be a single record or a list. Data reference supports Single, List and Many to Many modes."],"keyFacts":["Default inferred field type is Text (single line); names containing 'date' become Date only.","Integer strips leading zeros; use Text (single line) for IDs and zip codes without arithmetic.","Use Currency, not Decimal, for money; Date & time is stored normalized to UTC/GMT.","Email, Phone and URL show as action links; Email and URL include built-in validation.","Data object sourcing: local Pega SOR, external SOR, or none (entered in the case).","Reuse order: standard object (Address-Postal, Address-Email) -> extend a partial fit (Person-Employee) -> create new.","Draft data objects created from the workflow must be made permanent on the Data objects and integrations page.","Editing records = data change; adding/changing fields = process change.","Embedded list UI: Table or Field Group; Case reference UI: Autocomplete, Dropdown, Table; Data reference list UI: Table, Simple table, Combobox, Checkbox group.","Query fields need a list or lookup data page, use external parameters, and do not store the key."],"table":{"headers":["Relationship field type","Where the data lives","Typical example"],"rows":[["Embedded data","Inside the case (entered by the user)","Payment information (single); Ordered items (list)"],["Data reference","Outside the case: local records or external SOR","Ordering customer; Product selected in a line item"],["Case reference","Another case","Related policy case shown in a claim"],["Query","External, read-only, parameterized via a data page","Lookup that is displayed but not stored by key"]]},"examTraps":["Numeric-looking identifiers (badge numbers, zip codes) are a trap for Integer: leading zeros are lost.","Embedded data copies values into the case; for shared catalog-style data that lives elsewhere, pick Data reference.","Query is read-only and does not store the key, so it is wrong when the user's selection must be saved.","Draft data objects are not automatically permanent."]},{"objective":"Configure calculated values","modules":["Capturing and presenting data"],"summary":["App Studio lets a field be calculated instead of entered, in either a case type's or a data object's data model. There are three calculation kinds. Functions iterate over a list and return the Sum, Average, Maximum or Minimum of a field; they only produce Decimal, Currency or Integer results. Expressions combine fields with the dot operator, arithmetic (+ - * /), parentheses and AND/OR, and can concatenate text with quoted literals; they can return any simple type except Email, Phone and Picklist. Decision tables map conditions to a result.","Calculated fields form a calculation network: when an input changes, every dependent field recalculates automatically. Creating a custom decision table from App Studio is only available in Constellation applications; a decision table built in Dev Studio can be selected in App Studio only after it is marked as a relevant record. Decision tables evaluate rows top to bottom, return the first row whose conditions are all true, and use an 'otherwise' result as a guaranteed fallback."],"keyFacts":["Three calculation types in App Studio: Functions, Expressions, Decision tables.","Functions iterate a list: Sum of, Average of, Maximum of, Minimum of.","Function results limited to Decimal, Currency and Integer.","Expressions: dot operator, + - * /, parentheses, AND/OR, text concatenation with quotes.","Expressions can return any simple type except Email, Phone and Picklist.","Custom decision tables in App Studio: Constellation apps only.","Dev Studio decision tables must be marked relevant records to be used in App Studio.","The calculation network recalculates dependent fields automatically when inputs change.","Decision table: first matching row wins (top to bottom); 'otherwise' guarantees a result."],"table":{"headers":["Calculation type","Use it for","Restriction"],"rows":[["Function","Aggregate a list (sum, average, max, min)","Result must be Decimal, Currency or Integer"],["Expression","Arithmetic/logic on fields; concatenation","Not for Email, Phone, Picklist results"],["Decision table","Condition combinations mapped to a result","Custom DT in App Studio only for Constellation; Dev Studio DT must be relevant"]]},"examTraps":["Totaling a field across an embedded list is a Function (Sum of), not an Expression.","A Picklist result cannot come from an Expression even though Text can.","In a Traditional UI app, a missing App Studio option to build a decision table is expected behaviour, not a permissions problem."]},{"objective":"Apply validation rules and business logic to data","modules":["Validating data against business logic","Validating data in Dev Studio"],"summary":["Validation answers three questions: is the value the right type (handled by field type and control), is it one of the allowed values (lists/picklists), and does it make business sense (conditions). A calendar control guarantees a real date, but not that the date is in the past; that needs a business condition. In App Studio, a validation condition describes the invalid case and produces an error that blocks progress until fixed. The comparison can be against a constant or another field. Validations attach to a specific step instance, so the same field can carry different rules on different forms (a previous job's start date must be past; a new hire's start date must be future).","App Studio offers two trigger points: on form submit, where the user can correct the value immediately, and on stage entry, configured in the case type's Data model, which stops the case from entering the stage. With stage-entry validation the developer must give the user a way out, such as going back to a previous stage or closing the case. In Dev Studio, Validate rules (Process category) are attached to flow actions and extend App Studio validations; their Input tab can qualify when they run by input property, proposed work status, flow action or stage (stage-based equals App Studio stage-entry validation). Edit Validate rules use Java pattern checks on a property and are a security risk because of custom Java; set on the property's Advanced tab they run client-side as soon as the value changes, or they can be called from a Validate rule to run on submit. A library of validation functions covers checks like 'date within the past N weeks' or 'attachment of a category is present'."],"keyFacts":["Three validation needs: correct type, allowed values, business logic.","Calendar control ensures a valid date, not a past/future date.","App Studio validation conditions define the INVALID values.","Validations are tied to process step instances, so one field can have different validation per form.","Two App Studio trigger points: form submit and stage entry (stage entry configured in the case type Data model).","Stage-entry failures block entry; the designer must provide a recovery path.","Validate rules: Dev Studio, Process category, attached to flow actions; Input tab qualifies by property, proposed work status, flow action, stage.","Edit Validate rules: custom Java pattern checks (security risk); client-side immediately when set via 'Use Validate' on the property Advanced tab.","Validation function library: e.g., date within past N weeks, attachment of a category attached."],"table":{"headers":["Mechanism","When it fires","Configured in"],"rows":[["Form-submit validation","User submits the step's form","App Studio (step/view)"],["Stage-entry validation","Case tries to enter the stage","App Studio case type Data model (stage-based Validate rule)"],["Validate rule","Flow action submit, qualified on Input tab","Dev Studio"],["Edit Validate on property","Client-side on value change","Dev Studio property Advanced tab"]]},"examTraps":["Required = Always forces a value but does not check business meaning.","Stage-entry validation is not 'immediate feedback on the form' — that is form-submit validation.","Edit Validate rules are not best practice for general logic: custom Java weakens security."]},{"objective":"Use data transforms and set default values (pyDefault, Data initialization, superclass)","modules":["Application data manipulation","Application data manipulation in Dev Studio"],"summary":["Data transforms copy and convert data (for example a text 'December 4, 2021' into a date), move it, calculate values, format it, and set defaults. In App Studio they run from a Run Data Transform automation step or as pre- or post-processing on a step; a classic case is post-processing on a shipping-information step that copies the shipping address to billing. In Dev Studio a flow connector can apply a data transform (Set properties > Apply Data Transform), and transforms can loop through lists, for example copying only child items with quantity above zero to the parent.","Defaults are best set on the Data initialization page of the case type Settings tab, which writes into the pyDefault data transform; use pyDefault directly for complex logic. pyDefault runs when the case is created and calls pySetFieldDefaults. pySetFieldDefaults runs when a view loads and is maintained by the system, so do not edit it. Both are generated the first time you create a view. To read operator details such as pyFirstName in a Dev Studio transform, the OperatorID page must be declared on the Pages & Classes tab. Functions such as @CurrentDateTime() and @addCalendar(...) help compute values.","The superclass feature chains same-named transforms through the class hierarchy. When 'Call superclass data transform' is checked, the runtime finds each parent transform up to the highest class and runs them top-down, so the most specific class runs last and its values win. It is turned on automatically when App Studio created default fields. Work- pyDefault sets status, urgency 10 and organization info; a case type pyDefault can override, for example urgency 40."],"keyFacts":["Data transform uses: copy/convert types, move data, calculate, format, set defaults.","App Studio entry points: Run Data Transform automation, step pre-/post-processing.","Dev Studio: apply a transform on a flow connector (Set properties > Apply Data Transform).","Best practice for simple defaults: Data initialization page (Settings tab) which updates pyDefault.","pyDefault runs at case creation and calls pySetFieldDefaults.","pySetFieldDefaults runs on view load, is system-maintained and should not be modified.","Both pyDefault and pySetFieldDefaults are created the first time a view is created.","OperatorID page must be on the Pages & Classes tab to reference pyFirstName/pyLastName.","Superclass chaining: parents run top-down, subclass values override; Work- pyDefault sets urgency 10.","For parent-child data, referencing avoids the synchronization issues of copying."],"examTraps":["Do not customize pySetFieldDefaults for complex logic; use pyDefault.","With superclass enabled the subclass runs last, so its value (e.g., urgency 40) wins over Work- (10).","A reference to OperatorID failing in a transform is usually a missing Pages & Classes entry, not a relevant-record issue."]},{"objective":"Configure sourced data: data pages, scope, sources, parameters, refresh strategies, refer vs copy, and populating UI controls","modules":["Accessing sourced data in a Case"],"summary":["Data pages (prefix D_) retrieve and cache data and keep integration details out of the process; they also support persistence and services. They load on demand when referenced: the system checks the cache, queries the source if the page is missing or stale, and returns the result. A data object with a system of record gets three default pages: a list (D_xxxList), a single-record lookup (D_xxx) and a savable page (D_xxxSavable). The definition sets the structure (Page or List, with list items in pxResults), the object type (class), the edit mode (Read-Only, Editable, or Savable with a save plan) and the scope: Thread (per case/transaction), Requestor (per user session) or Node (shared by all users).","Sources include Data Transform, Activity, Connector, Report definition, Database lookup, Robotic Automation, Robotic desktop Automation, Lookup and Aggregate sources (several sources run in listed order). With multiple conditional sources the first true condition wins and the last is the Otherwise. Parameters narrow the content (lookup key, report filter) using D_Name[p1:v1,p2:v2]; each distinct parameter combination is its own instance unless 'Limit to a single data page' is set on the Load Management tab. Parameters have a name, data type, required flag and In/Out direction.","Refresh strategies are optional but strongly recommended; the page reloads if any configured condition is met, and never more than once per interaction. 'Reload once per interaction' works only for Thread or Requestor scope and cannot be combined with other options. 'Do not reload when' treats the page as current while its When condition is true. 'Reload if older than' marks the page stale after an interval, and it reloads on the next access. A property can Refer to a data page (always current, no copy held by the case, a parameter change points it at a new instance) or Copy data from it (a point-in-time snapshot stored in the case, re-retrieved only when parameters change). For list controls such as dropdowns, only the selected value is copied to the case, and it can differ from the displayed value."],"keyFacts":["Default data pages for a data object with a SOR: D_xxxList, D_xxx (lookup), D_xxxSavable.","List structure stores rows in pxResults, e.g., D_X.pxResults(n).","Scope: Thread = per case/transaction; Requestor = per user session; Node = shared by all users.","Multiple sources: conditions evaluated in order, first true wins, last is Otherwise; Aggregate sources run in listed order.","Each parameter combination creates a separate instance unless 'Limit to a single data page' (Load Management tab).","Refresh: reload if ANY condition is met, never more than once per interaction.","'Reload once per interaction' is Thread/Requestor only and cannot be combined with other options.","'Reload if older than' does not reload in the background; it reloads on the next access after going stale.","Refer = always current, nothing stored in case; Copy = snapshot stored, refreshed only when parameters change.","List controls copy only the selected value; Constellation cascading dropdowns use two Picklist fields and a parameterized data page."],"table":{"headers":["Setting","Option","Behavior / example"],"rows":[["Scope","Thread","One per case/transaction (stock price for this transaction)"],["Scope","Requestor","One per user session (this customer's shipping addresses)"],["Scope","Node","Shared by all users (vehicle makes and models)"],["Refresh","Reload once per interaction","Thread/Requestor only; cannot combine (flight seating)"],["Refresh","Do not reload when","When true, page is current (stocks after market close)"],["Refresh","Reload if older than","Stale after interval; reloads on next access"],["Autopopulate","Refer to a data page","Always current; no copy in case"],["Autopopulate","Copy data from a data page","Snapshot stored in case; re-fetch only when parameters change"]]},"examTraps":["'Reload once per interaction' on a Node-scoped page is invalid.","Refresh strategies are optional (though strongly recommended); a missing strategy is not a save error.","If the case must remember values as they were at a point in time, choose Copy, not Refer.","Parameterized pages create one instance per parameter set; this is expected, not a leak."]},{"objective":"Save data to a system of record (savable data pages, save plans, Save Data Page automation)","modules":["Saving data to a system of record"],"summary":["A savable data page writes a page or page list back to a system of record, inside Pega or external, in real time with transaction handling, so a failure rolls back both the case and the data save. The save plan lists save options, each with a When condition and an Otherwise: Database save (one page to one row, page structure only, transactional, mapped with a data transform), Database update, Database delete, Connector (REST only, not SOAP), Robotic automation (unattended RPA), Robotic desktop automation (RDA) and Activity (custom code; can call any connector including SOAP; not inherently transactional, so use sparingly).","The save is triggered by a Save Data Page automation step placed after the assignment, by post-processing on a flow action, or by the Save-DataPage method in an activity (which can choose the same or a separate transaction). In the Save Data Page step you either name the data page and its parameters explicitly or pick 'Use associated property' to take the page and parameters from an autopopulated property; one step per data source, and you can add an audit note. If a property is autopopulated from a savable data page, use Copy, not Refer, to avoid overwriting. To support both create and update, make the key parameter optional: when a key is supplied a Lookup source loads the existing record; when it is not, a Data Transform source initializes a new one. Best practice is to give savable pages used for creation an Index parameter (unless autopopulating by copy), which avoids a guardrail warning and allows multiple uses on one screen. Data objects also offer an Update Details action for changing only the needed information."],"keyFacts":["Savable data pages save in real time with transaction handling; failures roll back case and data together.","Database save: single page to single row; page structure only; transactional.","Connector save option supports REST only; SOAP requires the Activity option.","Activity save option is not inherently transactional; limit its use.","Triggers: Save Data Page step (after the assignment), flow action post-processing, Save-DataPage activity method.","Save Data Page step: explicit page + parameters, or 'Use associated property'; one step per source; optional audit note.","Autopopulated property backed by a savable page: use Copy, not Refer.","Update existing: key present -> Lookup source. Create new: no key -> Data Transform source. Key optional for both.","Best practice: Index parameter on savable pages that create records (avoids guardrail, multiple uses on a screen)."],"table":{"headers":["Save option","Notes"],"rows":[["Database save","One page -> one row; page structure only; transactional; data transform mapping"],["Database update / delete","Update or remove existing rows"],["Connector","REST only"],["Robotic automation / RDA","Unattended RPA / desktop automation"],["Activity","Custom; any connector incl. SOAP; not inherently transactional"]]},"examTraps":["Connector save option does not support SOAP.","Database save does not handle page lists into multiple rows; it is one page to one row.","Using Refer with a savable page risks overwriting user edits; Copy also helps avoid stale page errors.","Multiple target systems mean multiple Save Data Page steps, not one step with several sources."]},{"objective":"Simulate data for development and testing","modules":["Accessing sourced data in a Case","The Data Model","Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["When an external system is not available yet, has no data, or its API is still being built, a data page can use a simulated data source as a temporary stand-in. You need to know the expected request and response so the simulation matches reality. Simulation lets the UI team keep working and is useful for unit testing because you control the response. It must be replaced by the real source before production.","For local data, Autopilot can fill a data object with realistic sample records from the Records tab ('Generate sample records with Autopilot', choosing how many); values match the field types (an Email field gets email-shaped values). This is a good substitute for a system of record that is not integrated yet during testing."],"keyFacts":["Simulate when the external system is unavailable, has no data, or the API is still in development.","Expected request and response structures must be known.","Simulation dictates responses, which helps unit testing; the UI team is not blocked.","Simulated data pages are temporary: configure the real source before production.","Autopilot generates sample records on a data object's Records tab; you choose the count and values match field types."],"examTraps":["Simulation is never acceptable as the production source, even if responses look correct.","Autopilot sample records do not sync to the external system later; they are test data only."]}],"questions":[{"id":"di-001","objective":0,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"Before any rules exist, a Business Architect runs workshops with claims stakeholders to list entities such as Policy, Claimant and Vehicle, their key attributes, and how they relate. Which data model level is being produced?","options":["Integration Map","Conceptual data model","Logical data model","Physical data model"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The conceptual model is created by the Business Architect with stakeholders and captures entities, attributes and relationships before anything Pega-specific is decided. The logical model is wrong because it is the step that maps those entities to Pega data objects and fields. The physical model is wrong because it concerns storage, access and integration settings. The Integration Map is wrong because it is an optional diagram in the Integration Designer, not a modeling level."},{"id":"di-002","objective":0,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The Vehicle entity has already been mapped to a Vehicle data object with fields. The team now has to decide whether Vehicle records will be stored in a Pega table or retrieved from a state registry REST service, and configure the related integration settings. Which level of the data model is this, and where in App Studio would the team review the result?","options":["Logical level; reviewed on the case type's Data model tab","Conceptual level; reviewed in the Application profile","Physical level; reviewed in the Integration Designer","Physical level; reviewed in the Agile Workbench feature map"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Storage and access decisions, including integration settings, are the physical level, and the Integration Designer is the App Studio location that shows data objects, data pages, dependencies and external systems. Logical is wrong because the entity-to-data-object mapping is already done; that was the logical step. Conceptual is wrong because it is the tool-neutral stakeholder view, and the Application profile tracks features and stories rather than data sources. The Agile Workbench feature map is a backlog tool, not where integration settings are viewed."},{"id":"di-003","objective":0,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"An architect is explaining why the team should invest in a well-structured data model and data pages. Which statements are accurate? (Choose two.)","options":["Data pages separate integration from how the application uses data, so the source can be replaced without changing views.","Each channel should keep its own copy of the data model so that channels can evolve independently.","Because the data access layer is insulated, moving the system of record from on-premises to the cloud does not require changing business logic.","Once stakeholders approve the conceptual model, it becomes fixed and changes require a new application.","The physical data model must be completed before the conceptual model so that storage drives the design."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Center-out design insulates the data access layer, so a system of record can move without logic changes, and data pages decouple integration from views so a source can change underneath them. Per-channel copies of the data model describe the bottom-up/top-down problems (duplicate, siloed logic and data) that center-out avoids. The conceptual model is a living document meant to reduce rework, not a frozen artifact. The order is conceptual, then logical, then physical; storage does not come first."},{"id":"di-004","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"An HR case captures an employee badge number such as 000731. The value is displayed and searched but never used in calculations. A developer accepted App Studio's suggestion of Integer for the field, and testers report that badges appear as 731. What is the best fix?","options":["Keep Integer and add an Edit Validate rule that pads the value with zeros","Change the field type to Text (single line)","Change the field type to Picklist and load all badge numbers into it","Change the field type to Decimal and set zero decimal places"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Integer strips leading zeros, so identifiers that need them and are never used in arithmetic should be Text (single line). Decimal is still numeric and has the same problem with leading zeros. An Edit Validate rule checks values with custom Java patterns; it does not reformat storage and adds security risk. A Picklist constrains choices to a list and is inappropriate for a unique identifier per employee."},{"id":"di-005","objective":1,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"In an Order case, a customer can order several items. For each item the user enters a quantity and selects a product from the company product catalog, which is maintained as a Product data object with its own records. How should the relationships be modeled? (Choose two.)","options":["Product (inside each ordered item) as an Embedded data field, single record, so catalog values are copied into the case","Product (inside each ordered item) as a Data reference field, single record","Product as a Query field so the selected product key is stored in each ordered item","Ordered items as a Case reference field, list of records","Ordered items as an Embedded data field, list of records"],"answer":[1,4],"explanation":"Ordered items are user-entered data that belong to this case, so they are an Embedded data list; each item points to an existing catalog product that lives outside the case, so Product is a single Data reference. Case reference is for data from another case, and line items are not cases. Embedding the product would duplicate catalog data instead of reusing the data object. A Query field is read-only and does not store the key, so it cannot record the user's product choice."},{"id":"di-006","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A business user adds a new record, 'Platinum', to the Membership Tier data object. On the same day a developer adds a new field, 'Renewal date', to the same data object. How does Pega classify these two changes?","options":["Both are process changes because they touch the same data object","Adding the record is a data change that does not alter the process; adding the field is a process change","Adding the record is a process change; adding the field is only a data change","Both are data changes because neither modifies a flow rule"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Records are instances of a data object; adding or editing them changes data, not the application's process. Fields define the structure that views, rules and the process rely on, so changing fields is a process change. The other three options either treat both alike or reverse the classification."},{"id":"di-007","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A team needs an Employee data object with name, contact details and a postal address. A Person data object already exists with most, but not all, of the needed fields, and Pega provides standard address objects. What is the recommended approach?","options":["Add Employee as a draft data object from the workflow and rely on it becoming permanent when the first case runs","Extend Person as Person-Employee and use the standard Address-Postal object for the address","Create a brand-new Employee object from scratch so it has no inherited fields to maintain","Add employee-specific fields directly to the standard Address-Postal object"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Best practice is to use standard objects such as Address-Postal, extend an object that partially fits (Person-Employee, following Parent-Child naming), and create a new object only when nothing fits. Creating from scratch discards available reuse. Putting employee fields into an address object breaks its purpose and reuse. Draft data objects added from the workflow must be made permanent on the Data objects and integrations page; they do not become permanent on their own."},{"id":"di-008","objective":2,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"An expense report case holds an embedded list of expense lines, each with a Currency field Amount. The case needs a Total amount field that stays correct as lines are added or edited. Which App Studio calculation should you use?","options":["A Decision table whose otherwise row returns the total","A Run Data Transform automation step at the end of the stage","An Expression that adds .ExpenseLines.Amount with the + operator","A Function using 'Sum of' over the expense lines' Amount"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"Functions iterate over a list and support Sum, Average, Maximum and Minimum with Currency, Decimal or Integer results, and the calculation network recalculates the total whenever an input changes. Expressions work on individual fields with operators and do not aggregate a list. A decision table maps conditions to a result, not a list total. A data transform at the end of the stage would compute once at that point and would not keep the field current as lines change."},{"id":"di-009","objective":2,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A developer wants to create several calculated fields using App Studio Expressions. For which result field types is an Expression NOT available? (Choose two.)","options":["Text (single line)","Picklist","Currency","Date only","Email"],"answer":[1,4],"explanation":"Expressions can return any simple field type except Email, Phone and Picklist, so Email and Picklist are the correct choices. Text (single line) is allowed and supports concatenation with quoted text. Date only and Currency are simple types that Expressions can produce."},{"id":"di-010","objective":2,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"In an application built on Traditional UI, a citizen developer wants a calculated Risk tier field driven by a decision table, but App Studio does not offer the option to create a custom decision table. What should the system architect do?","options":["Grant the citizen developer a role with permission to create decision tables in App Studio","Build the decision table in Dev Studio and mark it as a relevant record so it can be selected in App Studio","Turn on draft mode for the case type so that decision tables can be created","Replace the decision table with a Function, because Functions can evaluate conditions"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Creating a custom decision table from App Studio is only supported in Constellation applications. A decision table created in Dev Studio becomes selectable in App Studio once it is marked as a relevant record. The issue is architectural, not a permission, so changing roles does not help. Functions aggregate list values and do not evaluate condition combinations. Draft mode affects whether incomplete processes can run, not which rule types App Studio can create."},{"id":"di-011","objective":3,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A Date of birth field uses a calendar control. Users can still enter tomorrow's date. What is the right way to prevent this in App Studio?","options":["Change the field type to Date & time so future values are rejected","Set the field's Required behavior to Always","Add a validation condition on the step that flags the value as invalid when it is after today","Rely on the calendar control, because it already blocks dates in the future"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"A calendar control only guarantees that the entry is a valid date; business meaning such as 'must be in the past' needs a validation condition, and App Studio validation conditions describe the invalid values. Date & time is still a type check and also normalizes to UTC; it does not enforce past dates. Required ensures a value exists, not that it is sensible. The calendar control does not block future dates, which is exactly the gap described."},{"id":"di-012","objective":3,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The same Start date field appears on a Job history step (it must be in the past) and on a New hire details step (it must be in the future). How should this be configured?","options":["Create two fields with different names because one field can only carry one validation","Use an Edit Validate rule on the property's Advanced tab with both conditions","Configure a separate validation on each step's form, because validations are tied to the step instance","Create one validation on the field in the data model so it applies wherever the field is used"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"App Studio validations are attached to process step instances, which is what lets one field have different validation on different forms. A single field-level validation would apply one rule everywhere and could not express opposite requirements. Duplicating the field is unnecessary and harms reuse. An Edit Validate on the property runs client-side whenever the value changes, with no knowledge of which step is active, and relies on custom Java, a security risk."},{"id":"di-013","objective":3,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A loan case must not enter the Underwriting stage unless the Applicant income field is greater than zero. Which statements about configuring this as stage-entry validation are true? (Choose two.)","options":["The designer must give the user a way to resolve the error, such as returning to a previous stage or closing the case.","A failure automatically routes the case to the work group manager for correction.","It is configured in the case type's Data model and prevents the case from entering the stage when invalid.","It fires on submit of each form in the previous stage, so the user can fix the value on the same screen.","It can only be configured in Dev Studio as an Edit Validate rule."],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Stage-entry validation is set in the case type Data model and blocks entry to the stage; because the case is stopped, the developer must provide a path to fix it (go back or close). Immediate correction on the same screen describes form-submit validation, the other App Studio trigger point. It is available in App Studio and corresponds to a stage-based Validate rule, not an Edit Validate rule. Nothing in the feature routes the case to a manager automatically."},{"id":"di-014","objective":3,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"In Dev Studio, a Validate rule checking Start date must run only when the Onboarding flow action is submitted, and a second check must run only when the proposed work status is Approved. Where are these conditions configured?","options":["On the Input tab of the Validate rule, using qualification by Flow Action and by Proposed work status","In the Validation functions library, by selecting a status-aware function","In a When rule referenced from the Pages & Classes tab of the Validate rule","On the Advanced tab of the Start date property, using 'Use Validate'"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Validate rules can be qualified on their Input tab by input property, proposed work status, flow action and stage, which covers both requirements. 'Use Validate' on the property Advanced tab attaches an Edit Validate that runs client-side whenever the value changes, regardless of flow action or status. Pages & Classes declares pages used by the rule; it is not where qualification happens. The validation functions library provides reusable checks (such as date within the past N weeks) but does not decide when a Validate rule applies."},{"id":"di-015","objective":4,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"When a Service request case is created, Priority should default to 'Normal' and Region should be copied from another field. Which approach follows best practice?","options":["Set default values in the Visible condition of each field","Add a Run Data Transform automation step at the start of the second stage","Set the values on the Data initialization page of the case type's Settings tab","Edit pySetFieldDefaults to set both values"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The Data initialization page on the Settings tab is the recommended place for simple defaults; it updates pyDefault, which runs at case creation, and supports exact values or values from another property. pySetFieldDefaults is system-maintained and should not be edited. A transform in the second stage runs too late to be a creation default. Visibility conditions control display, not values."},{"id":"di-016","objective":4,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which statements about pyDefault and pySetFieldDefaults are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["pyDefault runs when the case is created and calls pySetFieldDefaults.","Both rules are generated the first time a stage is added to the case lifecycle.","pyDefault runs every time a view is loaded.","pySetFieldDefaults is the recommended place to add complex default logic.","pySetFieldDefaults runs when a view loads and is maintained by the system."],"answer":[0,4],"explanation":"pyDefault runs at case creation and calls pySetFieldDefaults; pySetFieldDefaults runs when a view loads and is system-maintained. Complex defaults belong in pyDefault, not in the system-maintained rule. Both are generated the first time a view is created, not when a stage is added. Running on each view load describes pySetFieldDefaults, not pyDefault."},{"id":"di-017","objective":4,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Work- pyDefault sets status, organization info and an urgency of 10. The Complaint case type's pyDefault sets urgency to 40 and has 'Call superclass data transform' checked. What is the result when a Complaint case is created?","options":["Urgency is 10, because the Work- transform runs last and overrides the case type","Urgency is 40, but status and organization info are not set because only the most specific transform runs","Urgency is 40, and status and organization info from Work- are still applied","Urgency is 50, because values from each level are added together"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"With the superclass option, the runtime locates same-named parent transforms up the hierarchy and runs them top-down, so Work- runs first and the case type runs last; its urgency of 40 overrides 10 while the other parent values remain. Work- does not run last. Transforms assign values; they do not sum them across levels. Only the most specific transform running would describe the behavior without the superclass option."},{"id":"di-018","objective":4,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A Dev Studio data transform sets .RequestorName from OperatorID.pyFirstName and OperatorID.pyLastName, but the rule reports a problem with the page reference. What is the most likely fix?","options":["Enable 'Call superclass data transform'","Mark the data transform as a relevant record","Move the logic into pySetFieldDefaults","Add the OperatorID page to the Pages & Classes tab of the data transform"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"To reference OperatorID properties such as pyFirstName in a data transform, the OperatorID page must be declared on the Pages & Classes tab. Marking a rule as relevant only exposes it in App Studio. The superclass option chains same-named transforms through the hierarchy and does not declare pages. pySetFieldDefaults is system-maintained and should not be modified."},{"id":"di-019","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"An auto insurance application shows a list of vehicle makes and models. The list is identical for every user and changes only every month or two. Which data page scope fits best?","options":["Editable","Node","Requestor","Thread"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Node scope shares one page across all users, which suits reference data such as makes and models that is the same for everyone. Thread scope creates a page per case or transaction, which duplicates identical data. Requestor scope creates one per user session, again duplicating it. Editable is an edit mode, not a scope."},{"id":"di-020","objective":5,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which pairings of data page scope or refresh option with requirement are valid? (Choose two.)","options":["Thread scope cannot use 'Reload once per interaction', so use 'Reload if older than' instead","Thread scope for a stock price that applies to the current transaction","Requestor scope with 'Reload once per interaction' combined with 'Reload if older than 5 minutes'","Requestor scope for the logged-in customer's shipping addresses used throughout the session","Node scope with 'Reload once per interaction' for live seat availability"],"answer":[1,3],"explanation":"Thread scope fits per-transaction data like a stock price, and Requestor scope fits data tied to one user's session such as their shipping addresses. 'Reload once per interaction' is only for Thread or Requestor scope, so Node is invalid. It also cannot be combined with other refresh options, so the Requestor combination is invalid. Thread scope is one of the two scopes that does support 'Reload once per interaction', so the last statement is false."},{"id":"di-021","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A data page has the refresh strategy 'Reload if older than 10 minutes'. It is first loaded at 12:00 and next referenced at 13:00. What happens?","options":["It becomes stale at 12:10 and reloads when it is accessed at 13:00","It does not reload at 13:00 because it already loaded once in this interaction","It reloads automatically in the background at 12:10, 12:20 and so on","It reloads at 12:10 and is then current at 13:00 without another reload"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"'Reload if older than' marks the page stale once the interval passes; the reload happens on the next access, which here is 13:00. There is no background reload every ten minutes. The 'never more than once per interaction' rule caps reloads within one interaction; the 13:00 reference is a new access after the page went stale. Reloading at 12:10 would require something to reference the page then."},{"id":"di-022","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A brokerage data page returns quotes. Quotes should not be refreshed while the market is closed, and during trading hours they should be at most five minutes old. Which configuration is valid?","options":["Node scope with 'Reload once per interaction'","Thread scope with 'Do not reload when' (a When rule for market closed) and 'Reload if older than 5 minutes'","Requestor scope with 'Reload once per interaction' and 'Reload if older than 5 minutes'","Node scope with no refresh strategy, because data pages cannot save without one"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"'Do not reload when' treats the page as current while the When condition (market closed) is true, and 'Reload if older than' limits staleness during the day. 'Reload once per interaction' is unavailable for Node scope and cannot be combined with other options, which rules out both options that use it. Refresh strategies are optional though strongly recommended, so the claim that a page cannot save without one is false, and leaving it out would not meet the requirement anyway."},{"id":"di-023","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"An Order case shows product details from a catalog data page. Finance requires that the order keep the price as it was when the order was placed, even if the catalog price changes later. How should the Product field be autopopulated?","options":["Copy data from a data page, so a snapshot is stored in the case","Refer to a Node-scoped data page with 'Reload once per interaction'","Refer to a data page, so the case always shows the current catalog price","Use a Query field, so the price is read on demand from the catalog"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Copy data from a data page stores a point-in-time snapshot in the case and only re-retrieves if parameters change, which preserves the original price. Refer keeps the case pointing at current data, so later price changes would show. Node scope cannot use 'Reload once per interaction', and referring would still show current prices. A Query field is read-only lookup data that is not stored, so it would also reflect current values."},{"id":"di-024","objective":5,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which statements about data page sources and parameters are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["A Query field stores the key of the retrieved record in the case so it can be reloaded later.","With several conditional sources, conditions are checked in order, the first true one is used, and the last source is the Otherwise.","Every data page parameter is required; optional parameters are not supported.","Each distinct parameter combination produces its own page instance unless 'Limit to a single data page' is set on the Load Management tab.","Aggregate sources call every source in parallel and keep whichever responds first."],"answer":[1,3],"explanation":"Conditional sources are evaluated in order with the final one acting as Otherwise, and parameterized pages create one instance per parameter combination unless limited on the Load Management tab. Aggregate sources combine multiple sources in the listed order, not in a race. Parameters carry a required flag, so they can be optional (savable pages rely on an optional key). A Query field does not store the key."},{"id":"di-025","objective":5,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A Country dropdown is sourced from a data page. Users must see the country name, but the case must store the two-letter country code. What is true about this configuration?","options":["Only the selected value is copied to the case, and it can be a different property from the one displayed","Dropdowns can only be sourced from local data objects, so the countries must be imported as records","The stored value must match the displayed value, so a separate calculated field is required to hold the code","The entire list page is copied into the case when the user makes a selection"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"List-based controls copy only the selected item's value into the case, and that value can differ from what is shown, such as storing the code while displaying the name. The full list is not copied. No extra calculated field is needed. Data page sources can be local or external, so importing records is not required."},{"id":"di-026","objective":6,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Customer changes must be written back to an external CRM that exposes only a SOAP service. The team is designing the save plan of D_CustomerSavable. Which statements are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["Robotic desktop automation is the recommended option for unattended background saves.","The Connector save option supports REST only, so the Activity save option is needed for SOAP.","The Activity save option is not inherently transactional, so its use should be limited.","Database save can write a page list as multiple rows in the CRM.","The Connector save option supports both REST and SOAP, so no activity is needed."],"answer":[1,2],"explanation":"The Connector save option is REST only, so a SOAP target needs the Activity option, which can call any connector but is not inherently transactional and should be used sparingly. Database save writes a single page to a single row in a database; it neither handles page lists as multiple rows nor targets a SOAP service. Unattended background work maps to the Robotic automation option; RDA is desktop automation."},{"id":"di-027","objective":6,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A case property .Customer is autopopulated from D_CustomerSavable. The user edits address details in an assignment, and the change must be saved to the system of record. Which configuration choices are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["Use one Save Data Page step to save to both the CRM and the local audit table","Place the Save Data Page step before the assignment so the record is saved before edits","Autopopulate .Customer with 'Copy data from a data page'","Add a Save Data Page step after the assignment and select 'Use associated property'","Autopopulate .Customer with 'Refer to a data page' so edits are always current"],"answer":[2,3],"explanation":"When a property is autopopulated from a savable data page, use Copy rather than Refer; Copy also helps avoid stale page errors. A Save Data Page step after the assignment with 'Use associated property' takes the data page and parameters from the property rule. Refer risks overwriting the user's changes. Saving before the assignment would save the old data. Each data source needs its own Save Data Page step."},{"id":"di-028","objective":6,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"One savable data page must support both creating new Supplier records and updating existing ones from the same case type. Which design follows the guidance?","options":["Make the key parameter optional; use a Lookup source when a key is supplied and a Data Transform source when no key is supplied","Make the key parameter required and use Database delete followed by Database save for updates","Create two savable data pages because one page cannot both create and update","Refer to the savable page from the case and rely on the refresh strategy to insert new records"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"With an optional key, a supplied key loads the existing record through a Lookup source for update, while no key lets a Data Transform source initialize a new record. Two pages are unnecessary. A required key would prevent the create path, and delete-then-save is not the described pattern. Refresh strategies control reloading, not inserting, and Refer is discouraged for savable pages."},{"id":"di-029","objective":7,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A credit bureau's API is still being built, but the UI team needs a working Credit report data page and testers want predictable responses. What should the system architect do?","options":["Build an Activity save option on the savable page to mimic the API","Leave the data page without a source; data pages return empty pages until the connector exists, which is sufficient for UI work","Generate Autopilot sample records and set them as the production source","Configure a simulated data source for the data page using the expected request and response, and replace it with the real source before production"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"Simulation is designed for unavailable or unfinished external systems; it requires knowing the request and response, lets you dictate results for unit tests, unblocks the UI team, and must be replaced before production. An unsourced page gives no realistic data. Autopilot sample records are test data and are never a production source. A save option writes data back to a system of record; it does not supply simulated reads."},{"id":"di-030","objective":7,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which statements about simulating data and generating sample data are true? (Choose two.)","options":["Autopilot can generate a chosen number of sample records on a data object's Records tab with values that match the field types.","Simulation does not require knowledge of the request and response structure.","Autopilot sample records are synchronized to the external system of record once it is integrated.","Simulated sources let you dictate the response, which helps unit testing.","A simulated source may remain in production if its responses match the real system."],"answer":[0,3],"explanation":"Simulation lets you control responses, which is valuable for unit tests, and Autopilot can generate a chosen number of type-appropriate sample records from the Records tab. Simulated sources are temporary and must be replaced before production. Building a simulation requires knowing the expected request and response. Autopilot sample records are test data and are not pushed to the external system later."}]},{"id":"security","title":"Security","weight":10,"intro":"Covers who a user is (authentication), what they may do (authorization), the Security Checklist and security policies that harden an application before go-live, and the operator / access group / access role / persona chain behind role-based access control. Questions usually turn on precise distinctions: ARO 'No Access' vs Access Deny, most-permissive role wins, and production-level comparisons.","objectives":[{"objective":"Differentiate between authentication and authorization","modules":["Application security"],"summary":["Authentication establishes who the user is and is the first line of defense. In Pega the operator ID (usually an email address) plus a password is the basic form, and enterprises add single sign-on with SAML 2.0, OpenID Connect or tokens, configured through an authentication service rule. Authorization decides what an authenticated user can do: which data and actions are available at runtime, which rules can be created, read, updated or deleted at design time, and which developer tools such as Tracer are usable.","Pega also separates application-level security, which keeps outsiders out (password rules, authentication, MFA, third-party tools), from feature security, which controls what authorized users can reach (roles for personas, RBAC, ABAC, CBAC). A payroll manager who can see salary data that other HR staff cannot is a feature-security concern. On the record side, the Operator ID record supports authentication, while the access group together with the application supports authorization."],"keyFacts":["Authentication = who you are; authorization = what you can do.","Authentication is the first line of defense.","SSO options: SAML 2.0, OpenID Connect, tokens; configured via an authentication service rule.","Authorization covers runtime data/actions, design-time rule create/read/update/delete, and developer tools such as Tracer.","Application-level security protects against outsiders (passwords, authentication, MFA, third-party tools).","Feature security governs what authorized users can access (persona roles, RBAC, ABAC, CBAC).","Operator ID record -> authentication; access group + application -> authorization."],"table":{"headers":["Concern","Question answered","Examples"],"rows":[["Authentication","Who is this user?","Password login, SAML 2.0, OpenID Connect, MFA, authentication service rule"],["Authorization","What may this user do?","Access roles, ARO, Access Deny, ABAC/CBAC, permission to use Tracer or edit rules"]]},"examTraps":["MFA and password policies strengthen authentication; they do not grant or restrict features.","Permission to use developer tools or edit rules is authorization, even though it applies to developers.","The Operator ID record alone does not determine what a user can do; the access group does."]},{"objective":"Explain the benefits of the Security Checklist and security policies","modules":["Application security"],"summary":["The Security Checklist guides a team through hardening an application and is installed as an application guideline rule for each application version. Tasks are grouped by timing (at the start, during development, before deployment) and by area (monitoring, authentication, authorization, auditing, production testing), and the checklist tracks status and completion so security is built in early instead of reworked late. Deployment Manager blocks a production deployment until the checklist is complete, which also supports IT security sign-off. Its four focus areas are data encryption (passwords, properties, BLOBs; sensitive values like SSNs and card numbers), authentication, authorization/access control (RBAC, ABAC, CBAC) and auditing/accountability (failed logins, password changes, rule and data changes). The goals map to the AIC triad: availability, integrity and confidentiality. Security is a shared responsibility of Pega and the client; not every task applies, and Pega Cloud completes some automatically. The most important practices are staying guardrail-compliant and avoiding custom code, which may bypass platform security.","Security policies are configurable controls. Password policy sets length, complexity and predictability rules. CAPTCHA can be the default or custom, shown on initial login or made more likely after failed logins. Lockout, when enabled, uses a failure threshold, an initial penalty in seconds that grows, and a failure log window in minutes; when disabled, a simpler attempts-before-lock and lock-minutes setting applies. Audit policy controls detail captured for login attempts. MFA sends a one-time password by email or SMS that must be used within an allowed time. Operator disablement disables accounts inactive for a set number of days, with an exclusion list. MFA factors are knowledge (password), possession (mobile number) and being (fingerprint); two-factor authentication is a subset of MFA."],"keyFacts":["Checklist = application guideline rule per application version.","Deployment Manager blocks production deployment until the checklist is complete.","Four areas: data encryption, authentication, authorization/access control, auditing/accountability.","Tasks grouped by timing (start, during, before deployment) and area; status tracked to prevent rework.","AIC triad: Availability, Integrity, Confidentiality.","Shared responsibility; not all tasks required; Pega Cloud handles some automatically.","Top practices: guardrail compliance and avoiding custom code.","Lockout (enabled): threshold, increasing initial penalty in seconds, failure log window in minutes.","Operator disablement: inactive N days, with an exclusion list.","MFA: one-time password by email/SMS within an allowed time; factors knowledge, possession, being; 2FA is a subset."],"table":{"headers":["Policy","What it controls"],"rows":[["Password","Length, complexity, predictability"],["CAPTCHA","Default or custom; on initial login; likelihood after failed logins"],["Lockout","Enabled: threshold, growing penalty seconds, failure log minutes. Disabled: attempts before lock, lock minutes"],["Audit","Level of detail recorded for login attempts"],["MFA","One-time password via email or SMS within an allowed time"],["Operator disablement","Disable after N inactive days; exclusion list"]]},"examTraps":["The checklist is not 'all tasks mandatory'; some do not apply and Pega Cloud covers some.","Security is not solely Pega's job on Pega Cloud; it is shared with the client.","Growing penalties after failed logins are Lockout, not CAPTCHA; CAPTCHA only becomes more likely.","Automatically disabling dormant accounts is Operator disablement, not Lockout."]},{"objective":"Describe Operators, Access groups, Access roles and Personas","modules":["Managing application access","Inviting users to an application"],"summary":["A persona is the business-facing description of a type of user, managed in App Studio under Users > User management > Personas (the default persona is 'Users'). An access role describes a job function and holds the permissions to perform specific tasks; every persona has an associated role, but not every role has a persona. Creating a persona automatically creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group; creating an access group does not create a persona. Each role has a default channel interface and is associated with studio channels (App, Admin, Dev Studio) and channel interfaces such as a Work Portal.","The operator ID record identifies a person and drives authentication; the access group, together with the application it references, drives authorization. An access group bundles privileges by referencing one or more access roles, which keeps security modular, and several access groups can point at the same application. If an operator's roles conflict, the most permissive permission wins. Users are invited by email and assigned a persona or developer role; generic shared accounts such as 'admin' should not be used. Drafting persona-to-channel associations in the lifecycle does not configure access; by default a persona can reach all pages, and a portal is restricted by assigning personas when the portal is configured."],"keyFacts":["Persona = business representation; access role = job function with permissions.","Every persona has a role; not every role has a persona.","Creating a persona auto-creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group (not the reverse).","Operator ID -> authentication; access group + application -> authorization.","An access group references one or more roles; several access groups can reference one application.","Conflicting roles: the most permissive permission wins.","Default persona is 'Users'; avoid generic accounts such as admin.","Drafting personas against channels does not restrict access; restrict a portal by assigning personas on it.","Prediction Studio requires the pxPredictionStudio portal in the user's access group."],"table":{"headers":["Record","Purpose"],"rows":[["Operator ID","Identifies the user; supports authentication"],["Access group","Links user to an application and one or more roles; supports authorization"],["Access role","Job function; class-level permissions"],["Persona","Business view of a user type in App Studio; linked to a role"]]},"examTraps":["Most permissive wins, not most restrictive, when an operator has multiple roles.","Creating an access group in Dev Studio does not create a persona.","Listing a persona for a channel in the lifecycle draft does not limit portal access."]},{"objective":"Configure role-based access control (RBAC) including ARO, Access Deny, Access Manager and production levels","modules":["Managing application access"],"summary":["RBAC grants permissions through roles. Pega also supports attribute-based access control (ABAC), which compares attributes of a case, field or document with attributes of the user, and client-based access control (CBAC), which tracks and processes requests to view, update or remove a customer's personal data, as regulations such as GDPR require. Within RBAC, an Access of Role to Object (ARO) holds a role's permissions on a class; 'No Access' denies that permission for that role. An Access Deny rule is an explicit extra layer that blocks access regardless of what other roles grant, which is useful for regulatory requirements.","App Studio's User management landing page (select a persona) is the preferred place to manage access. Dev Studio's Access Manager (Configure > Org & Security > Access Manager) is needed for some permissions, such as deleting a case instance. Best practice is to use these tools instead of editing ARO or Access Deny forms directly. Permissions can be tied to production levels: 5 Production, 4 Staging, 3 QA, 2 Development, 1 Sandbox, 0 deny. Access is granted when the permission's value is greater than or equal to the system's production level. Access Manager only writes 0 or 5, so set other levels by opening the record. For example, a delete permission set to 2 gives full access on a development system (level 2) and no access in production (level 5). Access When rules can add conditions."],"keyFacts":["ARO = a role's permissions on a class; 'No Access' denies for that role.","Access Deny = explicit deny that overrides grants; extra layer for regulations.","Prefer App Studio User management; Access Manager (Dev Studio) for advanced permissions such as deleting case instances.","Best practice: do not edit ARO/Access Deny forms directly; use the landing page or Access Manager.","Production levels: 5 Production, 4 Staging, 3 QA, 2 Development, 1 Sandbox, 0 deny.","Access granted when permission level >= system production level.","Access Manager writes only 0 or 5; open the record to set intermediate levels.","Access When rules provide conditional access.","ABAC compares object attributes (case, field, document) with user attributes.","CBAC handles requests about customers' personal data (view, update, remove), e.g., GDPR."],"table":{"headers":["Permission level set","Sandbox (1)","Development (2)","QA (3)","Staging (4)","Production (5)"],"rows":[["0","No","No","No","No","No"],["2","Yes","Yes","No","No","No"],["4","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","No"],["5","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes"]]},"examTraps":["'No Access' on one role's ARO can be outweighed by another role (most permissive wins); Access Deny cannot.","The comparison is permission level >= system level; a lower number means fewer environments.","Access Manager is in Dev Studio, not App Studio; it only writes 0 or 5.","Region- or attribute-matching rules are ABAC; personal-data requests are CBAC."]}],"questions":[{"id":"sec-001","objective":0,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A bank requires that employees sign in once to the corporate identity provider and reach the Pega application without a separate password, using SAML 2.0. Which security concern does this address, and which Pega rule is configured?","options":["Authorization; an access role","Authorization; an Access Deny rule","Authentication; an authentication service rule","Auditing; the Security Checklist guideline rule"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Single sign-on through SAML 2.0 establishes the user's identity, which is authentication, and it is configured with an authentication service rule. Access roles and Access Deny rules control what an already identified user can do, which is authorization. The Security Checklist tracks hardening tasks; it does not implement SSO, and auditing records events rather than identifying users."},{"id":"sec-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which of the following are authorization concerns? (Choose two.)","options":["Requiring passwords to be at least a certain length","Whether a developer may use Tracer in the application","Requiring a one-time password sent by SMS","Whether an operator may update a decision table rule at design time","Verifying a user's identity through OpenID Connect"],"answer":[1,3],"explanation":"Authorization covers what an identified user can do, including design-time rule changes and access to developer tools such as Tracer. OpenID Connect, one-time passwords and password length rules all strengthen confirming who the user is, so they are authentication controls."},{"id":"sec-003","objective":0,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A security review lists two requirements: (1) MFA for every external login, and (2) only payroll managers can see salary fields that other HR users cannot. How are they classified?","options":["(1) application-level security; (2) feature security","Both are application-level security","(1) feature security; (2) application-level security","Both are completed automatically by the Security Checklist"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Application-level security keeps outsiders out with passwords, authentication and MFA, so (1) belongs there; feature security controls what authorized users can reach, such as the payroll manager's salary access. Calling both application-level ignores the authorized-user dimension of (2), and the reversed pairing swaps the definitions. The Security Checklist guides and tracks tasks but does not implement them automatically."},{"id":"sec-004","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"A release pipeline in Deployment Manager stops before the production stage even though all tests pass. Security staff say the application has not finished its security preparation. What is the most likely cause?","options":["An Access Deny rule blocks the deployment operator","The Security Checklist for the application version has not been completed","The lockout policy threshold was reached during testing","The operator disablement policy has disabled the pipeline's account"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Deployment Manager prevents deployment to production until the Security Checklist is complete. Access Deny rules restrict access to classes for users, not pipeline stages. Operator disablement targets inactive accounts, and the scenario points to unfinished security tasks. Lockout responds to failed logins and has nothing to do with test results or security preparation."},{"id":"sec-005","objective":1,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which statements about the Security Checklist are true? (Choose two.)","options":["It is installed as an application guideline rule for each application version.","Adding custom code is recommended to strengthen the platform's built-in security.","On Pega Cloud, security is entirely Pega's responsibility, so the client can skip the checklist.","Every task is mandatory on every environment, including tasks Pega Cloud already performs.","Its tasks are organized by timing (start, during development, before deployment) and by area."],"answer":[0,4],"explanation":"The checklist is an application guideline rule per application version, and its tasks are grouped by timing and area with tracked status. Not every task is required and Pega Cloud performs some automatically. Security is a shared responsibility between Pega and the client. Avoiding custom code is one of the most important practices, because custom code may not enforce platform security features."},{"id":"sec-006","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"After a threshold of failed logins, the user must wait a penalty measured in seconds, and the wait increases with each further failure. Failures are counted within a window measured in minutes. Which policy is this?","options":["CAPTCHA policy configured to appear after failed logins","Operator disablement policy","Audit policy for login attempts","Lockout policy with lockout enabled"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"Enabled lockout uses a failure threshold, an initial penalty in seconds that increases, and a failure log window in minutes. CAPTCHA can become more likely after failed logins but does not impose growing time penalties. Operator disablement disables accounts after a number of inactive days. Audit policy controls how much detail is recorded about login attempts; it does not block users."},{"id":"sec-007","objective":1,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Contractor accounts that have not been used for 90 days must be disabled automatically, but integration service accounts that rarely sign in must never be disabled. Which configuration meets both needs?","options":["MFA policy requiring a one-time password for contractors","Lockout policy with a low failed-attempt threshold","Password policy with high predictability restrictions","Operator disablement policy set to 90 inactive days with the service accounts on the exclusion list"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"Operator disablement disables operators inactive for a configured number of days and supports an exclusion list for accounts that must stay active. Lockout responds to failed logins, not inactivity. MFA adds an extra authentication factor but disables nothing. Password policy controls password strength, not account dormancy."},{"id":"sec-008","objective":2,"type":"single","difficulty":"medium","stem":"An administrator asks which records determine that Maria can sign in and which determine what she can do in the Claims application. Which answer is correct?","options":["Her work group handles sign-in; her work queue determines what she can do","Her access role handles sign-in; her persona determines what she can do","Her Operator ID record supports authentication; her access group, with the application it references, supports authorization","Her access group handles sign-in; her Operator ID record determines what she can do"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The Operator ID record identifies the user for authentication, and the access group, which references the application and one or more roles, drives authorization. Access roles hold permissions rather than identities. Work groups and work queues organize and route work; they are not security records. The final option reverses the two records' purposes."},{"id":"sec-009","objective":2,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"An operator's access group references two access roles. Role A grants Modify on the Claims class; role B gives no Modify permission on that class. Which statements are true? (Choose two.)","options":["Referencing several roles from one access group is a supported way to keep privileges modular.","Because two roles conflict, the application must be referenced by a second access group.","An access group can reference only one access role, so this configuration is invalid.","The operator can modify Claims, because the most permissive permission wins.","The operator cannot modify Claims, because the most restrictive permission wins."],"answer":[0,3],"explanation":"When roles conflict, Pega applies the most permissive permission, so the operator can modify Claims, and an access group can reference one or more roles to build modular privileges. Most restrictive is the wrong rule. Access groups are not limited to one role. Multiple access groups can reference the same application, but that is not required to resolve a role conflict; to override a grant you would need Access Deny."},{"id":"sec-010","objective":2,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A system architect creates a new persona named Auditor in App Studio. What is created automatically?","options":["A shared operator account named Auditor that all auditors sign in with","A new Dev Studio access role, and every existing role gets a matching persona","Default permissions, a work queue and an access group for the persona","Only the persona; access groups must be built separately in Dev Studio"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Creating a persona automatically creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group. The first option ignores this. Shared generic accounts are discouraged; users are invited individually and assigned a persona. Every persona has a role, but not every role has a persona, so personas are not generated for existing roles."},{"id":"sec-011","objective":2,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"In the case lifecycle, the team drafted that the Manager persona uses the Manager Portal for release MLP1. During testing, users with other personas can still open the Manager Portal. What explains this, and what is the fix?","options":["The other personas have an Access Deny rule that is being ignored; edit the Access Deny form directly","Drafting persona-channel associations does not configure access; by default a persona can reach all pages, so assign the Manager persona when configuring the Manager Portal","Lifecycle channel settings apply only to production; promote the application to see the restriction","Draft mode is still on for the case type; turning it off applies the channel restriction"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Persona and channel entries in the lifecycle are planning information; by default a persona has access to all pages, and a portal is restricted by assigning personas to it when it is configured. Draft mode controls whether incomplete processes can run, not portal access. An Access Deny rule would block, not allow, and editing such forms directly is against best practice. There is no rule that lifecycle channel drafts take effect only in production."},{"id":"sec-012","objective":3,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A role's Delete permission on the Order class is set to production level 2. The development system runs at level 2 and production at level 5. What happens?","options":["Delete is denied in development and allowed in production","Delete is allowed only on staging (4) and production (5)","Delete is allowed in both environments because 2 is a valid level","Delete is allowed on the development system and denied in production"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"Access is granted when the permission's level is greater than or equal to the system's production level: 2 >= 2 on development grants it, and 2 < 5 in production denies it. The other options either ignore the comparison or reverse it; staging at 4 is also above 2, so delete is denied there too."},{"id":"sec-013","objective":3,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which statements about managing access are true? (Choose three.)","options":["The User management landing page in App Studio is the preferred place to manage persona access.","Access Manager writes only the values 0 or 5; open the record to set intermediate production levels.","Access Manager is opened from the App Studio Users menu.","Some permissions, such as deleting a case instance, can only be set in Access Manager.","Editing ARO and Access Deny rule forms directly is the recommended practice."],"answer":[0,1,3],"explanation":"App Studio User management is the preferred tool; Access Manager is required for some permissions such as deleting case instances; and Access Manager writes only 0 or 5, so intermediate levels need the record itself. Best practice is to use the landing page or Access Manager rather than editing ARO or Access Deny forms directly. Access Manager is in Dev Studio under Configure > Org & Security."},{"id":"sec-014","objective":3,"type":"single","difficulty":"hard","stem":"A regulation states that interns must never view Payroll instances, even if an intern is later given another role that grants read access. What should the architect use?","options":["An Access Deny rule for the Payroll class","Set Read to No Access on the Intern role's ARO for Payroll","Configure the lockout policy for intern operators","Remove the Payroll class from the application's relevant records"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Access Deny is an explicit deny that adds a layer beyond role grants, which suits regulatory requirements. 'No Access' on the Intern ARO only affects that role; since the most permissive role wins, another role granting read would override it. Relevant records control what App Studio shows designers, not runtime data access. Lockout responds to failed logins."},{"id":"sec-015","objective":3,"type":"multi","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Match each requirement to the access control model. Which pairings are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["Handling a customer's request to view their personal data -> Access Deny","A customer asks the company to remove their personal data and the request must be tracked -> CBAC","Giving the Manager role Modify permission on the Claims class -> ABAC","Comparing a document's attributes with the user's attributes -> RBAC","Adjusters see only claims whose Region matches their own Region attribute -> ABAC"],"answer":[1,4],"explanation":"ABAC compares attributes of a case, field or document with user attributes, as in matching regions, and CBAC tracks and processes requests to view, update or remove a customer's personal data under rules such as GDPR. Granting a role permission on a class is RBAC, not ABAC. Comparing document and user attributes is ABAC, not RBAC. Access Deny blocks access; it does not handle personal-data requests."}]},{"id":"devops","title":"DevOps","weight":5,"intro":"A small but precise domain: how Pega applications move through a CI/CD pipeline with Deployment Manager, and which automated tests (unit tests, test cases, assertions, coverage) give confidence to deploy. Questions usually test pipeline stage names, assertion types and coverage roles.","objectives":[{"objective":"Explain Pega’s process of continuous development, integration, delivery, and deployment (CI/CD)","modules":["Application development management"],"summary":["DevOps combines people, process and technology so that development, QA and operations work as one flow, covering development, testing and post-go-live support. **Continuous integration** means frequent check-ins with automated builds and tests: changes become ready to share, are integrated, and are published to a development repository. **Continuous delivery** adds automated regression and staging, then moves through ready to accept (acceptance criteria), ready to deploy (performance, scale and compatibility), and deployment (validate in pre-production, deploy to production, run post-deployment tests, roll back if needed). A typical setup pairs a Pega repository with an automation server such as Jenkins.","**Deployment Manager** is Pega's own CI/CD pipeline tool: it merges branches, packages and promotes applications. It is accessed in Admin Studio and integrates with App Studio, where Settings > Versions lets you publish to higher environments. Third-party tools such as Jenkins and Azure DevOps can also be used. Deployment Manager blocks a production deployment if the Security Checklist has not been completed.","CI best practices: test early and often (run Pega unit tests and critical integration tests before packaging and stop the pipeline on failure), automate merges and builds, keep the product rule (Rule-Admin-Product) current, and publish archives to a repository for version history. CD best practices: use test environments (for example Docker) for UAT and exploratory testing, run regression through UI and service layers, target specific ruleset versions for production, roll back the latest import on failure, and annotate a package as ready to deploy before manual or automatic deployment."],"keyFacts":["CI stages: ready to share, integrate changes, publish to development repository.","CD stages: ready to accept (acceptance criteria), ready to deploy (performance, scale, compatibility), deploy.","Deploy includes pre-production validation, production deployment, post-deployment tests and rollback.","Deployment Manager = Pega's CI/CD tool for branch merge, packaging and promotion; opened in Admin Studio.","From App Studio, Settings > Versions publishes to higher environments through Deployment Manager.","Deployment Manager blocks production deployment when the Security Checklist is incomplete.","Stop the pipeline when unit or critical integration tests fail.","Keep Rule-Admin-Product up to date and publish archives to a repository.","On a failed production import, roll back the latest import.","Testing cycles: Pega unit, regression, performance, load."],"table":{"headers":["Pipeline checkpoint","Question it answers"],"rows":[["Ready to share","Is my change ready for others (CI)?"],["Integrate changes","Does it merge and build cleanly with everyone else's work?"],["Ready to accept","Does it meet acceptance criteria?"],["Ready to deploy","Does it meet performance, scale and compatibility needs?"],["Deploy","Validate in pre-production, deploy, post-deploy test, roll back if needed"]]},"examTraps":["Performance, scale and compatibility belong to ready to deploy, not ready to accept.","Deployment Manager lives in Admin Studio, not Dev Studio.","Skipping tests to speed up the pipeline contradicts CI best practice.","A blocked production deployment can come from an incomplete Security Checklist even when tests pass."]},{"objective":"Describe the tests required for successful deployment: unit tests, test cases, and assertions","modules":["DevOps in Pega Platform"],"summary":["The smallest testable unit is an individual rule, so unit tests isolate where an error comes from (for example whether the decision tree or the data page is wrong). Rule resolution applies during tests. A unit test run can be converted into a reusable **test case** with assertions: Property (for example `pxUrgencyWork` equals 10), Decision result, Expected run time (for example within 3 seconds) and Page (for example a list data page has no errors). Test cases can be grouped into test suites and should run at least on every merge and check-in.","Automate first where results are predictable, tests run often, manual effort on complex logic is high, or the rule is widely used. Low-priority candidates are rules that change frequently, are easy to test manually, are too complex, or depend on database persistence. Good tests cover positive and negative paths and are maintainable (clear names, comments, modular test data).","Test case details: the Disable checkbox excludes a test from suites and REST runs so it can only be run manually; the Setup & Cleanup tab prepares and removes data with data transforms, activities and clipboard pages; the label becomes the immutable identifier. Flows and case types are recorded with Actions > Record test case; captured inputs go into a data transform, and the `pxCapturePropertyIgnore` data transform excludes properties from capture. Since 8.5, a process containing the Create stage of a new case type cannot be recorded. Strategy tests use componentName and pzRandomSeed parameters."],"keyFacts":["Unit = individual rule; rule resolution still applies in tests.","Assertion types: Property, Decision result, Expected run time, Page.","Group test cases into test suites; run at least on every merge and check-in.","High automation priority: predictable, frequently run, complex manual effort, widely used.","Low automation priority: frequently changing, easy manually, too complex, database persistence.","Disabled test cases are excluded from suites and REST runs; manual run only.","Setup & Cleanup tab handles test data with data transforms, activities and clipboard pages.","Recorded flow inputs are stored in a data transform; exclude properties with pxCapturePropertyIgnore.","A test case label becomes its identifier, which cannot be changed."],"table":{"headers":["Assertion","Example check"],"rows":[["Property","pxUrgencyWork equals 10"],["Decision result","A decision rule returns the expected result"],["Expected run time","Rule completes within 3 seconds"],["Page","A data page has no errors"]]},"examTraps":["A rule that changes frequently is a poor automation candidate, even if it is important.","Disabled does not mean deleted; the test can still be run manually.","Excluding a volatile property from a recording uses pxCapturePropertyIgnore, not the cleanup tab."]},{"objective":"Describe the tests required for successful deployment: the test pyramid and test coverage","modules":["DevOps in Pega Platform"],"summary":["The **test pyramid** puts unit tests at the base because they are cheap, fast and should be the most numerous; end-to-end tests sit at the top because they are expensive and should be few.","The **test coverage** tool reports which rules were exercised; an uncovered rule is one that was not tested. User-level reports cover one user's session and keep a coverage history. Application-level reports combine data from multiple users, appear in the Application Quality Dashboard, and can be merged. Running test suites requires SysAdm4; only operators with SysAdm4 or User4 contribute coverage data; starting application-level coverage needs the `pzStartOrStopMasterAppRuleCoverage` privilege. Coverage and pass rate also feed branch quality metrics."],"keyFacts":["Pyramid: many unit tests at the base, few end-to-end tests at the top.","Uncovered rule = a rule not executed by any test.","User-level coverage: single user, coverage history.","Application-level coverage: multiple users, Application Quality Dashboard, reports can be merged.","SysAdm4 is required to run test suites.","Only SysAdm4 or User4 operators contribute coverage data.","Starting application-level coverage requires pzStartOrStopMasterAppRuleCoverage."],"table":{"headers":["Coverage report","Scope","Where/notes"],"rows":[["User-level","One user","Coverage history for that user"],["Application-level","Multiple users","Application Quality Dashboard; reports can be merged"]]},"examTraps":["An uncovered rule is untested, not a rule whose test failed.","Application-level coverage cannot be started by any operator; it needs a specific privilege.","An inverted pyramid (mostly end-to-end tests) is slow and costly."]}],"questions":[{"id":"do-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A release has passed acceptance testing. Before it can go to production, the operations team must confirm it performs under expected load, scales, and is compatible with the target environment. Which pipeline checkpoint does this describe?","options":["Ready to share","Integrate changes","Ready to accept","Ready to deploy"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"Ready to deploy covers performance, scale and compatibility. Ready to share and integrate changes are continuous integration checkpoints about sharing and merging work. Ready to accept is about acceptance criteria, which the release has already passed.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-002","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A system architect working in App Studio wants to promote the current application version to higher environments through the organization's Deployment Manager pipeline. Where is this initiated in App Studio?","options":["Settings > Versions, then publish","The Agile Workbench feature map","The Reuse Library","The Configurations landing page"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Deployment Manager integrates with App Studio through Settings > Versions, where you publish to higher environments. The Agile Workbench feature map tracks features and work items. The Reuse Library stores reusable assets. The Configurations landing page manages configuration sets and settings.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"do-003","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"A team is tuning its continuous integration process to catch problems earlier. Which two practices align with Pega CI best practices? (Choose two.)","options":["Run Pega unit tests and critical integration tests before packaging, and stop the pipeline when they fail.","Keep the product rule (Rule-Admin-Product) up to date.","Package the application only after it has been deployed to production.","Skip automated tests on routine check-ins to keep builds fast.","Have developers merge branches manually instead of letting Deployment Manager do it."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"CI best practice is to test early and often, stopping the pipeline on failure, and to keep Rule-Admin-Product current. Packaging happens before deployment, not after production. Skipping tests contradicts testing early and often. Automation (Deployment Manager handling merges and builds) is preferred over manual merging.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-004","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"All automated tests pass and the package is marked ready to deploy, yet Deployment Manager refuses to deploy the application to production. Which cause is most consistent with Pega's deployment controls?","options":["The application still has open Feedback items in Agile Workbench.","The Security Checklist for the application has not been completed.","A developer still has a Tracer session open on the development node.","The production target uses locked ruleset versions."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Deployment Manager blocks production deployments until the Security Checklist is complete. Open Feedback items are backlog tracking and do not gate deployment. A Tracer session on a development node has no bearing on promotion. Targeting specific locked ruleset versions for production is normal best practice, not a blocker.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-005","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A QA lead has budget to automate unit tests for only one more rule. Which candidate is the lowest priority for automation?","options":["A widely used decision table that returns predictable results","A complex calculation that is tedious to verify manually and runs on every check-in","A rule whose logic the business changes almost every sprint","A when rule reused across many case types with deterministic outcomes"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Frequently changing rules are low priority because their tests would need constant rework. Widely used rules, rules with predictable results, complex logic that is costly to test manually, and frequently run logic are all high-priority candidates for automation.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-006","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A tester converts a unit test run into a test case. It must confirm that the case urgency equals 10 and that the rule completes within 3 seconds. Which two assertion types should be added? (Choose two.)","options":["Property","Expected run time","Page","Decision result"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A Property assertion checks a value such as pxUrgencyWork equal to 10, and an Expected run time assertion checks duration. A Page assertion checks a page, such as a data page having no errors. A Decision result assertion checks what a decision rule returns, not urgency or timing.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-007","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A developer selects the Disable checkbox on a test case that is temporarily unreliable. What is the effect?","options":["The test case is deleted at the next branch merge.","The test case is excluded from test suites and REST-triggered runs but can still be run manually.","The test case still runs in suites but its result is ignored for coverage.","The test case can no longer be opened until it is re-enabled by a SysAdm4 operator."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Disabling excludes the test case from suites and REST execution; it can only be run manually. It is not deleted. It does not keep running in suites. It remains openable; disabling does not lock the record.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-008","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A QA analyst records a test case for a flow. The test fails on every rerun because a captured timestamp property differs each time. How should the analyst stop that property from being captured?","options":["Add a cleanup activity on the Setup & Cleanup tab.","Configure the pxCapturePropertyIgnore data transform to exclude the property.","Select the Disable checkbox on the test case.","Replace the assertions with an Expected run time assertion."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Recorded inputs are stored in a data transform, and pxCapturePropertyIgnore excludes properties from capture. The Setup & Cleanup tab prepares and removes test data, not capture rules. Disabling only removes the test from suites. Swapping to a run time assertion abandons the functional checks rather than fixing the capture.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"do-009","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"A quality manager wants a combined picture of rule coverage from testing done by several developers. Which two statements are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["Application-level coverage reports combine data from multiple users and are shown in the Application Quality Dashboard.","Only operators with the SysAdm4 or User4 role contribute coverage data.","User-level coverage automatically merges every developer's results into one report.","Any operator can start application-level coverage without a special privilege.","An uncovered rule is a rule whose unit test failed."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Application-level reports aggregate multiple users and appear in the Application Quality Dashboard, and only SysAdm4 or User4 operators contribute coverage data. User-level coverage covers one user. Starting application-level coverage requires pzStartOrStopMasterAppRuleCoverage. An uncovered rule is one that was not tested at all, not one that failed.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"do-010","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A team has 400 end-to-end UI tests and 20 unit tests. Builds take hours and failures are hard to localize. According to the test pyramid, what should they do?","options":["Add more end-to-end tests so every path is checked through the UI.","Shift effort toward many fast, cheap unit tests at the base and keep only a few end-to-end tests at the top.","Replace all automated tests with manual exploratory testing.","Keep the ratio but run end-to-end tests only in production."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The pyramid places numerous cheap, fast unit tests at the base and few expensive end-to-end tests at the top; unit tests also isolate errors to a rule. Adding more end-to-end tests worsens cost and speed. Abandoning automation contradicts CI/CD practice. Running tests only in production delays feedback and adds risk.","difficulty":"medium"}]},{"id":"user-experience","title":"User Experience","weight":10,"intro":"User Experience covers how Constellation turns your data model and rules into screens: the four view types, form layouts, field configuration, dynamic behavior, portals and channels, and the styling and localization that finish the job. Expect scenario questions where two answers both seem to work and only one matches Pega's prescribed default or best practice.","objectives":[{"objective":"Views and templates; Full Page View customization (summary panel, promoted action, utilities)","modules":["Application user interface design"],"summary":["In Constellation, a view is a reusable arrangement of UI elements, and a form is the kind of view that collects input. Every view is rendered through a template, and the template handles layout, responsiveness, breakpoints, whitespace, wrapping and accessibility, so you pick a template and fill its regions instead of placing controls by hand. There are four view types: the Full Page View, Partial Views, List Views and Form Views. Pega creates form views for you, one per user step, and each one takes the step's name. Automation steps do not get a form view.","Pega adds the Full Page View automatically to every case type and data object, and each one has exactly one. It uses three panes: the summary panel on the left, tabs in the center, and the utilities pane on the right. You edit it on the UX tab, but the preview there only shows placeholders, so you need to run a case to see real data. The summary panel sits at the upper left at roughly mobile width, and when collapsed it switches to responsive mode. It holds the Details view, which shows the primary fields by default, and the Pulse view for posting and replying in the context of the case.","The promoted action is a single button in the case header. You never add a button component for it, because Pega picks it: the first stage-wide action other than Edit details, or if there is none, the first case-wide action. Only user actions can be promoted, never optional processes. The utilities pane holds widgets such as Attachments, Case operator, Tags, GenAI Coach, Followers, Prediction, Stakeholders and Related Cases, and it is hidden when no widgets are configured."],"keyFacts":["Four Constellation view types: Full Page View, Partial View, List View and Form View. Only Form Views can be editable; the other three are read-only.","Each case type or data object has exactly **one** Full Page View, which Pega adds automatically. You edit it on the UX tab.","Full Page View panes: summary (left), center tabs, and utilities (right). The UX tab preview shows placeholders, so run a case to see data.","The summary panel contains the Details view (primary fields by default) and the Pulse view.","Promoted action: the first stage-wide action other than Edit details, otherwise the first case-wide action. Only user actions qualify, and no button component is needed.","Default templates: Form view uses Default form, List view uses Table, and Partial view uses Details (One/Two/Three column, Sub tabs, Narrow-wide, Wide-narrow) or Dynamic tabs for lists.","List views can use table, timeline or gallery layouts, but data objects do not support timeline.","A form view created on the UX tab under Other Views cannot be linked to a step directly. Embed it in the step's top-level form instead.","Best practice: build views from the lowest embedded level upward, and embed views rather than individual fields, which avoids similarly named views being auto-generated in the data class.","Constellation view changes save automatically in real time. There is no save, cancel or revert."],"table":{"headers":["View type","Purpose","Editable?","Notes"],"rows":[["Full Page View","Main interface for a case or data object","Read-only","One per case type/data object; 3 panes (summary, center tabs, utilities)"],["Partial View","Reusable read-only fragment; defaults are Details and Preview","Read-only","Confirmation view when enabled; Details and Dynamic tabs templates"],["List View","Shows records from a data source","Read-only","Table, timeline or gallery; default lists open cases with primary fields; no timeline for data objects"],["Form View","Collects input at a step; default Create and Edit views","Editable and read-only","Auto-created per user step (not automation steps); Default form template"]]},"examTraps":["A second Full Page View or a separate full page for each stage is never right. There is exactly one per case type or data object.","Adding a button component to the header to make a promoted action is wrong, because the platform derives it. An optional **process** can never be the promoted action.","Blank data in the UX tab preview is not a defect. The preview only shows placeholders.","Partial, List and Full Page views are read-only. If a question needs data entry, the answer is a Form View."]},{"objective":"Default forms vs hierarchical forms (and multi-step forms)","modules":["Application user interface design","Defining a Case Lifecycle"],"summary":["A form view normally uses the Default form template: one layout with one to three columns. Two columns is the recommended layout. Wide inputs such as Rich text, Text area, Radio group, Checkbox, Boolean and Attachment span both columns, and tab order runs left to right. Step instructions show on the form by default, and you can replace them with custom text or hide them.","A hierarchical form takes one form view and splits it into tabs called form groups. Use it when users can fill in the information in any order and you want less scrolling. Form groups are not independent or reusable views; they exist only inside that form. Do not use hierarchical forms inside a multi-step form, because tabs combined with Next and Previous buttons confuse users.","A multi-step form is still one assignment for one user, but it shows several views in sequence with Next and Previous, and Submit appears only on the last view. Navigation can be horizontal, vertical or standard. The default Create process is a multi-step form. The process option 'Allow users to go back to previous step' adds the Previous button."],"keyFacts":["Default form: a single layout with 1 to 3 columns. Two columns is the best practice.","Wide controls (Rich text, Text area, Radio group, Checkbox, Boolean, Attachment) span two columns, and tab order runs left to right.","Hierarchical form: one form view split into tabs (form groups) for information that can be completed in any order, which reduces scrolling.","Form groups are **not** independent or reusable views.","Do not put a hierarchical form inside a multi-step form.","Multi-step form: one assignment, one user, several views. Next and Previous move between views, and Submit appears only on the last view.","Multi-step navigation styles are horizontal, vertical and standard. The default Create process is a multi-step form.","Step instructions display by default and can be overridden with custom text or hidden.","Forms are views that collect input and carry action controls (submit, cancel, navigate). Not every view is a form."],"table":{"headers":["Layout","Structure","Use when","Watch out"],"rows":[["Default form","Single layout, 1-3 columns","Most steps; a short to medium number of fields","2 columns recommended; wide inputs span 2 columns"],["Hierarchical form","One form view split into tabs (form groups)","Fields can be completed in any order; long forms with a lot of scrolling","Form groups are not reusable views; not inside multi-step forms"],["Multi-step form","One assignment showing several views in sequence","One user enters data in a guided order (for example, Create)","Submit only on the last view; still a single assignment"]]},"examTraps":["A multi-step form does **not** create several assignments and cannot route views to different users.","Making form groups reusable in other case types is not possible. Reuse needs real views, such as embedded views.","Choosing a hierarchical form for an ordered, guided entry is a mistake, because tabs allow any order. Choose a multi-step form when the order matters."]},{"objective":"Field settings, primary fields and field groups (model-driven controls)","modules":["Application user interface design"],"summary":["When you select a field in a view, its settings cover the field type, label settings, editing options, additional helper text, form messages (warnings) and field behavior such as Required. Required can be always on or conditional. Because the UI is model-driven, the field type decides the control. Pega pairs each field with the best control automatically, such as autocomplete, checkbox, dropdown, link, rich text editor or text input.","Primary fields are the key fields of a case type or data object. Pega adds them automatically to the Create, Edit, Details and List views, so they appear in the Details view of the summary panel and in default lists. In a case type data model, Label and Description are primary fields by default, and you can also set primary fields on data objects. A field group puts related fields under a header with optional instructions. It can be collapsed, shows a vertical line at runtime, and can display an embedded list.","Picking the right field type is a UX decision. Use Currency for money, not Decimal. Use Text (single line) for numeric identifiers that never take part in arithmetic, because Integer strips leading zeros. A Picklist can show as a drop-down or radio buttons when the list is short, or as a search box when there are many records or you need several properties. In Constellation, a cascading dropdown is two Picklist fields where the second is sourced from a data page that takes a parameter. Traditional UI used a section with On Change and an action set instead."],"keyFacts":["Field settings: field type, label, editing options, helper text, form messages (warnings), and behavior such as Required (always or conditional).","Primary fields are added automatically to the Create, Edit, Details and List views.","Label and Description are primary fields by default in a case type data model. Data objects can have primary fields too.","Field group: a header, optional instructions, collapsible, a vertical line at runtime, and it can display an embedded list.","Picklist display: Drop-down or Radio buttons for short lists, Search box for many records or when several properties are needed.","Constellation cascading dropdown: two Picklist fields plus a parameterized data page.","Use Currency (currency codes and separators) for money. Use Text (single line) for IDs and zip codes, because Integer strips leading zeros.","Embedded data lists show as a Table (editable or read-only) or a Field Group (for complex records). Case reference can show as Autocomplete, Dropdown or Table.","Data reference modes are Single, List and Many to Many. List displays are Table, Simple table, Combobox and Checkbox group.","Location shows a Google Map. Email and URL include built-in validation and render as action links."],"table":{"headers":["Field type","UI display options"],"rows":[["Picklist","Drop-down (short list), Radio buttons (short list), Search box (many records or several properties)"],["Embedded data (list)","Table (editable or read-only), Field Group (complex records)"],["Case reference","Autocomplete, Dropdown, Table"],["Data reference (list)","Table, Simple table, Combobox, Checkbox group (modes: Single, List, Many to Many)"],["Query","Read-only data from a parameterized data page outside the case"]]},"examTraps":["Integer for an employee ID or zip code that keeps leading zeros is wrong. Use Text (single line).","Decimal for monetary amounts is wrong. Currency handles codes and separators.","A Constellation cascading dropdown does not use a section rule with an On Change action set. That is the Traditional UI approach.","Primary fields appear in views automatically. Adding them by hand to every Create and Details view is unnecessary."]},{"objective":"Patterns, conditional expressions and when rules for dynamic UI behavior (visible, disabled, required)","modules":["Configuring UI elements","Application user interface design"],"summary":["Constellation UI is model-driven. The data model and rules produce the UI through patterns, which are reusable templates for forms, lists, dashboards and case management screens. When the rules change, the UI adapts automatically and stays responsive. This follows the separation-of-concerns principle and the center-out approach: logic sits at the center and channels present it. Because UI generation is centralized, accessibility (WCAG 2.1 and Section 508) is built in by default. A fix to a central component reaches every application without editing each screen.","Dynamic behavior is set per field with three properties. Visible defaults to Always and can be set to Custom condition, When rule, If not blank or If not zero. Disabled defaults to Never and can be Always, Custom condition or When rule. Required defaults to Never and can be Always, Custom condition or When rule. A custom condition is an expression for one-off logic, while a When rule suits complex or reusable logic. Dynamic UI responds in real time, reduces clutter and avoids full-page refreshes.","A When rule built in Dev Studio can be used by App Studio authors only after it is marked as a relevant record. When rules are one of the rule types that can be marked relevant."],"keyFacts":["Patterns are reusable UI templates (forms, lists, dashboards, case management screens) generated from the data model and rules.","The model-driven UI adapts automatically when rules change and is responsive by default.","Accessibility (WCAG 2.1, Section 508) is built in. Fix a central component once and every app inherits the fix.","Visible options: Always (default), Custom condition, When rule, If not blank, If not zero.","Disabled options: Never (default), Always, Custom condition, When rule.","Required options: Never (default), Always, Custom condition, When rule.","Custom condition: a one-off expression. When rule: complex or reusable logic.","Benefits: real-time response, less clutter, fewer full-page refreshes.","Dev Studio When rules must be marked as relevant records before they appear in App Studio pickers."],"table":{"headers":["Property","Default","Other options"],"rows":[["Visible","Always","Custom condition, When rule, If not blank, If not zero"],["Disabled","Never","Always, Custom condition, When rule"],["Required","Never","Always, Custom condition, When rule"]]},"examTraps":["'If not blank' and 'If not zero' exist only for **Visible**, not for Disabled or Required.","Defaults differ: Visible starts as Always, while Disabled and Required start as Never.","Building a When rule for logic used on a single field is overkill. A custom condition is intended for one-off logic, and a When rule for reuse or complexity.","A Dev Studio When rule that does not appear in App Studio is usually missing its relevant-record flag. It is not a bug in the When rule."]},{"objective":"Channels vs personas; portal templates","modules":["Configuring Portal content"],"summary":["A persona is the business representation of a type of user. A channel is the interface those users work through: web portals, mobile, messaging and voice. Conversational channels include Facebook Messenger, WhatsApp, Apple Messages for Business, SMS/MMS through Twilio, embedded chat, Amazon Alexa and Email, supported by Intelligent Virtual Assistants (IVA) and Email Bots. Personas can share channels, and persona and channel access can be configured per stage. On the Channels landing page, a lock icon means the required component has not been added from Marketplace.","Drafting personas and channels in the case lifecycle, for example assigning them to a release such as MLP1, is a planning step. It does **not** configure channel access. By default a persona can reach every page. To limit a portal to certain users, assign the persona when you configure that portal, for example a Manager Portal. Creating a persona also creates a default permission set, a work queue and an access group. Creating an access group does not create a persona.","A portal is the UI of an application, and it is built from landing pages. Constellation has two portal families. Standard work portals serve professionals doing complex, recurring work. Self-service portals provide a minimal, guided experience for occasional tasks done by customers or employees. The default portal is the Web Portal. You can run one portal with visibility based on persona or several portals. Portal templates are responsive and include a mobile-only layout."],"keyFacts":["Persona = who (a business user type). Channel = how they interact (web, mobile, messaging, voice).","The default persona is 'Users'. A new persona creates default permissions, a work queue and an access group.","Personas can share channels. Persona and channel access can be set per stage.","Drafting personas and channels in the lifecycle for a release does **not** restrict access. By default a persona can reach all pages.","To restrict a portal, assign the persona while configuring that portal.","A lock icon on the Channels landing page means the component has not been added (Marketplace).","'Add channel process' on a stage lists only channels that have already been added. It is used for channel-specific processes such as IVA.","Standard work portal: professional, complex and recurring work. Self-service portal: minimal, guided and occasional tasks.","The default portal is the Web Portal. Templates are responsive and include a mobile-only layout.","Every role has a default channel interface, such as Work Portal or a custom portal."],"table":{"headers":["","Standard work portal","Self-service portal"],"rows":[["Audience","Professional, internal case workers","Customers or employees doing occasional tasks"],["Workflow style","Complex, recurring work","Minimal, guided, occasional"],["Built from","Landing pages (Home, My Work, Dashboard, Explore Data)","Landing pages (Self-service Main page)"]]},"examTraps":["Treating channels and personas as the same thing is a mistake. A persona is a user type, and a channel is an interface that several personas can share.","Assigning personas to channels in the lifecycle for a release is planning only. It is not access control.","A self-service portal is not the right choice for case workers who handle complex daily work.","Creating an access group does not create a persona. The reverse is true: a persona creates an access group."]},{"objective":"Landing page templates, themes, widgets and localization best practices","modules":["Configuring Portal content","Localizing application content"],"summary":["The ready-made landing pages are Dashboard, Home, Explore Data, My Work and the Self-service Main page. Custom landing pages use the Default page, Tabbed page, Dashboard page or Inline Dashboard page template. A default landing page can use a column layout, with or without a banner, or a dynamic layout where you drag, drop and resize. A dashboard is a landing page of widgets showing summaries and KPIs for each persona, organized as template, then regions, then widgets. New dashboards are Private by default and can be Shared (with all users or with your access group) or Public. A copy keeps the visibility of its source. Portal widgets include Pulse, To do (my worklist, team members' worklists, or a work queue) and App announcement (a message plus a link button). A developer must add the Pulse widget to the portal navigation.","The available themes are Pegasus (the default), Aries, Self-Service and Bootes. To customize one, save it under a new name. The Themes landing page flags colors that fail WCAG contrast. Themes do not support inheritance, so to specialize a theme you save a copy into a higher ruleset. Keep the design system (themes, fonts, icons, templates) in the enterprise or COE layer. Design tokens store styling centrally for consistency across platforms and can be changed per component.","For localization, Pega uses the browser locale first, and the locale on the operator profile overrides it. If a locale ruleset is missing, users get English. A language pack must match both the platform version and the UI architecture. A translation package bundles the application's text as JSON or Excel, can be pre-translated with GenAI, and is imported on the Localization landing page under App Studio Settings. Constellation cannot localize decision tables or trees, case statuses, or radio buttons and dropdowns inside a table. Picklist values are left out of the package, so you add them by editing the localization file in Dev Studio. Dynamic values need the pyMessages rule. Radio buttons and dropdowns sourced from data pages need values entered on the Additional text tab. Test by changing the locale on the operator record in Dev Studio; you may need to log in again."],"keyFacts":["Ready-made landing pages: Dashboard, Home, Explore Data, My Work, Self-service Main page.","Custom landing page templates: Default page, Tabbed page, Dashboard page, Inline Dashboard page.","Dashboard visibility: Private (default for new dashboards), Shared (all users or your access group), Public. A copy keeps its source's visibility.","Portal widgets: Pulse, To do, App announcement. A developer must configure Pulse in the portal navigation.","Themes: Pegasus (default), Aries, Self-Service, Bootes. Customize with save-as. Themes have **no inheritance**, so save a copy into a higher ruleset.","The Themes landing page flags colors that fail WCAG contrast. Design tokens centralize styling.","Locale order: browser locale first, then the operator profile overrides it. A missing locale ruleset falls back to English.","A language pack must match the platform version **and** the UI architecture.","Constellation cannot localize decision tables or trees, case statuses, or radios and dropdowns inside a table.","Character encoding cannot be switched from UTF-8 to UTF-16 after installation."],"table":{"headers":["Localization gap","Fix"],"rows":[["Picklist values missing from the translation package","Add them manually by editing the localization file in Dev Studio"],["Messages with dynamic values (for example {@P .userName})","Localize the pyMessages rule"],["Paragraph rules such as step instructions","Translate manually; edit the paragraph files (base and translated folders) in the package"],["Radio buttons or dropdowns sourced from a data page","Localization landing page, Additional text tab: enter values per row, rebuild and import"],["Decision tables or trees, case statuses, radios or dropdowns in a table","Cannot be localized in Constellation"]]},"examTraps":["Expecting a division theme to pick up enterprise theme changes through inheritance is wrong, because themes do not inherit.","A language pack that matches only the platform version is not enough. It must also match the UI architecture.","If the operator profile locale is set, it overrides the browser locale, not the other way around.","Dashboards are Private by default, not Public."]}],"questions":[{"id":"ux-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A case type has two optional actions in its Review stage. 'Request information' is a user action and comes first, and 'Escalate review' is an optional process and comes second. Case-wide, it has Edit details and Change stage. Which action appears as the promoted action button while the case is in the Review stage?","options":["Edit details","Change stage","Request information","Escalate review"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Pega promotes the first stage-wide action other than Edit details and only falls back to case-wide actions when the stage has none. 'Request information' is a user action and is first in the stage list, so it is promoted. Edit details is explicitly excluded from promotion. Change stage is only the fallback when the stage has no eligible action. 'Escalate review' is an optional process, and only user actions can be promoted.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"A case manager wants users to see uploaded files and the list of related cases on the right side of every case without opening a tab. Which two widgets can be added to the utilities pane of the Full Page View for this? (Choose two.)","options":["Attachments","App announcement","Related Cases","To do","Explore Data"],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Attachments and Related Cases are both utilities pane widgets, along with Case operator, Tags, GenAI Coach, Followers, Prediction and Stakeholders. App announcement and To do are portal widgets, not case utilities. Explore Data is a landing page for querying data, not a widget in the utilities pane.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-003","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A business architect built a form view called 'Shipping details' from the UX tab (Other Views). It needs to appear when users complete the 'Enter shipping' step, which already has its own auto-generated form view. What should the system architect do?","options":["Open the 'Shipping details' view settings and associate it directly with the 'Enter shipping' step","Embed 'Shipping details' inside the 'Enter shipping' step's top-level form view","Rename 'Shipping details' to 'Enter shipping' so the step picks it up automatically","Convert 'Shipping details' into a Partial view and add it to the summary panel"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A form created from the UX tab cannot be linked directly to a step. The supported approach is to embed it in the step's top-level form view, and embedding views is the recommended reuse pattern anyway. No setting links it directly to the step. Renaming a view does not rebind a step. A Partial view is read-only, so it cannot collect shipping input, and the summary panel is not where step data is entered.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-004","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A single 'Collect applicant details' step has about 40 fields covering personal, employment and financial information. Users complain about scrolling and say they often fill in the financial section before the employment section. The step is not part of a multi-step form. Which design best fits?","options":["Convert the step into a multi-step form with one view per section","Use a hierarchical form so each section is a form group shown as a tab","Switch the Default form template to three columns","Split the step into three assignments routed to the same user"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Hierarchical forms split one form view into tabbed form groups for information that can be completed in any order, and they cut down scrolling, which matches both complaints. A multi-step form forces a sequence with Next and Previous, which conflicts with users choosing their own order. Three columns does not address the ordering need, and two columns is the best practice anyway. Splitting the work into three assignments adds processing overhead and still imposes an order.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-005","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"The Create stage uses the default multi-step form. A developer wants to turn its second view into a hierarchical form with three tabs. What should the system architect advise?","options":["Proceed, because hierarchical forms are intended for the views of a multi-step form","Proceed, but make each form group a reusable view so the other case types can share it","Avoid it, because tabs inside a multi-step form clash with the Next and Previous navigation and confuse users","Avoid it, because hierarchical forms can be used only in Partial views"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Pega advises against hierarchical forms inside multi-step forms because the tab navigation clashes with Next and Previous. They are not designed for multi-step views, so the first option is wrong. Form groups are not independent or reusable views, so the second option is wrong. Hierarchical forms are a form view layout; Partial views are read-only and are not where hierarchical forms are used.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-006","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"Which two statements about multi-step forms are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["The views are all part of a single assignment completed by one user","Each view creates its own assignment, so views can be routed to different users","The Submit button appears only on the last view","Navigation must be horizontal; vertical navigation needs a hierarchical form","A multi-step form cannot be used in the Create stage"],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"A multi-step form is one assignment for one user with several views, and Submit appears only on the last view (Next and Previous appear before that). It does not create separate assignments or route views to different users. Horizontal, vertical and standard navigation styles are all available. The default Create process is itself a multi-step form.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-007","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"Employee badge numbers look like '004512'. They are never used in calculations but must display exactly as issued. A tester reports that after submission the value shows as '4512'. What is the most likely cause and fix?","options":["The field is an Integer, which strips leading zeros; change it to Text (single line)","The field is Decimal; change it to Integer","The field is Text (single line); change it to Percentage","The view uses a Search box display; change it to a Drop-down"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Integer fields drop leading zeros, so identifiers with no arithmetic use belong in Text (single line). Moving from Decimal to Integer would still drop the zeros. Percentage changes how the value displays (0.5 becomes 50%) and is meant for ratios. Search box and Drop-down are Picklist display options and have nothing to do with leading zeros.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-008","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"On a Constellation form, the 'Model' dropdown should list only models for the 'Make' the user picked in the dropdown above it. Which two configurations are needed? (Choose two.)","options":["Configure Make and Model as two Picklist fields","Source the Model list from a data page that takes the selected Make as a parameter","Create a section rule with an On Change event and an action set that refreshes the Model list","Set Model to Visible: If not blank","Change Make to a Radio buttons display so it triggers a refresh"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"In Constellation, a cascading dropdown is two Picklist fields where the dependent list comes from a parameterized data page. A section rule with On Change and an action set is the Traditional UI technique. 'If not blank' only controls whether Model is visible, not which values it lists. Changing the display to radio buttons does not create the parameter link.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-009","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A developer marks 'Policy number' as a primary field in the case type data model. Where does the field appear without any further view configuration?","options":["Only in the case header next to the promoted action","In the Create, Edit, Details and List views","Only in the utilities pane of the Full Page View","Only in views of data objects that reference the case"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Primary fields are added automatically to the Create, Edit, Details and List views. Because the summary panel's Details view shows primary fields, they also appear there. The case header holds the promoted action, not primary fields. The utilities pane holds widgets such as Attachments and Tags. Data object views are not where case primary fields go automatically.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-010","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"On one form, the 'Spouse name' field should appear only when 'Marital status' equals Married. No other view or rule needs this logic. Which configuration follows best practice?","options":["Visible: When rule, using a new When rule named IsMarried","Visible: Custom condition, Marital status equals Married","Visible: If not blank, applied to Marital status","Disabled: Custom condition, Marital status not equal to Married"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A custom condition is the expression option for one-off logic, which this is. A When rule is intended for complex or reusable logic, so creating one here adds an unneeded rule. 'If not blank' checks whether the field itself has a value; it cannot compare Marital status to Married. A disabled field is still shown, so the requirement to hide it is not met.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-011","objective":3,"type":"multi","stem":"A designer wants to reuse the same condition options across the Visible and Required settings of a field. Which two options can be chosen for Visible but are NOT available for Required? (Choose two.)","options":["If not blank","Custom condition","When rule","If not zero","Always"],"answer":[0,3],"explanation":"Visible offers Always, Custom condition, When rule, If not blank and If not zero. Required offers Never, Always, Custom condition and When rule. 'If not blank' and 'If not zero' are therefore Visible-only. Custom condition, When rule and Always are available for both settings.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-012","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"A senior system architect created a When rule 'IsHighValueClaim' in Dev Studio in the claim case type's class. A business architect wants to make the 'Supervisor notes' field Required: When rule in App Studio, but IsHighValueClaim is not in the list. What is the most likely reason?","options":["When rules can be used only for Visible, not for Required","The When rule has not been marked as a relevant record","App Studio cannot reference rules created in Dev Studio under any circumstances","Required must first be set to Always before a When rule can be chosen"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Rules created in Dev Studio, When rules included, appear in App Studio pickers only after they are marked as relevant records. Required supports When rule as an option, so the first option is wrong. App Studio can use Dev Studio rules once they are relevant records, which is the purpose of that feature. No prerequisite of setting Always first exists.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-013","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"In the case lifecycle's Personas and Channels section, a team drafted the Manager persona with the Manager Portal for release MLP1. In testing, users with the Manager persona can still open every portal page, and the requirement is that only the Manager Portal be available to them. What explains this, and what should the team do?","options":["Drafting in the lifecycle does not configure channel access; assign the Manager persona when configuring the Manager Portal","The release must be changed from MLP1 to the current release for the restriction to take effect","The Manager persona must be deleted and recreated as an access group","Channel access is set only per stage, so the team must add the Manager Portal to every stage"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Drafting personas and channels in the lifecycle is planning, and a persona can reach all pages by default. You restrict a portal by assigning the persona when configuring that portal. Changing the release target is still drafting and changes no access. Creating an access group does not create a persona; it works the other way round. Per-stage persona and channel settings are separate from limiting which portal a persona can open.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-014","objective":4,"type":"multi","stem":"An insurer needs a portal for policyholders who file a claim perhaps once a year. Which two characteristics support choosing a Constellation self-service portal over a standard work portal? (Choose two.)","options":["It provides a minimal, guided experience","It is aimed at occasional workflows performed by customers or employees","It is optimized for professionals handling complex, recurring workflows","It is the only portal type with a responsive, mobile-only layout","It is the default portal created for every new application"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Self-service portals are minimal and guided and are meant for occasional tasks done by customers or employees, which fits annual claim filing. Complex, recurring professional work describes a standard work portal. Responsive templates, including the mobile-only layout, are not unique to self-service portals. The default portal is the Web Portal.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ux-015","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"A Constellation application imported a Spanish translation package. Spanish-locale users now see translated labels and buttons, but case statuses and the values returned by a decision table still appear in English. What is the best explanation?","options":["The language pack does not match the UI architecture","The browser locale is overriding the operator profile locale","Constellation does not localize case statuses or decision table and tree content","These values come from a data page and must be added on the Additional text tab"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Constellation cannot localize case statuses, decision tables or trees, or radio buttons and dropdowns inside a table, so this behavior is expected. A language pack mismatch would affect platform labels too, but labels are translating here. The operator profile locale overrides the browser locale, not the reverse, and labels are already correct for these users. The Additional text tab handles data-page-sourced radio buttons and dropdowns, not case statuses or decision table results.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ux-016","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"An enterprise Center of Excellence is creating a corporate theme from Pegasus, and two divisions each need small variations. Which two statements reflect correct Pega practice? (Choose two.)","options":["Customize Pegasus by saving it under a new name instead of editing it in place","Each division theme automatically inherits later enterprise theme changes through theme inheritance","To specialize the theme for a division, save a copy of it into a higher ruleset","The Themes landing page automatically corrects colors that fail WCAG contrast","Store the design system in the implementation layer so each deployment owns its own theme"],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"You customize a theme by saving it under a new name, and because themes do not inherit, you specialize by saving a copy into a higher ruleset. For that same reason, division themes do not pick up enterprise changes automatically. The Themes landing page only flags contrast failures; it does not fix them. The design system belongs in the enterprise or COE layer, not the implementation layer.","difficulty":"hard"}]},{"id":"app-development","title":"Application Development","weight":15,"intro":"This domain tests how Pega applications are structured and built: the studios and low-code approach, layered reuse, center-out design, team roles and collaboration tools, the rule/ruleset/class model behind every application, and how to debug what you build. Expect many questions that hinge on precise vocabulary (pattern vs directed, workspace vs branch, major vs patch).","objectives":[{"objective":"Evaluate the benefits of using Pega Platform for creating digital solutions","modules":["Low-code defined"],"summary":["Pega is a low-code platform: instead of writing code, teams model applications visually and declaratively, so the platform effectively generates the software from the model. Business people and developers share one visual language, which shortens feedback loops, supports agile delivery, and lets one design serve many channels (web, mobile, chatbots, email, robots). Integrations are mapped visually and anything built once can be reused. Both citizen developers and professional developers benefit.","Work is split across studios aimed at different audiences. **App Studio** is the low-code workspace for case design, data and UX, used by business architects, citizen developers and system architects; best practice is to build there unless a feature is only available elsewhere. **Dev Studio** exposes the underlying rule forms for security, reuse, integration, class mapping and advanced SLA settings. **Prediction Studio** serves data scientists building adaptive, predictive and text models. **Admin Studio** is for IT system administrators monitoring the runtime. Every studio shares the same layout: header, navigation pane (explorers) and authoring space; the studio switcher is in the upper-left and a default studio opens at login.","Because App Studio uses business-friendly names, the same concept has two names. Rules are generated automatically behind App Studio configuration using these names."],"keyFacts":["Low-code means visual and declarative modeling instead of hand-written programming.","Best practice: build in App Studio; move to Dev Studio only when App Studio does not expose the feature.","Dev Studio is needed for advanced SLA options such as initial urgency/delay timers, programmatic interval length, business-day calendars, and escalation actions beyond notifications (reassign, resolve).","Prediction Studio requires the `pxPredictionStudio` portal in the operator's access group.","Admin Studio is for runtime monitoring by IT system administrators.","App Studio navigation: Overview, Case Types, Data, Channels, Library (Reuse Library), Users.","The Application profile tracks features, user stories, bugs and feedback for traceability.","Name mapping: Field = Property, Goal & deadline = SLA, User = Operator, Team = Work Group.","Co-development pattern: a business architect designs views in App Studio, a system architect adds functionality and tests in Dev Studio."],"table":{"headers":["Studio","Primary audience","Typical work"],"rows":[["App Studio","Business architects, citizen developers, system architects","Case types, data, UX, personas, low-code configuration"],["Dev Studio","System architects, QA","Rule forms, security, integration, class mapping, advanced SLA, reuse"],["Prediction Studio","Data scientists","Adaptive, predictive and text ML models"],["Admin Studio","IT system administrators","Runtime monitoring (also hosts Deployment Manager)"]]},"examTraps":["Dev Studio is not the default best-practice place to build just because it is more powerful; start in App Studio.","Runtime monitoring belongs to Admin Studio, not Dev Studio.","A User in App Studio is an Operator in Dev Studio, not an access group; a Team is a Work Group.","Not being able to open Prediction Studio is an access group/portal issue, not a licensing of App Studio issue."]},{"objective":"Explain how the Situational Layer Cake, prescriptive design, studio interoperability, and the Reuse Library support modular enterprise reuse","modules":["Modular architecture and enterprise reuse","Promoting rule reuse with relevant records"],"summary":["The **Situational Layer Cake** lets an organization reuse common logic while still supporting variations, avoiding both tangled if-then-else logic and many near-duplicate applications. Rules are organized in layers: Enterprise (platform-wide and company-wide assets such as authentication), Division (assets reusable within a division, for example a claims flow), Application (app-specific), and Implementation (deployment-specific customization such as regional regulation or localization). At runtime Pega picks the most specific applicable rule, so upper layers can specialize without disturbing the shared layers beneath them. Reusable features can be packaged as module applications stacked as built-on applications. A well-known example is an insurer consolidating 52 claim systems into one claims platform.","Modular enterprise reuse pays off in every phase (design, development, testing, deployment, maintenance) and follows five principles: Interoperable, Updateable, Configurable, Modular and Governed (typically through a Center of Excellence). Prescriptive design means App Studio guides builders toward proven patterns (prescriptive, model-driven UI) instead of free-form coding.","**Studio interoperability**: Dev Studio is where advanced applications, reusable components and modules, traditional UI, and automated unit testing are built, and where rules are marked as relevant records. App Studio consumes that work without code through AI-assisted development, prescriptive UI, relevant records and Configuration Sets. The **Reuse Library** (App Studio Library) is a central repository of reusable assets, backed by governance: lifecycle management (centralized or federated), a reuse catalog with tagging and search, deployment and maintenance tracking, value measurement (usage/ROI dashboards), and education and advocacy."],"keyFacts":["Layer cake layers, from shared to specific: Enterprise, Division, Application, Implementation.","Runtime selects the most specific rule available for the situation.","Regional regulation or localization variations belong in the Implementation layer.","Reusable features can be delivered as module applications used as built-on applications.","Five reuse principles: Interoperable, Updateable, Configurable, Modular, Governed.","Dev Studio: reusable components/modules, traditional UI, automated unit tests, marking relevant records.","App Studio: AI-assisted development, prescriptive UI, consuming relevant records, Configuration Sets.","Dev Studio can widen a rule's scope from a case type to the application, division or organization for reuse.","Reuse Library governance includes usage/ROI dashboards (value measurement) and a tagged, searchable reuse catalog."],"table":{"headers":["Layer","Contains","Example"],"rows":[["Enterprise","Platform and organization-wide assets","Authentication"],["Division","Assets reusable across a division","A shared claims flow"],["Application","Application-specific rules","One line-of-business app"],["Implementation","Deployment-specific customization","Regional regulation, localization"]]},"examTraps":["Handling variation with if-then-else conditions inside one rule is exactly what the layer cake avoids.","Copying an application per region is the other anti-pattern; specialize in an upper layer instead.","Configuration Sets and AI-assisted development are App Studio capabilities; building reusable modules and automated unit testing are Dev Studio work.","Know the Reuse Library governance aspects by purpose: catalog = find assets, value measurement = usage/ROI."]},{"objective":"Describe the benefits of using the Center-out architecture","modules":["Pega Center-out architecture"],"summary":["Traditional architectures fail in two ways. Top-down designs start at the channel, so logic gets duplicated in every channel. Bottom-up designs start at the data layer, producing siloed and duplicated data with no single view of the customer. **Center-out** puts the business logic layer at the center: the presentation layer responds dynamically to whichever channel is used, and the data access layer is insulated so that a change of system of record (for example moving from on-premises to cloud) requires no change to the business logic.","Five principles drive center-out: manage intelligence centrally; focus on outcomes and align the process (case management); connect up to the channels/experiences; connect down to the data while keeping logic nimble; and manage variations so the solution is scale-ready (the Situational Layer Cake). The benefits are efficiency (define once), agility (one change applies to all channels), governance (auditing and policy) and scalability (reuse logic for new channels). Model-driven UI completes the picture: the data model and rules generate the UI through reusable patterns, so the UI adapts automatically when rules change."],"keyFacts":["Top-down = channel-first, logic duplicated per channel.","Bottom-up = data-first, siloed duplicated data, no single view.","Center-out = business logic in the middle, channels above, data access below.","Changing the system of record does not require changing business logic.","Benefits: efficiency, agility, governance, scalability.","The fifth principle (manage variations to be scale-ready) is delivered by the Situational Layer Cake.","Model-driven UI uses patterns (forms, lists, dashboards, case UIs) generated from the data model and rules; it is responsive."],"table":{"headers":["Approach","Starts from","Main weakness / strength"],"rows":[["Top-down","Channel / UI","Logic duplicated in each channel"],["Bottom-up","Data layer","Siloed, duplicated data; no single view"],["Center-out","Business logic","Define once, reuse across channels; data layer insulated"]]},"examTraps":["Center-out does not mean the UI drives design; channels are connected up to central logic.","Agility in center-out means one change applies to every channel, not that each channel is changed independently.","A system-of-record move affects the data access layer only, not lifecycles or UI."]},{"objective":"Describe the accessibility features included in Pega Platform","modules":["Pega Center-out architecture"],"summary":["Because Pega generates UI centrally from patterns and templates, accessibility is implemented once and propagated everywhere. Pega supports WCAG 2.1 and Section 508. When a central component is updated, every application that uses it becomes compliant without per-screen edits. In Constellation, accessibility is built in by default, and templates handle concerns such as layout, responsiveness and accessibility.","Supporting tools reinforce this: the Themes landing page flags WCAG color-contrast failures, and UX designers on the project team focus on accessibility and consistency."],"keyFacts":["Supported standards: WCAG 2.1 and Section 508.","Implement once in central UI components/patterns; changes propagate to all apps.","Accessibility is built in by default in Constellation.","Templates handle accessibility along with layout, breakpoints and responsiveness.","The Themes landing page highlights WCAG color-contrast failures.","UX designers are the project role responsible for accessibility and consistency."],"examTraps":["Fixing accessibility screen by screen is the wrong answer; update the central component.","Accessibility is not an optional add-on to enable per view in Constellation; it is default."]},{"objective":"Define the responsibilities of key roles involved in Pega project delivery","modules":["Team application development"],"summary":["A Pega delivery team mixes client and implementation roles. The **Product Owner** (client business) owns scope and prioritizes requirements for sprints. The **Project Manager** leads the delivery team, handling governance, stakeholder liaison, schedule, budget and scope. The **Scrum Master** runs the ceremonies (sprint planning, sizing, backlog refinement, retrospectives) and removes impediments. **System Architects** (SA, SSA, LSA) configure the application; the LSA imports Blueprint files and configures functional and technical requirements and designs the class hierarchy with project leaders.","**Quality Analysts** build test scripts and scenario tests from user stories. **SMEs** supply operational knowledge and walkthroughs. The **Business Architect** translates needs into functional requirements, workflows, data models and UX. **UX Designers** cover user needs, accessibility and consistency. The **Solutions Consultant** works early in the sales cycle, co-designs a Blueprint with the client and hands it to delivery. Most roles work in App Studio; SAs and QAs use Dev Studio, data scientists use Prediction Studio and IT uses Admin Studio."],"keyFacts":["Product Owner: client-side, defines scope and prioritizes requirements per sprint.","Project Manager: governance, liaison, schedule, budget, scope.","Scrum Master: ceremonies and removing impediments.","LSA: imports Blueprint files, configures functional and technical requirements, designs class hierarchy.","Quality Analyst: test scripts and scenario tests from user stories.","Business Architect: translates business needs into functional requirements, workflows, data models, UX.","Solutions Consultant: pre-sales, co-designs the Blueprint, then hands off.","SMEs provide client operational knowledge."],"table":{"headers":["Role","Key responsibility","Typical studio"],"rows":[["Product Owner","Scope and requirement priority","App Studio"],["Project Manager","Governance, schedule, budget","App Studio (documentation)"],["Scrum Master","Ceremonies, remove impediments","App Studio (user stories)"],["System Architect / LSA","Configure app; LSA imports Blueprint","App Studio and Dev Studio"],["Quality Analyst","Test scripts from user stories","Dev Studio"],["Business Architect","Functional requirements, workflows","App Studio"],["Solutions Consultant","Pre-sales Blueprint co-design","Blueprint"]]},"examTraps":["Prioritizing requirements is the Product Owner, not the Scrum Master or Project Manager.","The Solutions Consultant co-designs the Blueprint but the LSA imports it.","Business Architect (requirements) is not the same as System Architect (configuration)."]},{"objective":"Explain the roles of Workspaces and Branches in development","modules":["Team application development"],"summary":["A **workspace** is a private development space tied to one operator (with developer access) and one application. Each operator gets one default workspace per application and can create more. Changes stay private until they are shared (pushed) into a branch. Workspaces are available in App Studio and Dev Studio, are enabled by the EnableWorkspace setting, and let you pick the application layer for new rules. The flow is Workspace, then Branch, then merge into the Application.","A **branch** is a container for rulesets that are changing rapidly; branch rulesets are based on another ruleset and hold rules in active development. Best practice is one branch per feature, merged only when the feature is complete, conflicts are resolved and approval is given. Before merge, the system checks for updated or missing rules. Branch quality metrics give a merge-readiness picture: guardrail score (weighted), total merge conflicts, test coverage (percentage of rules covered and test pass rate), and uncovered rules.","Collaboration is supported by Pulse (posts to users, teams and case stakeholders; developers open it through the developer collaboration toggle in the upper-right of App Studio; users through a Pulse widget the developer adds to the portal or case) with email notifications for mentions, likes and comments, and by hashtags that a Tags widget can display."],"keyFacts":["Workspace = one operator + one application; private until pushed to a branch.","Default one workspace per operator per application; more can be created.","Workspaces are switched on with the EnableWorkspace setting.","Promotion path: Workspace to Branch to Application merge.","One branch per feature; merge after completion, conflict resolution and approval.","Branch quality metrics: guardrail score, merge conflicts, rules covered % and test pass rate, uncovered rules.","Branch rulesets are based on an existing ruleset.","Replying to a Pulse email notification posts the reply as a comment."],"table":{"headers":["Branch quality metric","What it shows"],"rows":[["Guardrails","Weighted guardrail compliance score"],["Merge conflicts","Total conflicts to resolve before merge"],["Test coverage & unit tests","Percentage of rules covered and test pass rate"],["Uncovered rules","Rules not exercised by any test"]]},"examTraps":["Workspace changes are not visible to teammates until shared to a branch.","A workspace is not shared by a team; it belongs to one operator and one application.","Long-lived per-developer or per-release branches violate the one-branch-per-feature practice."]},{"objective":"Manage application development with Agile Workbench: user stories, feedback, bugs","modules":["Application development management","Collaboration with users"],"summary":["Agile Workbench lives in App Studio (Application profile and the Developer Assistant pane, whose tabs are Guidance, Knowledge, Work and Checklists). It organizes the application into a feature map of features and subfeatures; creating a case type automatically creates a feature with the same name.","Work items are Stories (user stories, which can be bulk-uploaded from Excel), Bugs and Feedback. Items move between To do, Doing and Done by dragging. During playback sessions with stakeholders, the team records bugs and feedback directly. Agile Workbench integrates with Agile Studio, Jira and CA Agile Central, and is recommended after a Blueprint import to generate the backlog."],"keyFacts":["Developer Assistant tabs: Guidance, Knowledge, Work, Checklists.","Creating a case type auto-creates a feature of the same name.","Work item types: Stories, Bugs, Feedback.","Statuses: To do, Doing, Done (drag to change).","User stories can be bulk-uploaded from Excel.","Integrates with Agile Studio, Jira and CA Agile Central.","Use playback sessions to capture bugs and feedback from stakeholders."],"examTraps":["Agile Workbench is an App Studio tool, not Admin Studio.","A defect found in playback is a Bug; a suggestion is Feedback; new scope is a Story.","It integrates with more than just Agile Studio (also Jira, CA Agile Central)."]},{"objective":"Create and maintain rules and rulesets","modules":["Creating a Rule"],"summary":["A rule is an instance of a rule type (a Flow rule models a process). App Studio creates rules automatically: a case type produces a work class, a pyDefault case type rule, a data page and properties; a process becomes a Flow rule; a view becomes a Section rule; a data object produces list, lookup and savable data pages. Modular rules provide versioning (history and revert), delegation (business users change values such as an approval threshold) and reuse; parameterization increases reuse further.","A **ruleset** groups rules (like an album groups songs) and can be shared. Its version is Name:MM-mm-pp (major, minor, patch, each 01–99, starting at 01-01-01). Major versions add substantial functionality (and are created for major platform upgrades), minor versions add enhancements, patch versions fix bugs; older versions should be locked. The ruleset stack is the order Pega searches; each listed version also includes lower minor and patch versions within the same major version, and every application version has its own stack. Names should be meaningful and start with the company plus purpose, must not use a Pega prefix, contain no spaces or special characters, and are at most 32 characters. Develop only in the topmost layer and keep built-on applications locked.","In Dev Studio, rules are created via the Create menu, the App Explorer, a rule reference, Save As or the Records explorer. Save As pre-fills everything but you must change the identifier, the class or the ruleset, otherwise saving fails. Every rule's `.pzInsKey` is built from the rule type's internal name, applies-to class, identifier and creation timestamp in GMT; rule resolution then chooses the right rule at runtime."],"keyFacts":["Ruleset version format: MM-mm-pp, each segment 01–99, first version 01-01-01.","Patch = bug fix, minor = enhancement, major = substantial new functionality or major upgrade.","Lock older ruleset versions; develop in the topmost layer only.","Ruleset names: company + purpose, no Pega prefix, no spaces or special characters, max 32 characters.","A new ruleset's top-level class inherits from @baseclass.","`.pzInsKey` = rule type + applies-to class + identifier + GMT create timestamp (some types add keys, e.g., Correspondence type).","Save As errors unless identifier, class or ruleset is changed.","The Label entered on a new record generates the identifier.","App Studio view = Section rule; process = Flow rule; case type = work class + pyDefault + data page.","Delegation lets business users change rule values (e.g., an auto-approve threshold) in production."],"table":{"headers":["Version segment","Increment when","Example from 02-03-04"],"rows":[["Patch (pp)","Bug fixes","02-03-05"],["Minor (mm)","Enhancements / interim releases","02-04-01"],["Major (MM)","Substantial new functionality, major upgrade","03-01-01"]]},"examTraps":["Abbreviated names such as UPTC are discouraged; use descriptive names like UPlusTelcoContracts.","Listing a version in the stack includes lower minor/patch versions of the same major only, never a different major version.","The .pzInsKey does not include the ruleset version or the creating operator.","Unlocking a locked version to fix a bug is wrong; create the next patch version."]},{"objective":"Classes, inheritance, and relevant records","modules":["Creating a Rule","Promoting rule reuse with relevant records"],"summary":["Classes group rules by purpose: Work classes for case processing, Integration (Int) classes for connectors, and Data classes for data types. A child class inherits its parent's rules; case type classes sit under Work, and a child case type gets a class under its parent's. The hierarchy runs from most to least specific: case type classes, Work, Application, Division/Unit (optional, only when necessary), Organization, other (framework) applications, and Pega base classes. A hyphen separates levels, so TGB-IT-Work is a child of TGB-IT, which is a child of TGB.","**Pattern inheritance** is automatic and based on the class-name prefix; it reuses rules by business suitability. **Directed inheritance** is set explicitly as the parent on the class rule form and is used to reuse Pega standard classes (Work-, Data-) or other applications by functional suitability. Rule resolution searches pattern parents first, then the directed parent (and its pattern parents), repeating up to @baseclass; if nothing is found, an error results. Place reusable rules as high as sensible and parameterize instead of duplicating.","**Relevant records** make Dev Studio rules available to App Studio builders. Fields, views, processes and user actions created in App Studio are marked automatically; others are marked in Dev Studio via Actions > Mark as relevant record, after confirming the rule is guardrail-compliant. Markable types include properties, flows, flow actions, SLAs, data transforms, decision tables and trees, when rules, validate rules, correspondence, paragraphs, notifications, Pulse feed rules, sections, harnesses, views, strategies and proposition filters. The inventory is at Configure > Application > Inventory > Relevant Records, with Show inactive and Show inherited options; relevant records inherit down the class hierarchy."],"keyFacts":["Three class groups: Work (cases), Integration/Int (connectors), Data (data types).","The Division/Unit class layer is optional and used only when necessary.","Pattern = name prefix, automatic; Directed = explicit parent on the class form.","Search order: pattern parents first, then directed parent, repeat to @baseclass.","Example: .pxCreateOpName in Work-Cover- is reached through directed inheritance.","App Studio-created fields, views, processes and user actions are relevant records automatically.","Manual marking: Actions > Mark as relevant record; verify guardrail compliance first.","An existing Dev Studio SLA appears in App Studio's dropdown only if marked relevant.","Relevant records inherit downward (Insurance-Claims to Insurance-Claims-Auto and -Home).","A relevant proposition filter needs its properties, strategies and when rules relevant for the primary context class."],"table":{"headers":["","Pattern inheritance","Directed inheritance"],"rows":[["How set","Automatically from the class name prefix","Explicitly on the class rule form"],["Reuse basis","Business suitability (org/division/app)","Functional suitability (Pega Work-, Data-, other apps)"],["Search order","Searched first","Searched after pattern parents"],["End point","Both continue up to @baseclass","Both continue up to @baseclass"]]},"examTraps":["Directed is not searched first; pattern parents come first.","App Studio-created fields do not need manual marking as relevant.","Marking as relevant should follow a guardrail check, not precede it blindly.","Division layer is optional, Organization is not."]},{"objective":"Debug application errors","modules":["Debugging application errors"],"summary":["**Tracer** captures runtime events for troubleshooting; it is not for performance analysis because it has heavy impact. Open it from the App Studio runtime toolbar (lower-right toggle) or the Dev Studio developer toolbar. Event colors: gray for activities, orange for flows, decisions and declaratives, light blue for database and cache. Each event shows Good or Fail, and the real root cause may sit before the final failed step. In Constellation, events are grouped by request ID because the DX API is stateless. One Tracer can run per operator per node.","The toolbar offers Pause, Clear and Save, plus Settings (event types, rulesets, break conditions, number of lines, filters such as removing View rules), Breakpoints (pause the thread on an activity; resume with Play or automatically after one hour), Watch (track property value changes, shown in the Property Inspector with old and current values and a yellow highlight) and Remote Tracer. Breakpoints and Watch are not supported for Service rules or Constellation applications.","For Constellation front ends, other tools help: view rule metadata (JSON when the pxEnableC11nDev when rule is true), browser F12 tools (Console, Network for DX API payloads, Application storage), React DevTools, Redux DevTools (actions, state, time travel), XRay toggled with `PCore.getDebugger().toggleXRay(true)`, the DebugPegaAPI setting for verbose DX API logs, `PCore.getDataPageUtils()` for client-side data pages, and UI Inspector. PCore/PConnect are not available inside the App Studio shell, so launch the portal from Dev Studio."],"keyFacts":["Tracer is for troubleshooting, not performance analysis.","Colors: gray = activity, orange = flow/decision/declarative, light blue = database and cache.","Root cause can occur before the last failed event.","Constellation events are grouped by request ID.","One Tracer per operator per node.","Breakpoints pause the thread; resume on Play or after one hour.","Watch shows old vs current values in the Property Inspector with yellow highlight.","Breakpoints and Watch are unsupported for Service rules and Constellation apps.","XRay: `PCore.getDebugger().toggleXRay(true/false)`; DebugPegaAPI = true gives verbose DX API logs.","PCore/PConnect are unavailable in the App Studio shell; launch the portal from Dev Studio."],"table":{"headers":["Tracer color","Event types"],"rows":[["Gray","Activities"],["Orange","Flows, decisions, declaratives"],["Light blue","Database and cache"]]},"examTraps":["Using Tracer to measure performance is wrong; it adds heavy overhead.","Breakpoints do not wait forever; they resume after one hour.","Do not expect Breakpoints/Watch to work for a Constellation app.","The final red Fail line is not necessarily the root cause."]},{"objective":"Configuration Sets (supporting topic)","modules":["Creating and setting application variables"],"summary":["Configuration sets and settings provide a unified framework for parameters that control application behavior, reducing the need for rule overrides and replacing Dynamic System Settings. Typical uses: toggling features (disable outbound email until the integration is finished), steering process paths (an approval monetary limit) and UI behavior (show instructions for a new process). They are managed in App Studio on the Settings tab, Configurations landing page, and referenced via the Condition Builder.","Settings are instances of Data-Configuration-Setting; sets live in the Pega-Configuration class. A set does not appear until it holds at least one setting, and its name (pyLabel) can be up to 64 characters. Access: SysAdm4 can read, write and delete both sets and settings; WorkMgr4 can read/write/delete sets but only read settings; User4 can only read both. They travel in the product file when Include associated data is selected."],"keyFacts":["Configuration settings replace Dynamic System Settings for app behavior parameters.","Location: App Studio Settings tab > Configurations landing page.","Settings = Data-Configuration-Setting; sets = Pega-Configuration class.","A set is hidden until it contains at least one setting.","Set name (pyLabel) max 64 characters.","Roles: SysAdm4 full on both; WorkMgr4 full on sets, read settings; User4 read only.","Packaged when Include associated data is checked on the product file.","Referenced in logic through the Condition Builder."],"examTraps":["A newly created empty set not showing is expected behavior, not a bug.","Do not reach for Dynamic System Settings; configuration settings replace them.","WorkMgr4 cannot edit settings, only sets."]}],"questions":[{"id":"ad-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A citizen developer and a system architect are building a claims case type. Most requirements are standard, but the business also needs an SLA that measures time using a business-days calendar and reassigns the case when the deadline passes. Which approach follows Pega best practice?","options":["Build the entire case type in Dev Studio, since one requirement needs Dev Studio and mixing studios causes conflicts.","Build the case type in App Studio and switch to Dev Studio only to configure the advanced SLA options App Studio does not expose.","Configure the SLA in Admin Studio, which manages runtime timing and escalations for all applications.","Build the case type in App Studio and model the business-day calendar with a decision table referenced from a calculated field."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Best practice is to build in App Studio and use Dev Studio only for capabilities App Studio lacks; business-day calendars and escalation actions such as reassignment are advanced SLA options in Dev Studio. Building everything in Dev Studio discards the low-code benefits and the studios are designed to interoperate. Admin Studio is for runtime monitoring by IT, not SLA design. A decision table in a calculated field does not create SLA timing or escalation behavior.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"A business architect reads App Studio documentation while a developer works in Dev Studio. Which two term mappings between App Studio and Dev Studio are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["A Team in App Studio is a Work Group in Dev Studio.","A Field in App Studio is a Property in Dev Studio.","A User in App Studio is an Access Group in Dev Studio.","A Goal & deadline in App Studio is a When rule in Dev Studio.","A Process in App Studio is a Section rule in Dev Studio."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Team maps to Work Group and Field maps to Property. A User is an Operator (the access group is a separate record that grants authorization). Goal & deadline maps to an SLA rule, not a When rule. A Process becomes a Flow rule; Section rules come from Views.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-003","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"An insurer runs one claims application in three countries. Authentication is shared across the company, the claims intake flow is shared across the claims division, and each country must apply its own regulatory rules. Using the Situational Layer Cake, where should the country-specific regulatory rules be placed?","options":["In the Enterprise layer, so every application in the company can evaluate the regulations.","In the Division layer, alongside the shared claims intake flow.","In the Implementation layer, as deployment-specific specializations for each country.","In the Application layer, using conditional logic in each rule that checks the country."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The Implementation layer holds deployment-specific customization such as regional regulation or localization; runtime chooses the most specific rule. The Enterprise layer is for organization-wide assets like authentication, not one country's rules. The Division layer holds assets reusable across the division, which regional rules are not. Putting if-then-else country checks inside shared rules is exactly the tangled logic the layer cake is designed to avoid.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-004","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A Center of Excellence is deciding which work its professional developers do in Dev Studio versus what citizen developers do in App Studio. In Pega's studio interoperability model, which two activities belong in Dev Studio? (Choose two.)","options":["Building reusable components and module applications","Creating automated unit tests","Configuring application behavior through Configuration Sets","Using AI-assisted development to generate case lifecycles","Assembling forms with prescriptive UI"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Dev Studio is where reusable components and modules, traditional UI and automated unit testing are built, and where rules are marked relevant. Configuration Sets, AI-assisted development and prescriptive UI are the App Studio side of interoperability, letting citizen developers consume Dev Studio work without code.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-005","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"Leadership asks the reuse team to prove that the components published in the Reuse Library are worth the investment by showing how often each one is used and the return it produces. Which Reuse Library governance aspect addresses this?","options":["Reuse catalog","Value measurement","Lifecycle management","Education and advocacy"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Value measurement covers usage and ROI dashboards. The reuse catalog is about tagging and searching so builders can find assets. Lifecycle management decides whether assets are governed centrally or federated. Education and advocacy promotes reuse but does not measure it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-006","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A bank built its loan application using a center-out architecture. The customer system of record is moving from an on-premises database to a cloud service. What is the expected impact?","options":["The business logic remains unchanged because the data access layer insulates it; only the data connection needs updating.","Each channel's UI must be rebuilt because the presentation layer reads directly from the system of record.","The case lifecycle must be redesigned, since stages depend on where data is stored.","The business rules must be duplicated into the cloud service so both systems stay consistent."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"In center-out, the data access layer is insulated from the central business logic, so a system-of-record change does not change the logic. The presentation layer connects to central logic, not directly to the SOR, so channels are not rebuilt. Lifecycles are business logic and do not depend on data location. Duplicating logic into data systems is the bottom-up anti-pattern.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-007","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A retailer has separate web, mobile and chat applications, each with its own copy of the discount rules. A discount change took three releases to roll out. Which statement best describes the problem and the center-out remedy?","options":["It is a bottom-up problem; center-out fixes it by creating one data warehouse per channel.","It is a top-down problem; center-out fixes it by defining the discount logic once in the central layer so one change applies to all channels.","It is a top-down problem; center-out fixes it by moving discount logic into the presentation layer of each channel.","It is a bottom-up problem; center-out fixes it by having each channel query the system of record directly."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Channel-first (top-down) design duplicates logic in every channel. Center-out puts logic at the center, giving agility: one change applies everywhere. A data warehouse per channel would worsen silos (the bottom-up weakness). Moving logic into each presentation layer keeps duplication. Channels querying the SOR directly bypasses the insulated data layer and central logic.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-008","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"An organization runs 30 Constellation applications that must comply with WCAG 2.1 and Section 508. A new accessibility requirement affects how a common input pattern behaves. What approach minimizes effort?","options":["Audit every view in each application and edit the affected fields individually.","Create a custom stylesheet per portal that overrides the input pattern.","Update the central UI component or pattern once so the change propagates to every application using it.","Enable the accessibility add-on on each view that contains the input pattern."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Pega generates UI from central patterns, so accessibility is implemented once and propagates everywhere. Per-view edits defeat that model and multiply effort. Per-portal custom styling duplicates work and diverges from the design system. Accessibility in Constellation is built in by default, not an add-on enabled per view.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-009","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"During a pre-sales engagement, a team co-designed a Blueprint with the client. The project is now won and a delivery team is assembled. Who is responsible for importing the Blueprint file and configuring the functional and technical requirements?","options":["The Solutions Consultant who co-designed the Blueprint","The Lead System Architect","The Business Architect","The Product Owner"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The LSA imports Blueprint files and configures functional and technical requirements. The Solutions Consultant works in the sales phase and hands the Blueprint to delivery. The Business Architect translates needs into functional requirements but does not import the build. The Product Owner owns scope and priority on the client side.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-010","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"Midway through a sprint, developers are blocked waiting for test environment access, and backlog refinement keeps getting skipped. Which project role should address both issues?","options":["Product Owner","Project Manager","Scrum Master","Quality Analyst"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The Scrum Master runs Scrum ceremonies such as backlog refinement and removes impediments. The Product Owner prioritizes requirements but does not run ceremonies. The Project Manager handles governance, schedule, budget and scope at the delivery level, not day-to-day Scrum ceremonies. The Quality Analyst writes tests from user stories.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-011","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"Two developers share an application. Developer A configured a new view in her workspace yesterday. Developer B, working in his own workspace, cannot see it. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["Developer B must be added as a member of Developer A's workspace.","Workspaces are available only in Dev Studio, so the view was not saved.","Developer A has not yet shared (pushed) her workspace changes to a branch.","Developer A's changes must first be merged directly from the workspace into the locked base ruleset."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Workspace changes remain private until they are shared to a branch. A workspace is tied to a single operator, so there is no workspace membership to add. Workspaces exist in both App Studio and Dev Studio. The flow is Workspace to Branch to Application; changes are not merged straight from a workspace into locked rulesets.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-012","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"A team lead reviews a feature branch before approving its merge. Which two items are reported as branch quality metrics? (Choose two.)","options":["A weighted guardrail compliance score","The percentage of rules covered by tests and the test pass rate","The number of user stories completed in the sprint","The count of Tracer failures captured during development","Average page load time for views in the branch"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Branch quality metrics include the guardrail score (weighted), merge conflicts, test coverage (rules covered and pass rate) and uncovered rules. User story counts are tracked in Agile Workbench, not branch quality. Tracer captures runtime events and is not a branch metric. Page load performance is not part of branch quality metrics.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-013","objective":6,"type":"single","stem":"During a playback, a stakeholder notices that the Submit button on the address form saves an incorrect postal code. How should the team record this in Agile Workbench?","options":["Create a Bug work item","Create a Feedback work item","Create a new Story under a new feature","Post the issue to Pulse and tag it #defect"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A defect in existing behavior is recorded as a Bug. Feedback is for comments or suggestions rather than defects. A Story represents new requirements, not broken functionality. Pulse is a collaboration feed; tags there do not create tracked work items.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-014","objective":6,"type":"multi","stem":"A new Scrum team is adopting Agile Workbench. Which two statements about it are accurate? (Choose two.)","options":["Creating a case type automatically creates a feature with the same name.","User stories can be bulk-uploaded from an Excel file.","It integrates only with Agile Studio.","Work items move through New, In Progress and Closed statuses.","It is opened from Admin Studio."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A case type auto-creates a matching feature, and stories can be bulk-uploaded from Excel. Integration also covers Jira and CA Agile Central, not only Agile Studio. Statuses are To do, Doing and Done. Agile Workbench is part of App Studio (Application profile and Developer Assistant pane), not Admin Studio.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-015","objective":7,"type":"single","stem":"The latest version of ruleset ClaimsMain is 02-03-04 and it is locked. A defect in a calculation must be fixed with no new functionality. Which version should the fix go into?","options":["Unlock 02-03-04 and edit the rule in place","02-03-05","02-04-01","03-01-01"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Bug fixes increment the patch segment, giving 02-03-05. Older versions should stay locked, so unlocking 02-03-04 is poor practice. 02-04-01 is a minor version for enhancements. 03-01-01 is a major version for substantial new functionality or major upgrades.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-016","objective":7,"type":"single","stem":"A developer opens an existing decision table, clicks Save As, leaves every pre-filled value unchanged, and clicks Create and open. The system reports an error. What must the developer do?","options":["Nothing; wait a second and retry so a new creation timestamp produces a unique key.","Change at least one of the identifier, the applies-to class or the ruleset.","Use the Create menu instead, because Save As cannot copy decision tables.","Change the rule type, because a copy cannot share the original's type."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Save As pre-fills all values, and you must change the identifier, class or ruleset or an error occurs. Although the timestamp is part of .pzInsKey, retrying does not satisfy this requirement. Save As works for decision tables. Changing rule type is not how Save As works; a copy keeps the type.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-017","objective":7,"type":"multi","stem":"A developer inspects the .pzInsKey of an SLA rule while troubleshooting. Which two values are part of that key? (Choose two.)","options":["The rule's applies-to class","The rule's creation timestamp in GMT","The ruleset version that contains the rule","The operator ID of the rule's author","The name of the application"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The .pzInsKey combines the rule type's internal name, the applies-to class, the identifier and the creation timestamp in GMT. The ruleset version, the author's operator ID and the application name are not components of the key.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-018","objective":7,"type":"multi","stem":"UPlus Telco is creating organization-level rulesets. Which two proposed names follow Pega naming best practices? (Choose two.)","options":["UPlusTelcoContracts","UPlusTelcoBilling","UPTC","Pega-UPlusContracts","UPlus Telco Claims"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Organization rulesets should be meaningful, beginning with the company name plus purpose, with no spaces, no special characters and at most 32 characters. UPTC is an opaque abbreviation. Names must not start with Pega or Pega-, and the hyphen is a disallowed special character. Spaces are not allowed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-019","objective":8,"type":"single","stem":"Class TGB-HR-Work-Relocation uses pattern inheritance and has Work-Cover- as its directed parent. A flow references property .pxCreateOpName, which is defined only in Work-Cover-. How does rule resolution find it?","options":["It searches Work-Cover- first because directed inheritance always takes precedence.","It searches the pattern parents TGB-HR-Work, TGB-HR and TGB first, then the directed parent Work-Cover-, where it finds the property.","It returns an error, because pattern inheritance cannot reach a class that does not share the TGB prefix.","It skips directly to @baseclass and then works downward to Work-Cover-."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Pega checks pattern parents first, then the directed parent (and its pattern parents), repeating to @baseclass, so it finds the property in Work-Cover-. Directed is not searched first. Directed inheritance exists precisely to reach classes outside the prefix, so no error occurs. Search moves upward toward @baseclass, not downward from it.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-020","objective":8,"type":"multi","stem":"A senior developer created an SLA rule in Dev Studio. A citizen developer configuring a step in App Studio cannot select it from the existing SLA dropdown. Which two statements are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["Mark the SLA as a relevant record using Actions > Mark as relevant record in Dev Studio.","Confirm the SLA is guardrail-compliant before marking it.","Fields created in App Studio must also be marked manually before they appear.","Move the SLA to @baseclass so App Studio can find it.","Relevant records apply only to the exact class and never to its subclasses."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"An existing Dev Studio SLA shows in App Studio only when marked relevant, which is done via Actions > Mark as relevant record after verifying guardrail compliance. App Studio-created fields are marked automatically. Moving the rule to @baseclass is not how App Studio visibility works. Relevant records inherit down the class hierarchy to subclasses.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-021","objective":8,"type":"single","stem":"An LSA is designing the class hierarchy for a mid-size company with a single line of business. Which layer of the hierarchy should be added only when necessary?","options":["Organization","Division/Unit","Application","Work"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The Division/Unit layer is optional and used only when necessary. Organization, Application and Work are standard layers of the hierarchy; Work is where case type classes live and the organization layer anchors pattern inheritance.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ad-022","objective":9,"type":"single","stem":"A developer is debugging a Constellation application and wants Tracer to pause on a specific activity and track how a property value changes. What should the developer expect?","options":["Breakpoints and Watch work normally, but only from the App Studio runtime toolbar.","Breakpoints and Watch are not supported for Constellation applications, so the developer must rely on other events and tools such as request-ID grouping, browser tools or XRay.","Breakpoints work, but the thread stays paused indefinitely until the developer clicks Play.","Tracer cannot be used at all in Constellation; only Redux DevTools is available."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Breakpoints and Watch are not supported for Service rules or Constellation apps; Tracer still groups Constellation events by request ID, and front-end tools help. The toolbar used does not change that limitation. Where breakpoints are supported, they resume automatically after one hour, not indefinitely. Tracer is available for Constellation apps, just without breakpoints and watch.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-023","objective":9,"type":"multi","stem":"A developer is reviewing a Tracer session. Which two statements are accurate? (Choose two.)","options":["Light blue rows represent database and cache events.","Only one Tracer can run per operator per node.","Orange rows represent activity events.","Tracer is the recommended tool for measuring application performance.","The last row marked Fail always contains the root cause."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Light blue marks database and cache events, and one Tracer runs per operator per node. Activities are gray; orange is for flows, decisions and declaratives. Tracer has heavy impact and is meant for troubleshooting, not performance analysis. The root cause can occur earlier than the final failed step.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ad-024","objective":10,"type":"single","stem":"A developer creates a configuration set named EmailControls to switch off outbound email until an integration is ready, but the set does not appear on the Configurations landing page. What is the most likely reason?","options":["Configuration sets are visible only in Dev Studio.","The set has no configuration settings yet, and a set is not shown until it contains at least one.","The product file was built without Include associated data.","The developer must first create a Dynamic System Setting with the same name."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A configuration set is not displayed until it has at least one setting. Sets are managed in App Studio on the Settings tab Configurations landing page. Include associated data affects packaging for deployment, not visibility in the development system. Configuration settings replace Dynamic System Settings, so no matching DSS is required.","difficulty":"hard"}]},{"id":"insights","title":"Insights","weight":5,"intro":"Insights are Pega's reporting building blocks in Constellation: queries built in Explore Data and saved as tables or charts that can be shared and placed on dashboards, portal landing pages and Full Page case views. Expect questions about where an Insight can and cannot go, simple value charts, and common troubleshooting fixes.","objectives":[{"objective":"Describe the benefits of using Insights","modules":["Exploring application data with Insights"],"summary":["An **Insight** is a rule that turns a data query into a table or chart. Because it is a saved rule rather than a one-off query, it can be shared with other users and reused in several places, such as dashboards, portal landing pages and case pages. Insights come in table-based and chart-based forms.","Insights are built in the Explore Data landing page, which is the preferred reporting experience for Constellation applications. Pega GenAI Autopilot can also help create an Insight from a natural-language request."],"keyFacts":["Insight = a rule that turns a query into a table or chart.","Insights are shareable and reusable across dashboards, portal landing pages and Full Page case views.","Two forms: table-based and chart-based.","Explore Data is the preferred place to build them for Constellation.","Autopilot can create an Insight from natural language."],"examTraps":["An Insight is a saved rule, not a temporary query that disappears after the session.","Insights are a Constellation-oriented feature; Traditional UI apps need extra enablement (see Explore Data)."]},{"objective":"Use the Explore Data landing page to create Insights","modules":["Exploring application data with Insights"],"summary":["The **Explore Data** landing page lets users query, sort, filter and group application data and then save the result as an Insight. It is the preferred option for Constellation applications. Traditional UI applications must enable Constellation UI for landing pages before they can use it. Object names shown in Explore Data come from each class's short description.","A **simple value chart** displays a single headline number. It is produced when only Measures fields are used (no grouping dimensions). Layouts are Highlight, Highlight + summary, and Custom highlight + summary, and conditional formatting can color the value based on thresholds."],"keyFacts":["Explore Data supports query, sort, filter and group, then save as Insight.","Traditional UI apps must enable Constellation UI for landing pages to use Explore Data.","Object names in Explore Data come from the class short description.","Simple value chart appears when only Measures fields are selected.","Simple value layouts: Highlight; Highlight + summary; Custom highlight + summary.","Simple value charts support conditional formatting."],"table":{"headers":["Simple value layout","Shows"],"rows":[["Highlight","The single measure value"],["Highlight + summary","The value plus a summary"],["Custom highlight + summary","A customized highlight plus a summary"]]},"examTraps":["Adding a grouping/dimension field means it is no longer a simple value chart; only measures qualify.","Explore Data is not automatically available to Traditional UI apps.","Odd object names in Explore Data are fixed through the class short description, not the Insight."]},{"objective":"Describe the relationship between Insights, Dashboards, the Dashboards landing page, Portal landing pages, and Full Page Case Views","modules":["Exploring application data with Insights","Configuring Portal content"],"summary":["Insights can be placed in three kinds of location. On a **Full Page case view**, an Insight can only be added as a tab, not directly inside a View. On **portal landing pages**, public Insights can be added to a region. On **dashboards**, Insights are arranged in a template; dashboards have private, shared (users with access, either all users or your access group) or public visibility, and a promoted filter applies across all Insights on the dashboard.","The **Dashboards landing page** is available by default in App Studio and in work portals and is not configured through portal authoring; a default Work metrics dashboard is provided. Dashboards are landing pages made of widgets that summarize KPIs per persona, built from a template with regions (for example a two-column template with Region A and Region B). New dashboards are private by default, and a copy keeps the source dashboard's visibility. Out-of-the-box portal landing pages include Dashboard, Home, Explore Data, My Work and Self-service Main page."],"keyFacts":["Full Page case view: Insights only as tabs, never directly inside a View.","Portal landing pages: only public Insights can be added to a region.","Dashboard visibility: private (default for new), shared, public.","A copied dashboard keeps the source's visibility.","A promoted filter applies to every Insight on a dashboard.","Dashboards landing page is available by default in App Studio and work portals; not set up through portal authoring.","A default Work metrics dashboard exists.","Explore Data and Dashboard are among the out-of-the-box portal landing pages."],"table":{"headers":["Location","How Insights appear","Key constraint"],"rows":[["Full Page case view","As a tab","Not directly inside a View"],["Portal landing page","In a region","Public Insights only"],["Dashboard","In a dashboard template","Visibility private/shared/public; promoted filter spans all Insights"],["Dashboards landing page","Lists dashboards","Available by default in App Studio and work portals"]]},"examTraps":["Insights cannot be embedded in a partial or form View; use a tab on the Full Page case view.","A private Insight cannot be placed on a portal landing page.","New dashboards default to private, not public.","Do not look for the Dashboards landing page in portal authoring."]},{"objective":"Troubleshoot common Insights issues (supporting topic)","modules":["Exploring application data with Insights"],"summary":["Common Insights problems have specific fixes. Repeated login prompts are addressed by adjusting the access token lifetime. Unwanted number separators in table columns are fixed by modifying the UI controls. Strange object names in Explore Data come from class short descriptions. A missing column usually means the backing property must be optimized for reporting (in the parent classes as well as the specific class) or marked as a relevant record.","Stale display issues can come from the browser cache: close pages, use an incognito window or clear session storage. If the Constellation portal or Explore Data fails to load, verify the application settings, alias and authentication types, and the OAuth 2.0 Client Registration rule."],"keyFacts":["Repeated login prompts: adjust the access token lifetime.","Number separators in tables: modify the UI controls.","Explore Data object names: class short description.","Missing column: optimize the property for reporting in parent classes and the specific class, or mark it relevant.","Browser cache issues: close pages, use incognito, clear session storage.","Portal/Explore Data load failures: check application settings, alias, authentication types and the OAuth 2.0 Client Registration rule."],"examTraps":["Optimizing a property only in the specific class may not be enough; parent classes matter too.","Login prompts are a token lifetime issue, not an access role issue.","Load failures point to app settings/auth/OAuth client registration, not to the Insight definition."]}],"questions":[{"id":"in-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A regional manager rebuilds the same filtered, grouped list of overdue cases every Monday and emails screenshots to colleagues. What Insights capability best removes this rework?","options":["Save the query in Explore Data as an Insight so it is stored as a rule that can be shared and reused.","Export the list to Excel each week and store it in the Reuse Library.","Create a Pulse post every week with the results attached.","Ask a developer to add the list as a new field on the case type."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"An Insight is a rule that turns a query into a table or chart and can be shared and reused, so the manager builds it once. The Reuse Library holds reusable application assets, not weekly exports. Pulse posts are collaboration messages, not saved reports. A case field stores per-case data and does not produce an aggregated report.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"in-002","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A company has an older application built on Traditional UI. Business users want to use Explore Data to build their own Insights. What must happen first?","options":["Nothing; Explore Data is available to every application by default.","Enable Constellation UI for landing pages in the application.","Rebuild every case type from a Blueprint.","Grant each user the SysAdm4 role."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Explore Data is preferred for Constellation; Traditional UI apps must enable Constellation UI for landing pages to use it. It is therefore not available by default to Traditional UI apps. Rebuilding from a Blueprint is unnecessary. SysAdm4 relates to test suites and administration, not enabling Explore Data.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-003","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"An analyst in Explore Data wants a tile showing only the total number of open claims as one large number. She selects the Count measure and also adds Region as a column, but the visualization does not offer a simple value chart. Why?","options":["Simple value charts require a table-based Insight first.","A simple value chart is produced only when the query uses Measures fields alone; adding Region as a dimension prevents it.","Simple value charts are only available on portal landing pages, not in Explore Data.","Count is not a valid measure for any chart."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A simple value chart is available only when the query contains just Measures fields; adding a grouping dimension like Region changes the visualization. A table-based Insight is not a prerequisite. Simple value charts are created in Explore Data and can then be placed elsewhere. Count is a valid measure.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-004","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A dashboard designer is configuring a simple value chart. Which two layouts can she choose? (Choose two.)","options":["Highlight + summary","Custom highlight + summary","Highlight + trend line","Gauge","Stacked highlight"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Simple value charts support Highlight, Highlight + summary and Custom highlight + summary layouts, plus conditional formatting. Highlight + trend line, Gauge and Stacked highlight are not simple value layouts.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-005","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"A developer wants case workers to see an Insight of related open cases directly on the case page. How should it be added?","options":["Embed the Insight inside the Details partial view.","Add the Insight to the Create form view so it shows on case creation.","Add the Insight as a tab on the case type's Full Page case view.","Add the Insight as a field in the case data model."],"answer":[2],"explanation":"On case pages, Insights can only be placed as tabs of the Full Page case view, not directly inside a View, so partial and form views are not valid targets. An Insight is not a data model field.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-006","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"A team lead is setting up dashboards for their team. Which two statements are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["The Dashboards landing page is available by default in App Studio and work portals.","A promoted filter on a dashboard applies to all Insights on that dashboard.","New dashboards are public by default.","The Dashboards landing page must be added through portal authoring before users can see it.","Copying a dashboard always resets the copy to private."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The Dashboards landing page is available by default in App Studio and work portals, and a promoted filter spans every Insight on the dashboard. New dashboards are private by default. The Dashboards landing page is not configured through portal authoring. A copy keeps the source dashboard's visibility.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-007","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"While editing a portal landing page, a developer tries to add an Insight she created yesterday to a region, but it does not appear in the selection list. Other colleagues' Insights do appear. What is the most likely reason?","options":["Her Insight is not public; only public Insights can be added to portal landing pages.","Her Insight is chart-based; only table-based Insights can be added to regions.","Insights can only be added to Full Page case views.","The landing page uses a dashboard template that does not allow Insights."],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Portal landing pages accept public Insights. Both chart-based and table-based Insights are valid Insights. Insights are not restricted to Full Page case views; portal landing pages and dashboards also host them. Dashboards exist specifically to arrange Insights, so a dashboard template would not block them.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"in-008","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"A developer adds a new property to a case type, but it does not appear as a column option in Explore Data. Which action is most likely to resolve this?","options":["Clear the browser session storage.","Optimize the backing property for reporting in the parent classes and the specific class, or mark it as a relevant record.","Increase the access token lifetime.","Change the class short description."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A missing column is resolved by optimizing the property for reporting (including parent classes, not just the specific class) or marking it relevant. Clearing session storage addresses stale cached display, not missing properties. Token lifetime fixes repeated login prompts. The class short description controls object names, not available columns.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"in-009","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"Users browsing Insights in the portal are repeatedly prompted to log in again after short periods. What should the administrator adjust?","options":["The access token lifetime","The UI controls for number columns","The class short description","The dashboard visibility setting"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Repeated login prompts are addressed by adjusting the access token lifetime. UI controls fix number separator formatting. The class short description affects object names in Explore Data. Dashboard visibility controls who can see a dashboard, not session prompts.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"in-010","objective":3,"type":"multi","stem":"After a configuration change, the Constellation portal and Explore Data fail to load for all users. Which two checks are appropriate? (Choose two.)","options":["Verify the application settings, alias and authentication types.","Verify the OAuth 2.0 Client Registration rule.","Modify the UI controls to remove number separators.","Change the class short descriptions of the case types.","Convert all chart-based Insights to table-based Insights."],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Portal and Explore Data load failures are diagnosed by verifying the application settings, alias and authentication types, and the OAuth 2.0 Client Registration rule. Number separators are a display formatting issue. Class short descriptions only affect object names. Converting Insight types does not affect whether the portal loads.","difficulty":"hard"}]},{"id":"genai","title":"Pega GenAI","weight":10,"intro":"Pega GenAI is a family of tools, and the exam mostly tests whether you can match a need to the right tool: Blueprint for designing a new app, Autopilot for design-time suggestions, Connect for custom GenAI automation steps, Coach for runtime guidance, Knowledge Buddy for answers from your own content, and Agents with Tool rules for autonomous case processing. Blueprint import options and Connect response structures are the most detail-heavy areas.","objectives":[{"objective":"Describe how to leverage Pega GenAI Knowledge Buddy to provide client-specific contextual knowledge and support","modules":["Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["Knowledge Buddy gives users instant answers drawn from an organization's own procedural documents and knowledge bases. Its value is knowledge that is specific to the client and to the context, rather than general internet knowledge. It can be enabled for Intelligent Virtual Assistants (IVA), so conversational channels can serve the same grounded answers. Pega uses it for its own Search Assistant on Pega Community, and GenAI agents can list Knowledge Buddies as context sources.","On the exam, Knowledge Buddy is mainly a distractor or a correct choice in a 'which tool' scenario. Choose it when the need is answering questions from company content. Choose GenAI Coach when the need is case-specific advice on the task in front of the user. Choose Autopilot when the need is design-time help building the application."],"keyFacts":["Knowledge Buddy gives instant answers grounded in the client's own procedures, documents and knowledge bases.","Its distinctive value is knowledge specific to the client and to the context.","Knowledge Buddy can be enabled for IVA (Intelligent Virtual Assistant) channels.","Pega Community's Search Assistant is powered by Knowledge Buddy.","GenAI agents can list Knowledge Buddies (and data pages) as context.","It is a runtime, end-user tool, unlike Blueprint and Autopilot, which are design-time tools."],"table":{"headers":["GenAI tool","When it is used","Purpose"],"rows":[["Blueprint","Before or at the start of the build (SaaS)","Turn an idea into an app design (case types, lifecycles, data objects, personas) and export or import it"],["Autopilot","Design time in App Studio (plus Fill form with AI while testing)","Suggestions for case type names, lifecycles, data models, personas, sample records and Insights"],["Connect Generative AI","Runtime automation step designed in App Studio","Custom GenAI capability through the GenAI gateway, driven by a prompt with a response structure"],["GenAI Coach","Runtime","Expert, case-specific advice on the task the end user is doing"],["Knowledge Buddy","Runtime (including IVA)","Answers from the organization's own documents and knowledge bases"],["Agents and Tool rules","Runtime","Conversational, autonomous case processing; Tool rules add governance, security and compliance"]]},"examTraps":["When the source of truth is the company's own manuals or knowledge base, the answer is Knowledge Buddy, not Coach.","Knowledge Buddy does not design or generate application rules. That is Blueprint or Autopilot."]},{"objective":"Describe the role of Pega Blueprint in transforming application design; create, import, and modify a Blueprint","modules":["Accelerating application building with Pega Blueprint"],"summary":["Blueprint is a free SaaS tool at pega.com/blueprint, and you do not need a Pega instance or login to use it. You start from the My Blueprint dashboard and pick an industry, a sub-industry and a department or function. If yours is not listed, choose Other and describe it. You then choose an application purpose: either pre-built industry templates or Pega GenAI solutions. Next you write a functional description (help is available under 'What should I write?'), enter the organization name and language, and optionally upload supporting documents such as requirements. Over about seven steps you refine case types, case lifecycles (primary and alternate stages, processes and steps), the case data model (fields, field types, descriptions, primary fields, single or multiple records, user input or calculated), data objects and personas (access, channels, release planning and effort), and then review a summary. 'Generate more' asks GenAI for additional case types. Blueprint supports App Studio rather than replacing it.","Best practices: use Notes to record routing, SLA and condition details, use 'Preview my app' to validate the design (this includes a center-out, multichannel preview with chat and dashboards), and after import use Agile Workbench to build the backlog. You can export a PDF summary, a .blueprint file and the revision history, or spin up a Pega instance with the app already imported.","To import, create a new application in App Studio, choose 'Build from a Blueprint', and select the file. You do not need PremBridge. The app is built on Pega Platform with Constellation UI. The wizard has a Blueprint Details tab and then steps for Case Types (build, inherit from an existing one, or don't build), Data Objects (build, inherit, reuse an existing one with no class of its own, or skip), Personas (build or not), and Configurations (class layers, case and data classes). Every generated asset except access roles is created in a branch, and the base rulesets are locked with a password that you must store securely. Download the PDF to keep beside you during the import. To use an existing application in the New Application wizard, save it as a template first."],"keyFacts":["Blueprint is free SaaS at pega.com/blueprint. It needs no Pega instance or login.","Application purpose: pre-built industry templates OR Pega GenAI solutions. Supporting assets such as requirements documents can be uploaded.","Blueprint designs case types, lifecycles (primary and alternate stages), the data model (including primary fields, single or multiple records, user input or calculated), data objects and personas.","Exports: PDF summary, .blueprint file, revision history. You can also spin up a Pega instance with the app imported.","Import path: App Studio, New application, Build from a Blueprint. PremBridge is not required, and the result uses Constellation UI.","Import options. Case types: build, inherit from existing, don't build. Data objects: build, inherit, reuse existing (no class of its own), skip. Personas: build or not.","Everything except **access roles** is created in a branch. Base rulesets are locked with a password you must store securely.","Checklist: Pre-import, Import, Post-import, Review, and Common challenges.","Best practices: Notes for routing, SLAs and conditions; 'Preview my app'; Agile Workbench backlog after import; keep the PDF handy during import.","After import, add users and assign them personas."],"table":{"headers":["Checklist phase / import step","What to do or decide"],"rows":[["Pre-import","Confirm app name, case types, data objects, personas and organization details"],["Import","New app vs. extend an existing one; watch for overlaps and conflicts in rulesets and class inheritance"],["Post-import","Merge branches, update rulesets, set up the developer application"],["Review","Decision shapes, flow actions, locking strategies; data model DB mapping, data pages, property settings"],["Common challenges","Alternate stages, approval steps, automation steps, child case management"],["Import: Case Types","Build / Inherit from existing / Don't build"],["Import: Data Objects","Build / Inherit / Reuse existing (no own class) / Skip"],["Import: Personas","Build / Don't build"],["Import: Configurations","Class layers, case and data classes"]]},"examTraps":["Saying Blueprint needs a Pega instance, a login or PremBridge to import is wrong.","Access roles are the one asset type **not** created in the branch during import.","'Reuse existing' for a data object creates no class of its own. 'Inherit' creates a class that inherits from the existing one.","Blueprint is not limited to templates. It can also generate solutions with GenAI, and 'Generate more' adds case types."]},{"objective":"Analyze the role of Pega GenAI Autopilot in developing applications faster","modules":["Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["Autopilot is the umbrella name for GenAI help at design time in App Studio. It suggests case type names based on the application name and metadata, lifecycle stages and steps, data model fields (including picklist choices), personas with descriptions, and sample data records. It also offers 'Fill form with AI' while you test at runtime and builds Insights from natural-language requests. In '23 and '24 it was inactive by default. You must review every suggestion, because Pega is not responsible for the generated content.","To create a case type, go to the Case Types tab and accept a suggested name. Select 'Use Autopilot to suggest a Life Cycle and Data Model', review the suggested stages and steps, click Next, and review the fields (name, type, ID). Field types can include Text, Date only, Picklist, Currency, Integer, Embedded data and Data reference. For a Data reference field, Autopilot also creates the data object. After you submit, the results appear on the Workflow and Data model tabs. On a data object's Records tab, 'Generate sample records with Autopilot' creates the number of records you choose, with values that match each field type (an Email field gets email-like values). This lets testing continue before the real system of record is integrated. You find persona suggestions under Users > User management, and you add a persona to a case type in the Personas and Channels section of the lifecycle."],"keyFacts":["Autopilot covers GenAI design-time features in App Studio.","It suggests case type names, lifecycle stages and steps, data model fields (including picklist choices), personas, sample records and natural-language Insights.","'Fill form with AI' fills a form while you test at runtime.","A Data reference field suggested by Autopilot causes Autopilot to create that data object.","Sample records come from the data object's Records tab: you choose how many, and values match the field types.","Sample records stand in for a system of record that is not yet integrated, during testing.","Persona suggestions are under Users > User management. Personas are then added to a case type in the lifecycle's Personas and Channels section.","Inactive by default in '23 and '24. Always review suggestions, because Pega is not responsible for generated content."],"table":{"headers":["Need","Where Autopilot helps"],"rows":[["New case type with stages, steps and fields","Case Types tab: suggested name, then 'Use Autopilot to suggest a Life Cycle and Data Model'"],["Realistic test data without a system of record","Data object Records tab: 'Generate sample records with Autopilot'"],["User types for the app","Users > User management: persona suggestions with descriptions"],["Faster manual testing","Fill form with AI at runtime"],["Reports from a plain-language question","Insight from natural language"]]},"examTraps":["Autopilot output is a suggestion. Developers must review it rather than trust it as validated.","Runtime, case-specific expert advice for end users is GenAI Coach, not Autopilot.","Autopilot sample records are for testing. They do not replace configuring the real data source before production."]},{"objective":"Explain how to integrate generative AI features into your application by using Pega GenAI Connect Rules","modules":["Pega GenAI in Pega Platform","Automation shapes in the Case Life Cycle"],"summary":["Connect Generative AI lets you build your own GenAI capabilities, and calls go through the GenAI gateway. In App Studio ('24 and later) it appears as the 'Connect Generative AI' automation step, and you build it in AI Designer. Every Connect starts with a prompt. You can write a custom prompt or reuse an existing prompt from Dev Studio.","The response structure decides how the output lands in the case. Structured-Single maps the response to one field. Structured-List maps it to an embedded data list. Unstructured maps free-form output to a Text (paragraph) field. In every case you must pick a target field and add that field to the UI, or users will not see the result. Best practices: mask sensitive data in prompts, and note that the response comes back in the language of the user's locale."],"keyFacts":["Connect builds custom GenAI capabilities through the GenAI gateway.","In App Studio it is the 'Connect Generative AI' automation step, built in AI Designer.","Each Connect starts from a prompt, either custom or an existing prompt from Dev Studio.","Structured-Single maps to one field.","Structured-List maps to an embedded data list.","Unstructured maps to a Text (paragraph) field.","You need a target field, and that field must be added to a view for the result to show.","Mask sensitive data. Responses come back in the language of the user's locale."],"table":{"headers":["Response structure","Maps to","Typical use"],"rows":[["Structured-Single","One field","Extract one discrete value, such as a category or sentiment"],["Structured-List","An embedded data list","Extract repeating items, such as line items from a document"],["Unstructured","A Text (paragraph) field","Free-form output such as a summary or draft text"]]},"examTraps":["A Structured-List response does not go into a single text field. It needs an embedded data list.","Configuring the step is not enough. If the target field is not on a view, users never see the output.","Connect calls go through the GenAI gateway; they do not call a model directly. Sensitive data should be masked."]},{"objective":"Explain how to guide users through complex processes with custom AI-powered capabilities using Pega GenAI Coach","modules":["Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["GenAI Coach is a runtime tool that gives end users expert advice on the task they are working on, with guidance specific to the current case. It is available as a widget in the utilities pane of a case's Full Page View, beside Attachments, Tags, Followers and the other case utilities.","Separate it from Knowledge Buddy, which answers questions from the organization's own documents and knowledge bases, and from Autopilot, which helps developers at design time. When a question says a worker needs advice in the context of the case they are handling, the answer is Coach."],"keyFacts":["GenAI Coach provides runtime, expert advice on end-user tasks.","Its guidance is specific to the current case.","It is surfaced as the GenAI Coach widget in the Full Page View utilities pane.","It is aimed at end users working cases, not at developers designing the app.","Contrast: Knowledge Buddy grounds answers in the client's own documents, and Autopilot works at design time."],"examTraps":["Coach is not a design-time tool, so do not choose it for generating lifecycles or data models.","Coach is not added as an automation step. Connect Generative AI is the automation step, while Coach is a utilities widget."]},{"objective":"Describe how Pega GenAI Agents and Tool Rules benefit application users","modules":["Pega GenAI in Pega Platform"],"summary":["Agents automate case processing and work conversationally. An agent can run an automation, start a case, call another agent or launch a user action. You give it context by listing data pages and Knowledge Buddies, and it can process uploaded documents. Agents can talk to other agents and use the MCP protocol. Each agent needs a clear description and guardrails so its behavior stays within bounds.","Tool rules supply the governance, security and compliance layer for what agents can use and do. On the exam, link Tool rules to governance and control and link agents to autonomous, conversational case work. Neither one shapes GenAI output into fields; that is the job of a Connect response structure."],"keyFacts":["Agents automate case processing and are conversational.","Agent actions: run an automation, start a case, call another agent, launch a user action.","Context comes from listed data pages and Knowledge Buddies.","Supports agent-to-agent communication and MCP protocols.","Agents need descriptions and guardrails, and they can process uploaded documents.","Tool rules provide governance, security and compliance."],"table":{"headers":["Element","Role"],"rows":[["Agent actions","Run automation, start a case, call another agent, launch a user action"],["Context sources","Data pages and Knowledge Buddies"],["Protocols","Agent-to-agent and MCP"],["Descriptions and guardrails","Define the agent's purpose and keep its behavior within bounds"],["Tool rules","Governance, security and compliance"]]},"examTraps":["Tool rules are about governance, security and compliance. They are not prompts or response structures.","Agents get context from data pages and Knowledge Buddies, not by importing Blueprint files."]}],"questions":[{"id":"ai-001","objective":0,"type":"single","stem":"A bank's service center wants representatives, and later its chatbot (IVA), to get instant answers to questions such as 'What documents are needed to close a deceased customer's account?' The answers must come from the bank's own procedure manuals and internal knowledge base. Which Pega GenAI capability best fits?","options":["GenAI Coach","Knowledge Buddy","Autopilot","Pega GenAI Blueprint"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Knowledge Buddy gives instant answers grounded in the client's own procedures and knowledge bases, and it can be enabled for IVA. GenAI Coach gives case-specific advice on the task the user is doing and is not the tool for answering from enterprise documents. Autopilot gives design-time suggestions to developers. Blueprint designs applications before or at the start of a build.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-002","objective":0,"type":"multi","stem":"Which two statements about Knowledge Buddy are correct? (Choose two.)","options":["It grounds its answers in the organization's own documents and knowledge bases","It generates case types and lifecycles from a functional description","It can be enabled for Intelligent Virtual Assistants","It is one of the response structures of a Connect Generative AI step","It must be created by importing a Blueprint file"],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Knowledge Buddy answers from client-specific content and can be enabled for IVA. Generating case types and lifecycles is the work of Blueprint and Autopilot. Connect response structures are Structured-Single, Structured-List and Unstructured. Blueprint import creates application assets such as case types, data objects and personas, not Knowledge Buddies.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ai-003","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"A team is importing a Blueprint into a new application. Its 'Customer' data object duplicates a Customer data object already maintained in an enterprise application the new app will use. The team wants the new case types to use that existing object as is, without creating a Customer class in the new app. Which import option should they choose for the data object?","options":["Build","Inherit from existing","Reuse existing","Skip"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"'Reuse existing' points the application at the existing data object without creating a class of its own. 'Inherit' creates a new class that inherits from the existing one, which is the extra class the team wants to avoid. 'Build' generates a brand-new data object, which creates the duplicate. 'Skip' leaves the object out, so the case types would have no link to the existing Customer object.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-004","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A lead system architect has just completed a Blueprint import in App Studio. Which two statements describe the resulting application? (Choose two.)","options":["All generated assets except access roles are created in a branch","The base rulesets are locked, and the password provided must be stored securely","Access roles are created in the branch along with the other assets","The import requires PremBridge to be installed first","The application is built on Traditional UI with section-based views"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Import puts every asset except access roles into a branch and locks the base rulesets with a password you must keep safe. Access roles are the exception and are not created in the branch. PremBridge is not required for import. Blueprint apps are built on Pega Platform with Constellation UI, not Traditional UI.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-005","objective":1,"type":"single","stem":"Following the Blueprint checklist, a team is reviewing decision shapes, flow actions and locking strategies, and checking database mapping, data pages and property settings in the data model. Which checklist phase are they in?","options":["Pre-import","Import","Post-import","Review"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"The Review phase covers decision shapes, flow actions, locking strategies and data model details such as DB mapping, data pages and property settings. Pre-import confirms the app name, case types, data objects, personas and organization details. Import decides between a new app and extending an existing one and watches for ruleset and class conflicts. Post-import merges branches, updates rulesets and sets up the developer application.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ai-006","objective":1,"type":"multi","stem":"A solutions consultant wants to co-design an application with a prospect who has no Pega environment. Which two statements about Pega Blueprint support this? (Choose two.)","options":["Blueprint runs as SaaS and needs no Pega instance or login","The design can be exported as a .blueprint file and later imported into Pega","Blueprint can generate applications only from pre-built industry templates","Blueprint replaces App Studio for all further configuration after import","A Blueprint can be imported only into an existing application, not a new one"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Blueprint is a free SaaS tool that needs no Pega instance or login, and it exports a .blueprint file (plus a PDF and revision history) that can be imported. It offers Pega GenAI solutions as well as pre-built templates. It supports App Studio rather than replacing it. Import can create a new application through 'Build from a Blueprint', and extending an existing app is a separate option.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-007","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"While creating a 'Purchase Request' case type with Autopilot, a developer accepts a suggested field 'Vendor' of type Data reference. No Vendor data object exists yet. What happens when the developer submits?","options":["Submission fails until a Vendor data object is created manually","Autopilot creates the Vendor data object along with the field","The field is converted to Text (single line) because the reference cannot be resolved","The field becomes Embedded data stored only in the case"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"When Autopilot suggests a Data reference field, it also creates the data object behind it. Nothing has to be created by hand first, so submission does not fail. Autopilot does not quietly change the type to Text. Embedded data is a different relationship type that stores data inside the case, and Autopilot does not substitute it.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-008","objective":2,"type":"single","stem":"The external customer database is not integrated yet, but testers need about 50 realistic Customer records with valid-looking email addresses to test case flows. What is the fastest App Studio approach?","options":["Use 'Generate sample records with Autopilot' on the Customer data object's Records tab","Add a Connect Generative AI step to the case type to create customers at runtime","Re-import the Blueprint with the Customer data object set to Build","Use 'Fill form with AI' on the Customer data object's Full Page View"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Autopilot can generate the number of sample records you choose on a data object's Records tab, with values that match each field type, such as email-formatted values for Email fields. It is meant to stand in for a system of record that is not yet integrated. A Connect step produces GenAI output inside a case, not a set of test data records. Re-importing a Blueprint creates rules, not populated records. 'Fill form with AI' fills one form during runtime testing; it does not create a batch of records.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ai-009","objective":2,"type":"multi","stem":"Which two capabilities are part of Pega GenAI Autopilot? (Choose two.)","options":["Suggesting personas, with descriptions, for the application","Filling in a form with AI while testing at runtime","Giving end users expert, case-specific advice while they work a task","Answering employee questions from the company's knowledge base","Mapping a GenAI response to an embedded data list through a response structure"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Autopilot covers design-time GenAI help, including persona suggestions and 'Fill form with AI' during runtime testing. Case-specific advice for end users is GenAI Coach. Answering from the company knowledge base is Knowledge Buddy. Mapping output to an embedded data list is a Connect Generative AI Structured-List response.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-010","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"An accounts payable case receives a supplier invoice. A Connect Generative AI step must extract every line item (description, quantity, unit price) into the case's 'Invoice lines' field. Which response structure and target should be configured?","options":["Structured-Single mapped to the Invoice lines field","Structured-List mapped to Invoice lines defined as an embedded data list","Unstructured mapped to Invoice lines defined as Text (paragraph)","Unstructured mapped to Invoice lines defined as an embedded data list"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Repeating structured items belong in a Structured-List response, which maps to an embedded data list. Structured-Single maps to one field, so it fits one discrete value, not many line items. Unstructured output maps to a Text (paragraph) field, which would leave the lines as free text with no structure. Unstructured cannot be mapped to an embedded data list.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-011","objective":3,"type":"single","stem":"A complaints case should show adjusters a free-form narrative summary of the customer's emails, created by a Connect Generative AI step. The step runs successfully, but adjusters never see the summary. The output is configured as Unstructured. What is the most likely missing configuration?","options":["The response structure must be Structured-Single for any output to be stored","A Text (paragraph) target field must be set and added to a view the adjusters use","The prompt must be rebuilt in Dev Studio, because App Studio prompts cannot return text","GenAI Coach must be enabled before Connect output can be displayed"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Unstructured output maps to a Text (paragraph) field, and that target field must be added to the UI before users can see it. Structured-Single is for one discrete value; free-form narrative belongs in an Unstructured response. Connect can use custom prompts or existing Dev Studio prompts, and App Studio's AI Designer is where Connect is built. GenAI Coach is a separate runtime advice widget and does not control Connect output.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ai-012","objective":3,"type":"multi","stem":"A system architect is designing a Connect Generative AI step that summarizes medical claim notes. Which two statements reflect how Connect works and the best practices for it? (Choose two.)","options":["The step starts from a prompt, either a custom prompt or an existing one from Dev Studio","Sensitive data such as patient identifiers should be masked","Connect calls the language model directly, bypassing the GenAI gateway","The response is shown automatically in the case without adding any field to a view","Connect steps can be configured only in Dev Studio"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Every Connect starts from a prompt, either custom or reused from Dev Studio, and sensitive data should be masked. Connect calls go through the GenAI gateway rather than straight to a model. The output goes into a target field, and that field must be added to the UI to be seen. Connect is built in AI Designer in App Studio, where it is the 'Connect Generative AI' automation step.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-013","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"New claims adjusters want expert guidance tailored to the specific claim they have open, such as advice on what to check next for this claim, while they work their assignments. Which capability fits best?","options":["Knowledge Buddy","GenAI Coach","Autopilot","A Connect Generative AI step with a Structured-Single response"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"GenAI Coach gives runtime, expert advice on end-user tasks that is specific to the current case. Knowledge Buddy answers from the organization's documents rather than coaching the user through a particular case's task. Autopilot is design-time help for developers. A Connect step with a Structured-Single response writes one value into a field during an automation; it is not interactive coaching.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ai-014","objective":4,"type":"single","stem":"A product owner wants GenAI Coach available to case workers on every Service Request case. Where should the system architect make it available?","options":["Add a Connect Generative AI automation step to each stage","Add the GenAI Coach widget to the utilities pane of the case type's Full Page View","Add the GenAI Coach widget to the portal navigation in place of the To do widget","Enable Coach in Blueprint and re-import the application"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"GenAI Coach is a utilities pane widget on the case's Full Page View, so adding it there makes it available on every case of that type. A Connect step is an automation that writes GenAI output to fields; it is not Coach. Portal navigation widgets are Pulse, To do and App announcement, and Coach is a case utility. Blueprint designs case types, data and personas and does not enable runtime widgets.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-015","objective":5,"type":"multi","stem":"A team is designing a GenAI agent to process incoming service requests conversationally. Which two actions can an agent take? (Choose two.)","options":["Start a case","Call another agent","Bypass Tool rule governance when a user grants consent in chat","Edit and lock application rulesets in production","Import a Blueprint file to create new case types"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"An agent can run an automation, start a case, call another agent or launch a user action. Tool rules exist to enforce governance, security and compliance, so bypassing them is not an agent capability. Editing and locking rulesets is a development and DevOps task, not an agent action. Blueprint import is done by a developer in App Studio's New Application wizard.","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ai-016","objective":5,"type":"single","stem":"A compliance officer asks how the organization will control what its GenAI agents are permitted to use and do when processing cases. Which Pega element is designed for that purpose?","options":["Tool rules","Connect response structures","Data pages listed as agent context","The Blueprint pre-import checklist"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Tool rules provide the governance, security and compliance layer for agents. Connect response structures decide how GenAI output maps to fields, not what an agent may do. Data pages listed on an agent give it context but do not govern it. The Blueprint pre-import checklist covers application design before import and has nothing to do with runtime agent control.","difficulty":"hard"}]}]};
